  (24851, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Fleming यांच्या उजव्या हाताच्या नियमानुसार, अंगठा, तर्जनी आणि मधले बोट एकमेकांना काटकोनात ठेवले असता तर्जनी चुंबकीय क्षेत्राची दिशा, अंगठा वाहकाच्या गतिची दिशा आणि मधले बोट............ दर्शवते.', '{"A": "विद्युतचुंबकीय बलाची दिशा", "B": "प्रेरित विद्युतप्रवाहाची दिशा", "C": "इलेक्ट्रॉन्सची दिशा", "D": "काहीही नाही"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय B आहे पर्याय B (प्रेरित विद्युतप्रवाहाची दिशा) — Fleming यांच्या उजव्या हाताच्या नियमात मधले बोट प्रेरित विद्युतप्रवाहाची दिशा दर्शवते. चुकीचे पर्याय A (विद्युतचुंबकीय बलाची दिशा), C (इलेक्ट्रॉन्सची दिशा), D (काहीही नाही) पर्याय A (विद्युतचुंबकीय बलाची दिशा) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (इलेक्ट्रॉन्सची दिशा) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (काहीही नाही) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24852, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'बरोबर विधान ओळखा. अ) प्रतिचुंबकीय पदार्थ चुंबकीय क्षेत्राच्या विरुद्ध दिशेने दुर्बल चुंबकत्व प्राप्त करतात. ब) अनुचुंबकीय पदार्थ चुंबकीय क्षेत्राच्या दिशेत दुर्बल चुंबकत्व प्राप्त करतात. क) लोहचुंबकीय पदार्थ चुंबकीय क्षेत्राच्या दिशेत तीव्रपणे चुंबित होतात.', '{"A": "अ) आणि ब)", "B": "फक्त क)", "C": "ब) आणि क)", "D": "अ), ब) आणि क)"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'पर्याय D (अ), ब) आणि क)) — पर्याय A (अ) आणि ब)) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (फक्त क)) मध्ये फक्त काही विधाने घेतली आहेत, पण प्रश्नातील सर्व बरोबर विधाने एकत्र येतात; पर्याय C (ब) आणि क)) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही. पर्याय A (अ) आणि ब)) आणि ब)) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (फक्त क)) ) मध्ये फक्त काही विधाने घेतली आहेत, पण प्रश्नातील सर्व बरोबर विधाने एकत्र येतात; पर्याय C (ब) आणि क)) आणि क)) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही. पर्याय A (अ) आणि ब)) आणि ब)) आणि ब)) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (फक्त क)) ) ) मध्ये फक्त काही विधाने घेतली आहेत, पण प्रश्नातील सर्व बरोबर विधाने एकत्र येतात; पर्याय C (ब) आणि क)) आणि क)) आणि क)) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24853, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'जोड्या जुळवा. अ) प्रतिचुंबकीय | i) Fe, Co, Ni ब) अनुचुंबकीय | ii) लाकूड, प्लास्टिक क) लोहचुंबकीय | iii) Al, O, Na, Pt, हवा ड) अचुंबकीय | iv) Cu, Au, Ag, H₂O', '{"A": "i, ii, iii, iv", "B": "ii, iii, iv, i", "C": "iv, iii, i, ii", "D": "iii, i, ii, iv"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'सोडवत गेल्यास पर्याय C येतो प्रतिचुंबक Cu/Au/Ag, अनुचुंबक Al/O/Na, लोहचुंबक Fe/Co/Ni, अचुंबक लाकूड/प्लास्टिक. चुकीचे पर्याय A (i, ii, iii, iv), B (ii, iii, iv, i), D (iii, i, ii, iv) पर्याय A (i, ii, iii, iv) मध्ये किमान एक जोडी चुकीच्या क्रमाने मांडली आहे, म्हणून ती वर्गातून वगळावी; पर्याय B (ii, iii, iv, i) मध्ये किमान एक जोडी चुकीच्या क्रमाने मांडली आहे, म्हणून ती वर्गातून वगळावी; पर्याय D (iii, i, ii, iv) मध्ये किमान एक जोडी चुकीच्या क्रमाने मांडली आहे, म्हणून ती वर्गातून वगळावी।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24854, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'अ) चुंबकीय क्षेत्राला परिमाण आणि दिशा दोन्ही असतात. ब) चुंबकीय प्रेरणाचे (B) एकक Tesla आहे.', '{"A": "अ बरोबर, ब चूक", "B": "अ चूक, ब बरोबर", "C": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर", "D": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय C आहे पर्याय C (अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर) — चुंबकीय क्षेत्राला परिमाण आणि दिशा दोन्ही; Tesla हे चुंबकीय प्रेरण (B) चे SI एकक. चुकीचे पर्याय A (अ बरोबर, ब चूक), B (अ चूक, ब बरोबर), D (अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक) पर्याय A (अ बरोबर, ब चूक) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (अ चूक, ब बरोबर) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24855, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'एका ठराविक तापमानावर लोहचुंबकीय पदार्थ अनुचुंबकीय पदार्थात रूपांतरित होतो; या तापमानाला....... म्हणतात.', '{"A": "Curie तापमान", "B": "समी तापमान", "C": "Faraday तापमान", "D": "सीमा तापमान"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'पर्याय A (Curie तापमान) पर्याय B (समी तापमान) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (Faraday तापमान) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (सीमा तापमान) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24856, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'पृथ्वीचे चुंबकीय क्षेत्र............ मुळे निर्माण होते.', '{"A": "डायनॅमो परिणाम", "B": "डॉप्लर परिणाम", "C": "सौर परिणाम", "D": "मॅग्नस परिणाम"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय A आहे पर्याय A (डायनॅमो परिणाम) — पृथ्वीचे चुंबकीय क्षेत्र वितळलेल्या कोरमधील विद्युतप्रवाह (डायनॅमो परिणाम) मुळे. चुकीचे पर्याय B (डॉप्लर परिणाम), C (सौर परिणाम), D (मॅग्नस परिणाम) पर्यायाचे नाव, सूत्र व एकक एकत्र लक्षात ठेवा — जवळचे चुकीचा पर्याय सापळे टाळा पर्याय B (डॉप्लर परिणाम) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (सौर परिणाम) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (मॅग्नस परिणाम) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24857, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'चुंबकीय प्रेरण ही............ राशी आहे.', '{"A": "सदिश", "B": "अदिश", "C": "दोन्ही", "D": "काहीही नाही"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय B आहे चुंबकीय प्रेरण ही अदिश राशी; दिशा B-रेषांनी दर्शवतात. चुकीचे पर्याय A (सदिश), C (दोन्ही), D (काहीही नाही) पर्याय A (सदिश) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (दोन्ही) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (काहीही नाही) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24858, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'चुंबकीय प्रवाह 12×10⁻³ Wb पासून 6×10⁻³ Wb पर्यंत 0.01 सेकंदात बदलल्यास, तारेच्या कुंडलीत प्रेरित emf किती असेल?', '{"A": "1 V", "B": "12 V", "C": "0.6 V", "D": "1.2 V"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'गणितात हे सोडवत गेल्यास पर्याय C येतो। emf = ΔΦ/Δt = (12−6)×10⁻³/0.01 = 0.6 V। चुकीचे पर्याय A (1 V), B (12 V), D (1.2 V) या प्रश्नाच्या मुख्य संकल्पनेशी जुळत नाहीत. परीक्षेत जवळचे पर्याय सापडतात, त्यामुळे प्रश्नाचा मजकूर आणि मुख्य तथ्य पुन्हा वाचून निश्चित करा।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24859, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'दंड चुंबकाच्या केंद्रावर चुंबकीय बलरेषांचे मान किती असते?', '{"A": "कमाल", "B": "शून्य", "C": "शून्य नसलेले किमान", "D": "ध्रुवांवरील एवढेच"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय B आहे पर्याय B (शून्य) — दंड चुंबकाच्या केंद्रावर बलरेषा एकमेकांना रद्द होऊन शून्य. चुकीचे पर्याय A (कमाल), C (शून्य नसलेले किमान), D (ध्रुवांवरील एवढेच) पर्याय A (कमाल) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (शून्य नसलेले किमान) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (ध्रुवांवरील एवढेच) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24860, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '1 m लांबीचा वाहक 0.3 Wb/m² चुंबकीय क्षेत्रात 10 m/s वेगाने प्रवेश करतो. वाहकाचा वेग आणि चुंबकीय क्षेत्र यांतील कोन 30° असल्यास प्रेरित emf किती?', '{"A": "1.5 V", "B": "1 V", "C": "15 V", "D": "20 V"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय A आहे पर्याय A (1.5 V) — ε = Blv sinθ = 1×0.3×10×sin30° = 1.5 V। चुकीचे पर्याय B (1 V), C (15 V), D (20 V) पर्याय B (1 V) या प्रश्नाच्या मुख्य संकल्पनेशी सुसंगत नाही; पर्याय C (15 V) या प्रश्नाच्या मुख्य संकल्पनेशी सुसंगत नाही; पर्याय D (20 V) या प्रश्नाच्या मुख्य संकल्पनेशी सुसंगत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24861, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'विद्युतचुंबकीय किरणांची उदाहरणे कोणती? अ) X-किरणे ब) सूर्यप्रकाश क) गामा किरणे ड) वैश्विक किरणे इ) बीटा किरणे', '{"A": "अ, ब, क", "B": "अ, ब, क, ड, इ", "C": "अ आणि क", "D": "ड, इ, क"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'गणितात हे सोडवत गेल्यास पर्याय A येतो पर्याय A (अ, ब, क) — X-किरणे, सूर्यप्रकाश आणि गामा किरणे EM किरणे; वैश्विक आणि बीटा किरणे कण विकिरण. चुकीचे पर्याय B (अ, ब, क, ड, इ), C (अ आणि क), D (ड, इ, क) पर्याय B (अ, ब, क, ड, इ) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (अ आणि क) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (ड, इ, क) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24862, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'विद्युतचुंबकीय स्पेक्ट्रमात दृश्य प्रकाश पट्ट्याची तरंगलांबी किती असते?', '{"A": "400 - 700 नॅनोमीटर", "B": "0.4 - 0.7 मायक्रोमीटर", "C": "4000 - 7000 Å", "D": "सर्व"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय D आहे। 400–700 nm, 0.4–0.7 μm आणि 4000–7000 Å — दृश्य प्रकाशाची तरंगलांबीचे तीनही एकरूप मान. चुकीचे पर्याय A (400 - 700 नॅनोमीटर), B (0.4 - 0.7 मायक्रोमीटर), C (4000 - 7000 Å) या प्रश्नाच्या मुख्य संकल्पनेशी जुळत नाहीत। MPSC प्रश्नपत्रिकेत अशा तपशीलांची पुनरावृत्ती होते, म्हणून मुख्य संकल्पना लक्षात ठेवा।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24863, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'चुंबकीय दिशादर्शकाची सुई नेहमी............ दिशेत स्थिरावते.', '{"A": "उत्तर-दक्षिण", "B": "उत्तर-पूर्व", "C": "पूर्व-पश्चिम", "D": "दक्षिण-पश्चिम"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'पर्याय A (उत्तर-दक्षिण) पर्याय B (उत्तर-पूर्व) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (पूर्व-पश्चिम) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (दक्षिण-पश्चिम) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24864, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'खालील तरंगांची तरंगलांबी वाढत्या क्रमाने मांडा. अ) रेडिओ तरंग ब) दृश्य प्रकाश क) गामा किरणे ड) मायक्रोवेव्ह', '{"A": "अ, ड, ब, क", "B": "क, ड, ब, अ", "C": "अ, ब, क, ड", "D": "क, ब, ड, अ"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय D आहे पर्याय D (क, ब, ड, अ) — तरंगलांबी वाढ: गामा < X < UV < दृश्य < IR < μwave < रेडिओ — क, ब, ड, अ. चुकीचे पर्याय A (अ, ड, ब, क), B (क, ड, ब, अ), C (अ, ब, क, ड) पर्याय A (अ, ड, ब, क) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (क, ड, ब, अ) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (अ, ब, क, ड) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24865, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'जोड्या जुळवा. अ) रेडिओ तरंग | i) Hertz ब) मायक्रोवेव्ह | ii) Marconi क) अवरक्त तरंग | iii) Herschel ड) पराबैंगनी तरंग | iv) Ritter इ) गामा किरणे | v) Becquerel', '{"A": "i, ii, iii, iv, v", "B": "v, iv, iii, i, ii", "C": "iv, ii, i, iii, v", "D": "v, iv, iii, ii, i"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'पर्याय A (i, ii, iii, iv, v) — रेडिओ–Hertz, μwave–Marconi, IR–Herschel, UV–Ritter, गामा–Becquerel पर्याय B (v, iv, iii, i, ii) मध्ये किमान एक जोडी चुकीच्या क्रमाने मांडली आहे, म्हणून ती वर्गातून वगळावी; पर्याय C (iv, ii, i, iii, v) मध्ये किमान एक जोडी चुकीच्या क्रमाने मांडली आहे, म्हणून ती वर्गातून वगळावी; पर्याय D (v, iv, iii, ii, i) मध्ये किमान एक जोडी चुकीच्या क्रमाने मांडली आहे, म्हणून ती वर्गातून वगळावी।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24866, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'अ) दृश्य प्रकाशाचा शोध Newton यांनी लावला. ब) दृश्य प्रकाश सात रंगांनी बनलेला असतो. क) दृश्य प्रकाशाची उच्च तीव्रता डोळ्यांसाठी हानिकारक असते. वरीलपैकी कोणती विधाने बरोबर आहेत?', '{"A": "अ आणि ब", "B": "ब आणि क", "C": "अ आणि क", "D": "अ, ब आणि क"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'पर्याय D (अ, ब आणि क) पर्याय A (अ आणि ब) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (ब आणि क) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (अ आणि क) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24867, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'घरगुती वापरासाठी कोणत्या तारा वापरल्या जातात?', '{"A": "फेज तार", "B": "न्यूट्रल तार", "C": "अर्थिंग तार", "D": "सर्व"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय D आहे पर्याय D (सर्व) — घरगुली वीज वितरणात फेज, न्यूट्रल आणि अर्थिंग — तीनही तारा वापरतात. चुकीचे पर्याय A (फेज तार), B (न्यूट्रल तार), C (अर्थिंग तार) पर्यायाचे नाव, सूत्र व एकक एकत्र लक्षात ठेवा — जवळचे चुकीचा पर्याय सापळे टाळा पर्याय A (फेज तार) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (न्यूट्रल तार) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (अर्थिंग तार) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24868, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'दृश्य प्रकाशाचे उपयोग ओळखा. अ) ऑप्टिकल फायबर संदेशवहन ब) इलेक्ट्रॉनिक उपकरणे क) छायाचित्रण ड) लेसर मुद्रण', '{"A": "अ, ब, क", "B": "ब, क, ड", "C": "अ, क, ड", "D": "अ, ब, क, ड"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय D आहे पर्याय D (अ, ब, क, ड) — ऑप्टिकल फायबर, इलेक्ट्रॉनिक उपकरणे, छायाचित्रण, लेसर मुद्रण — दृश्यप्रकाशाचे सर्व उपयोग. चुकीचे पर्याय A (अ, ब, क), B (ब, क, ड), C (अ, क, ड) पर्याय A (अ, ब, क) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (ब, क, ड) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (अ, क, ड) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24869, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'कोणत्या किरणांमुळे वातावरणातील कार्बन डायऑक्साइड शोषला जातो?', '{"A": "पराबैंगनी", "B": "अवरक्त", "C": "मायक्रोवेव्ह", "D": "दृश्य प्रकाश"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय C आहे मायक्रोवेव्ह CO₂ शोषणासाठी — कीनुसार. चुकीचे पर्याय A (पराबैंगनी), B (अवरक्त), D (दृश्य प्रकाश) पर्याय A (पराबैंगनी) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (अवरक्त) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (दृश्य प्रकाश) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24870, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'बरोबर विधाने ओळखा. अ) X-किरणांचा शोध रॉन्टगेन यांनी लावला. ब) X-किरणे रोगनिदानासाठी वापरतात. क) X-किरणे शुद्ध व अशुद्ध हिरे ओळखण्यासाठी वापरतात. ड) X-किरणांचे दुष्परिणाम नाहीत.', '{"A": "अ, ब, क", "B": "ब, क, ड", "C": "अ, क, ड", "D": "अ, ब, ड"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'प्रश्नातील विधाने तपासल्यास पर्याय A बरोबर आहे पर्याय A (अ, ब, क) — Röntgen शोध, रोगनिदान, हिरे तपासणी — बरोबर; गामा/X-किरणांचे दुष्परिणाम आहेत. चुकीचे पर्याय B (ब, क, ड), C (अ, क, ड), D (अ, ब, ड) पर्याय B (ब, क, ड) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (अ, क, ड) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (अ, ब, ड) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24871, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'DVD व CD वरील माहिती वाचण्यासाठी कोणते किरण वापरले जातात?', '{"A": "अवरक्त", "B": "सूक्ष्म तरंग", "C": "दृश्य प्रकाश", "D": "पराबैंगनी"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय D आहे CD/DVD वाचण्यासाठी पराबैंगनी लेーザर वापरले जातात. चुकीचे पर्याय A (अवरक्त), B (सूक्ष्म तरंग), C (दृश्य प्रकाश) पर्यायाचे नाव, सूत्र व एकक एकत्र लक्षात ठेवा — जवळचे चुकीचा पर्याय सापळे टाळा पर्याय A (अवरक्त) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (सूक्ष्म तरंग) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (दृश्य प्रकाश) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24872, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'अ) अवरक्त तरंगांची वारंवारता दृश्य प्रकाशापेक्षा जास्त असते. ब) पराबैंगनी तरंगांची वारंवारता दृश्य प्रकाशापेक्षा कमी असते.', '{"A": "अ बरोबर, ब चूक", "B": "अ चूक, ब बरोबर", "C": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर", "D": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय D आहे पर्याय D (अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक) — IR ची वारंवारता दृश्यापेक्षा कमी; UV ची जास्त — अ आणि ब दोन्ही चुकीची. चुकीचे पर्याय A (अ बरोबर, ब चूक), B (अ चूक, ब बरोबर), C (अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर) पर्याय A (अ बरोबर, ब चूक) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (अ चूक, ब बरोबर) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24873, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'खालीलपैकी पराबैंगनी किरणांचे कोणते उपयोग आहेत? अ) शरीरात विटामिन D निर्मितीस मदत होते. ब) कीटाणूनाशक म्हणून क) दूरस्थ नियंत्रणात ड) कॅमेर्यात', '{"A": "अ, ब, क", "B": "फक्त अ आणि ब", "C": "ब, क, ड", "D": "फक्त अ आणि क"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'पर्याय B (फक्त अ आणि ब) — UV: विट D आणि कीटाणूनाशक; रिमोट IR; कॅमेरा दृश्यप्रकाश. पर्याय A (अ, ब, क) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (ब, क, ड) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (फक्त अ आणि क) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24874, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'दूर संवेदनात खालीलपैकी कोणत्या तरंगांचा वापर होतो? अ) अवरक्त ब) दृश्य प्रकाश क) पराबैंगनी ड) X-किरणे', '{"A": "अ, ब, क", "B": "ब, क, ड", "C": "फक्त अ आणि ब", "D": "सर्व"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'पर्याय A (अ, ब, क) — दूर संवेदनात IR, दृश्य आणि UV — X-किरणे सामान्यतः वापरले जात नाहीत. पर्याय B (ब, क, ड) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (फक्त अ आणि ब) मध्ये फक्त काही विधाने घेतली आहेत, पण प्रश्नातील सर्व बरोबर विधाने एकत्र येतात; पर्याय D (सर्व) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24875, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'अ) क्वांटम यांत्रिकीनुसार, विद्युतचुंबकीय तरंग विशिष्ट कण म्हणून वागतात, ज्याला फोटॉन म्हणतात. ब) विद्युतचुंबकत्वाचा एकत्रित सिद्धांत मॅक्सवेल यांनी मांडला.', '{"A": "अ बरोबर, ब चूक", "B": "अ चूक, ब बरोबर", "C": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर", "D": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय C आहे पर्याय C (अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर) — EM तरंग फोटॉन म्हणून; Maxwell ने विद्युतचुंबकत्व एकत्रित केले. चुकीचे पर्याय A (अ बरोबर, ब चूक), B (अ चूक, ब बरोबर), D (अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक) पर्याय A (अ बरोबर, ब चूक) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (अ चूक, ब बरोबर) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24876, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'जर विद्युतचुंबकीय किरणांची वारंवारता 6 × 10^14 Hz असेल, तर तरंगलांबी काढा आणि ते कोणत्या स्पेक्ट्रमात वर्गीकृत करता येईल?', '{"A": "500 nm, अवरक्त", "B": "0.5 mm, सूक्ष्म तरंग", "C": "500 nm, दृश्य प्रकाश", "D": "50 nm, पराबैंगनी प्रकाश"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'गणितात हे सोडवत गेल्यास पर्याय C येतो। λ = c/f ≈ 500 nm; 6×10¹⁴ Hz दृश्यप्रकाश पट्ट्यात. चुकीचे पर्याय A (500 nm, अवरक्त), B (0.5 mm, सूक्ष्म तरंग), D (50 nm, पराबैंगनी प्रकाश) या प्रश्नाच्या मुख्य संकल्पनेशी जुळत नाहीत. पर्यायाचे नाव, सूत्र व एकक एकत्र लक्षात ठेवा — जवळचे चुकीचा पर्याय सापळे टाळा इतर पर्याय या प्रश्नाच्या मुख्य संकल्पनेशी जुळत नाहीत, त्यामुळे ते वगळावेत।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24877, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'गामा किरणांबद्दल खालीलपैकी चुकीची विधाने कोणती? अ) गामा किरणे अतिशय उच्च वारंवारतेची असून कमी ऊर्जा वाहून नेतात. ब) गामा किरणांचा वापर अन्न पदार्थांच्या कीटाणूनाशनासाठी होतो.', '{"A": "फक्त अ", "B": "फक्त ब", "C": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही", "D": "काहीही नाही"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'पर्याय A (फक्त अ) पर्याय B (फक्त ब) मध्ये फक्त काही विधाने घेतली आहेत, पण प्रश्नातील सर्व बरोबर विधाने एकत्र येतात; पर्याय C (अ आणि ब दोन्ही) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (काहीही नाही) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24878, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'अ) सूक्ष्म तरंग पाणी व चरबीच्या रेणूंना कंपित करतात. ब) अन्न शिजवण्यासाठी सूक्ष्म तरंग वापरतात.', '{"A": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर; ब हे अ चे कारण आहे.", "B": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर; अ हे ब चे कारण आहे.", "C": "अ चूक, ब बरोबर", "D": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय B आहे पर्याय B (अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर; अ हे ब चे कारण आह…) — μwave पाणी/चरबी कंपित करतात (अ), त्यामुळे अन्न शिजते (ब) — कारण-परिणाम. चुकीचे पर्याय A (अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर; ब हे अ चे कार…), C (अ चूक, ब बरोबर), D (अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक) पर्याय A (अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर; ब हे अ चे कारण आह…) प्रश्नातील तथ्ये/गणना/व्याख्येशी विरोधाभासी आहे; पर्याय C (अ चूक, ब बरोबर) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24879, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'अ) अल्फा, बीटा व गामा किरणे विद्युतचुंबकीय पट्ट्यात समाविष्ट आहेत. ब) विद्युतचुंबकीय तरंगांत विद्युत व चुंबकीय क्षेत्रे परस्परांना समांतर असतात.', '{"A": "अ बरोबर, ब चूक", "B": "अ चूक, ब बरोबर", "C": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर", "D": "अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय D आहे पर्याय D (अ आणि ब दोन्ही चूक) — α, β कण विकिरण EM पट्ट्यात नाहीत; E आणि B क्षेत्रे परस्पर लंब. चुकीचे पर्याय A (अ बरोबर, ब चूक), B (अ चूक, ब बरोबर), C (अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर) पर्याय A (अ बरोबर, ब चूक) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (अ चूक, ब बरोबर) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (अ आणि ब दोन्ही बरोबर) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24880, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'अ) विद्युतचुंबकीय पट्ट्यात गामा किरणांची वारंवारता सर्वाधिक असते. ब) रेडिओ तरंगांची तरंगलांबी सर्वाधिक असते. क) एका माध्यमात सर्व विद्युतचुंबकीय किरणांचा वेग समान असतो. वरीलपैकी चुकीची विधाने कोणती?', '{"A": "अ", "B": "ब", "C": "क", "D": "काहीही नाही"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'प्रश्नातील विधाने तपासल्यास पर्याय D बरोबर आहे पर्याय D (काहीही नाही) — गामा सर्वाधिक ν, रेडिओ सर्वाधिक λ, एका माध्यमात v समान — सर्व बरोबर. चुकीचे पर्याय A (अ), B (ब), C (क) पर्याय A (अ) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (ब) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (क) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24881, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'जेव्हा विद्युतचुंबकीय तरंग एका माध्यमातून दुसऱ्या माध्यमात जातात, तेव्हा खालीलपैकी कोणती राशी बदलत नाही?', '{"A": "तरंगलांबी", "B": "वेग", "C": "वारंवारता", "D": "काहीही नाही"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय C आहे पर्याय C (वारंवारता) — माध्यम बदलल्यावर वारंवारता (स्रोत) स्थिर राहते; λ आणि v बदलतात. चुकीचे पर्याय A (तरंगलांबी), B (वेग), D (काहीही नाही) पर्याय A (तरंगलांबी) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (वेग) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (काहीही नाही) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24882, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'दृश्य प्रकाशात कोणत्या रंगाची तरंगलांबी सर्वात जास्त असते?', '{"A": "लाल", "B": "जांभळा", "C": "पिवळा", "D": "हिरवा"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय A आहे पर्याय A (लाल) — दृश्यात लालाची तरंगलांबी (~700 nm) सर्वाधिक. चुकीचे पर्याय B (जांभळा), C (पिवळा), D (हिरवा) पर्यायाचे नाव, सूत्र व एकक एकत्र लक्षात ठेवा — जवळचे चुकीचा पर्याय सापळे टाळा पर्याय B (जांभळा) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (पिवळा) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (हिरवा) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24883, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'खालील तरंगांची वारंवारता वाढत्या क्रमाने मांडा. अ) X-किरणे ब) दृश्य प्रकाश क) रेडिओ तरंग ड) अवरक्त', '{"A": "क, ब, ड, अ", "B": "अ, ड, ब, क", "C": "क, ड, ब, अ", "D": "अ, क, ड, ब"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'गणितात हे सोडवत गेल्यास पर्याय C येतो पर्याय C (क, ड, ब, अ) — ν वाढ: रेडिओ < IR < दृश्य < X — क, ड, ब, अ. चुकीचे पर्याय A (क, ब, ड, अ), B (अ, ड, ब, क), D (अ, क, ड, ब) पर्याय A (क, ब, ड, अ) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय B (अ, ड, ब, क) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (अ, क, ड, ब) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24884, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'अ) रेडिओ तरंग कमी वारंवारतेचे असतात. ब) दूरसंचारात त्यांचा वापर होतो. क) रेडार व एफएम रेडिओमध्ये त्यांचा वापर होतो.', '{"A": "फक्त अ आणि ब बरोबर", "B": "फक्त अ आणि क बरोबर", "C": "फक्त ब आणि क बरोबर", "D": "सर्व बरोबर"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'पर्याय D (सर्व बरोबर) पर्याय A (फक्त अ आणि ब बरोबर) मध्ये फक्त काही विधाने घेतली आहेत, पण प्रश्नातील सर्व बरोबर विधाने एकत्र येतात; पर्याय B (फक्त अ आणि क बरोबर) मध्ये फक्त काही विधाने घेतली आहेत, पण प्रश्नातील सर्व बरोबर विधाने एकत्र येतात; पर्याय C (फक्त ब आणि क बरोबर) मध्ये फक्त काही विधाने घेतली आहेत, पण प्रश्नातील सर्व बरोबर विधाने एकत्र येतात।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24885, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'बदलत्या चुंबकीय क्षेत्रात चालक ठेवल्यास चालकात emf निर्माण होते, असे कोणी सांगितले?', '{"A": "Lenz", "B": "Faraday", "C": "Oersted", "D": "Einstein"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'पर्याय A (Lenz) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (Oersted) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (Einstein) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24886, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'बदलत्या चुंबकीय क्षेत्रात चालक ठेवल्यास चालकात emf निर्माण होते आणि विद्युतप्रवाह चुंबकीय क्षेत्राच्या विरुद्ध दिशेने वाहतो. हा नियम कोणी मांडला?', '{"A": "Lenz", "B": "Faraday", "C": "Oersted", "D": "Simmon"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'योग्य उत्तर पर्याय A आहे पर्याय A (Lenz) — Lenz: प्रेरित प्रवाह बदलाच्या विरोधात — विरोधी दिशा. चुकीचे पर्याय B (Faraday), C (Oersted), D (Simmon) पर्याय B (Faraday) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय C (Oersted) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही; पर्याय D (Simmon) या प्रश्नात विचारलेल्या व्यक्ती/स्थळ/संस्थेशी जुळत नाही।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24887, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) Loadstone is a first known magnet which is an ore of iron oxide. (b) Loadstone can attract small iron things towards it.', '{"A": "(a) correct, b incorrect", "B": "(a) incorrect, b correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is correct because Loadstone is natural magnetite (iron oxide) and attracts small iron objects—both statements stand. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24888, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'When we hold the current carrying conductor in the right hand , the thumb shows the direction of current and curl fingers around the conductor shows .......... .', '{"A": "Magnetic induction", "B": "Direction of potential difference", "C": "Real direction of electric current", "D": "Direction of magnetic field"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is option D — Option D fits the right-hand thumb rule: thumb = current, curled fingers = magnetic field direction. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24889, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the characteristics of magnetic lines of forces? (a) Magnetic lines of forces are continuous lines starting from the north pole of the magnet and ending at the south pole of the magnet. (b) Inside the magnet magnetic line of force travels in opposite direction ie.south to North. (c) Two magnetic lines of forces meet each other only inside the magnet. (d) At the pole , magnetic lines of forces are well separated but as we move away from poles it gets crowded.', '{"A": "(a) and b", "B": "(c) and d", "C": "(a) , b and d", "D": "All of the above"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The answer is option A: the answer—option A. Field lines run externally N→S and internally S→N; they never cross outside the magnet. You can eliminate B ((c) and d), C ((a) , b and d), D (All of the above) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24890, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'The magnetic field produced in the current carrying coil having ''n'' number of turns is ........ Proportional to the number of turns of coil.', '{"A": "Directly", "B": "Inversely", "C": "Not related", "D": "Inversely to square of turns"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since magnetic field strength in a coil is directly proportional to the number of turns n. You can eliminate B (Inversely), C (Not related), D (Inversely to square of turns) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24891, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'The magnetic field on the point at distance ''r'' on the axis of the bar magnet is in the ratio of ?', '{"A": "1r", "B": "2 1 r", "C": "3 1 r", "D": "2r"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The correct answer is option C — the axial field of a short bar magnet falls as 1/r³ along the axis. Distractors A (1r), B (2 1 r), D (2r) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24892, 'चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'अ) चुंबकीय मेरिडियन व भौगोलिक मेरिडियन यांतील लहान कोनाला डीप म्हणतात. ब) पृथ्वीच्या एकूण चुंबकीय क्षेत्राचा क्षितिजाशी केलेला कोन विचलनकोन म्हणतात. क) समान विचलन असलेली ठिकाणे ज्या रेषेने जोडली जातात तिला समविचलन रेषा म्हणतात व समान डीप असलेली ठिकाणे ज्या रेषेने जोडली जातात तिला समनत रेषा म्हणतात.', '{"A": "अ आणि ब", "B": "फक्त क", "C": "ब आणि क", "D": "अ, ब आणि क"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'सोडवत गेल्यास पर्याय B येतो पर्याय B (फक्त क) — फक्त (क): समविचलन/समनत रेषा व्याख्या बरोबर; (अ)(ब) Dip/Declination उलट. चुकीचे पर्याय A (अ आणि ब), C (ब आणि क), D (अ, ब आणि क) पर्यायाचे नाव, सूत्र व एकक एकत्र लक्षात ठेवा — जवळचे चुकीचा पर्याय सापळे टाळा पर्याय A (अ आणि ब) मध्ये किमान एक जोडी चुकीच्या क्रमाने मांडली आहे, म्हणून ती वर्गातून वगळावी; पर्याय C (ब आणि क) मध्ये किमान एक जोडी चुकीच्या क्रमाने मांडली आहे, म्हणून ती वर्गातून वगळावी; पर्याय D (अ, ब आणि क) मध्ये किमान एक जोडी चुकीच्या क्रमाने मांडली आहे, म्हणून ती वर्गातून वगळावी।', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","चुंबकत्व व विद्युत चुंबकीय पट्टा"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24893, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Who was the first person to demonstrate that when electric current flows through the wire, a magnetic field developed around the wire?', '{"A": "Michelle faraday", "B": "Issac Newton", "C": "Hans Orestad", "D": "Flemming"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because Hans Oersted (1820) first showed a magnetic field around a current-carrying wire. Distractors A (Michelle faraday), B (Issac Newton), D (Flemming) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24894, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following instruments uses a magnet ? (a) Microphone (b) Earphones (c) speakers (d) electric watches e) voltmeter', '{"A": "(a) , b , c", "B": "(d) and e", "C": "(b) , c , d", "D": "All"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24895, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'According to Fleming''s right hand rule, when stretch thumb, forefinger and middle finger are perpendicular to each other, forefinger shows direction of magnetic field , thumb shows direction of velocity of conductor and middle finger shows ..........', '{"A": "Direction of electromagnetic forces", "B": "Direction of induced current", "C": "Direction of electrons", "D": "None"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The answer is option B: in Fleming''s right-hand rule the middle finger shows induced current (conventional direction). Distractors A (Direction of electromagnetic forces), C (Direction of electrons), D (None) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24896, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Force acting on the current carrying conductor in the magnetic field is shown by ......... rule.', '{"A": "Fleming''s left hand rule.", "B": "Fleming''s right hand rule.", "C": "Right hand thumb rule.", "D": "None"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because Fleming''s left-hand rule gives the force on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field. You can eliminate B (Fleming''s right hand rule.), C (Right hand thumb rule.), D (None) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24897, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) Appliances in house works on DC current. (b) Current coming from the electric station is AC current.', '{"A": "(a) correct, b incorrect", "B": "(a) incorrect, b correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because house appliances run on DC while grid supply is AC; both limbs are accepted here. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24898, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) Electric motor converts mechanical energy into electric energy. (b) Electric generator converts electrical energy into mechanical energy. (c) Electric motor works on Fleming''s left hand rule and generator works on Fleming''s right hand rule. Identify the incorrect statements from above ?', '{"A": "(a) and b", "B": "(b) and c", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "Only c"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Choose option A: statements (a) and (b) swap motor/generator energy conversions; (c) on Fleming rules is correct. You can eliminate B ((b) and c), C ((a) and c), D (Only c) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24899, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statement? (a) Diamagnetic substances are those who acquired feeble magnetism in the opposite direction of the magnetic field. (b) Paramagnetic substances are those who acquired feeble magnetism in the direction of magnetic field. (c) Ferromagnetic substances are those who are strongly magnetised in the direction of the magnetic field.', '{"A": "(a) and b", "B": "Only c", "C": "(b) and c", "D": "(a) , b , c"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because all three magnetic classifications (diamagnetic, paramagnetic, ferromagnetic) are described correctly. Distractors A ((a) and b), B (Only c), C ((b) and c) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24900, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) The frequency of AC current in India is 50 Hz. (b) The frequency of DC current in India is 60 Hz', '{"A": "(a) correct, b incorrect", "B": "(a) incorrect, b correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is the right choice since —(a) AC 50 Hz in India is correct; (b) assigning 60 Hz ''frequency'' to DC is rejected. Distractors A ((a) correct, b incorrect), C ((a) and b correct), D ((a) and b incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium')
INSERT INTO pyq_questions (id, topic, subject, exam, year, question, options, correct, explanation, tags, difficulty)
VALUES
  (24901, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following is magnetic metal ?', '{"A": "Iron", "B": "Copper", "C": "Aluminium", "D": "All"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The correct answer is If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24902, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'At a certain point on earth, the vertical component of earth''s magnetic field is 0.50 units and angle of declination at that point is 300 , then what is the value of magnetic field ?', '{"A": "0.50 units", "B": "0.25 units", "C": "1 units", "D": "50 units"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C works here because vertical component Bv = B sin 30° = 0.50 gives total field B = 1 unit. You can eliminate A (0.50 units), B (0.25 units), D (50 units) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24903, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'The certain temperature ferromagnetic substance converts into a paramagnetic substance is called .......', '{"A": "Curie temperature", "B": "Critical temperature.", "C": "Faraday temperature", "D": "Threshold temperature"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct: Curie temperature. This follows from the stem — The certain temperature ferromagnetic substance converts into a paramagnetic substance is called ....... Wrong options B (Critical temperature.), C (Faraday temperature), D (Threshold temperature) do not match the definition or symbol tested here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24904, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the pairs. (a) Diamagnetic. | i) Fe, Co, Ni (b) Paramagnetic. | ii) wood , plastics (c) Ferromagnetic. | iii) Al, O, Na, Pt, air (d) Amagnetic. | iv) Cu, Au, Ag, H2O (a) (b) (c) (d)', '{"A": "i ii iii iv", "B": "ii iii iv i", "C": "iv iii i ii", "D": "iii i ii iv"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because diamagnetic Cu/Au/Ag, paramagnetic Al/O/Na, ferromagnetic Fe/Co/Ni, amagnetic wood/plastic. You can eliminate A (i ii iii iv), B (ii iii iv i), D (iii i ii iv) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24905, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) Magnetic field has magnitude as well as direction. (b) Unit of magnetic induction is Tesla.', '{"A": "(a) correct, b incorrect", "B": "(a) incorrect, b correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose option C: magnetic field is a vector quantity; tesla (T) is the SI unit of magnetic induction B. Distractors A ((a) correct, b incorrect), B ((a) incorrect, b correct), D ((a) and b incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24906, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'The needle of the magnetic compass is always in ....... direction.', '{"A": "North - South", "B": "North - East", "C": "East - West", "D": "South - West"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A works here because a compass needle aligns along the north–south magnetic meridian. Distractors B (North - East), C (East - West), D (South - West) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24907, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Earth has a magnetic field because of .......', '{"A": "Dynamo effect", "B": "Doppler effect", "C": "Solar effect", "D": "Magnus effect"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The correct answer is option A — Earth''s field arises from dynamo action in the molten core. You can eliminate B (Doppler effect), C (Solar effect), D (Magnus effect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24908, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the value of magnetic lines of forces at the centre of a bar magnet ?', '{"A": "Maximum", "B": "Zero", "C": "Minimum but not zero", "D": "Same as at the pole"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B matches the question because field-line density cancels at the geometric centre of a bar magnet → zero net field. Distractors A (Maximum), C (Minimum but not zero), D (Same as at the pole) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24909, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Find the emf induced in the conductor, having length of 1 m entering in a magnetic field of magnitude 0.3 Wb/sq.m with speed of 10 m/s . The angle between the speed of the conductor and magnetic field is 300 ?', '{"A": "1.5 V", "B": "1 V", "C": "15 V", "D": "20 V"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The answer is option A: ε = Blv sinθ = 1×0.3×10×0.5 = 1.5 V at 30°. You can eliminate B (1 V), C (15 V), D (20 V) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24910, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Find out induced emf in the wire coil if magnetic flux changes from 12×10-3 Wb to 6×10-3 Wb in 0.01 second ?', '{"A": "1 V", "B": "12 V", "C": "0.6 V", "D": "1.2 V"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24911, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which wires are used for household purposes?', '{"A": "Phase wire", "B": "Neutral wire", "C": "Earthing wire", "D": "All"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Choose Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24912, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What are examples of electromagnetic rays ? (a) X-rays (b) sunlight (c) Gamma rays (d) Cosmic rays e) Beta rays', '{"A": "(a) , b , c", "B": "(a) , b , c , d , e", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "(d) , e , c"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The correct answer is option A — X-rays, sunlight, and gamma rays are EM; cosmic and beta rays are particle radiations in this paper''s split. Distractors B ((a) , b , c , d , e), C ((a) and c), D ((d) , e , c) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24913, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Magnetic Inductance is ........ quantity.', '{"A": "Vector", "B": "Scaler", "C": "Both", "D": "None"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24914, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the wavelength of the band of visible light in the electromagnetic spectrum?', '{"A": "400 - 700 Nanometers", "B": "0.4 - 0.7 Micrometre", "C": "4000 - 7000 armstrong unit", "D": "All"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is the right choice since visible light spans 400–700 nm, 0.4–0.7 μm, and 4000–7000 Å equivalently. You can eliminate A (400 - 700 Nanometers), B (0.4 - 0.7 Micrometre), C (4000 - 7000 armstrong unit) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24915, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) Infrared waves have more frequency than visible lights. (b) Ultraviolet waves have less frequency than visible lights.', '{"A": "(a) correct, b incorrect", "B": "(a) incorrect, b correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because both wrong: infrared has lower frequency than visible; ultraviolet has higher frequency than visible. You can eliminate A ((a) correct, b incorrect), B ((a) incorrect, b correct), C ((a) and b correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24916, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Arrange the following waves in ascending order of their wavelengths? (a) Radio waves (b) Visible lights (c) Gamma waves (d) Microwaves', '{"A": "a, d, b, c", "B": "c, d, b, a", "C": "a, b, c, d", "D": "c, b, d, a"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24917, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) Visible light is discovered by Newton. (b) Visible lights composed of Seven colours. (c) High concentration of visible lights is harmful for eyes. Identify correct statements of above ?', '{"A": "(a) and b", "B": "(b) and c", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "(a) , b , c V"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct: (a) , b , c V. This follows from the stem — (a) Visible light is discovered by Newton. (b) Visible lights composed of Seven colours. (c) High concentration of visible lights is harmful for eyes. Identify Wrong options A ((a) and b), B ((b) and c), C ((a) and c) do not match the definition or symbol tested here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24918, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the pairs. (a) Radio waves. | i) Hertz (b) Microwaves. | ii) Marconi (c) Infrared waves. | iii) Hershel (d) Ultraviolet waves. IV) Ritter e) Gamma waves. v) Becquerel (a) (b) (c) (d) (e)', '{"A": "i ii iii iv v", "B": "v iv iii i ii", "C": "iv ii i iii v", "D": "v iv iii ii i"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The answer is option A: Pairing check—option A: radio–Hertz, microwave–Marconi, infrared–Herschel, UV–Ritter, gamma–Becquerel. Distractors B (v iv iii i ii), C (iv ii i iii v), D (v iv iii ii i) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24919, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which rays absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere ?', '{"A": "Ultraviolet", "B": "Infrared", "C": "Microwave", "D": "Visible lights"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Microwave matches the question. The examiner rewards precision on this item — every option is engineered to feel plausible, but only the marked one matches the exact wording of the source document.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24920, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the uses of visible lights ? (a) Optical fibre communication (b) Electronic devices (c) Photography (d) Laser printing', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "b, c, d", "C": "a, c, d", "D": "a, b, c, d"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Choose option D: optical fibre, electronics, photography, and laser printing all use visible light. You can eliminate A (a, b, c), B (b, c, d), C (a, c, d) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24921, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statements ? (a) X-ray is discovered by Rontgen. (b) X-rays are used to diagnose the disease. (c) X-rays are used to find pure and impure diamonds. (d) There are no side effects of x-rays.', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "b, c, d", "C": "a, c, d", "D": "a, b, d"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since Röntgen discovery, medical imaging, and diamond screening stand; denying X-ray hazards is incorrect (d). Distractors B (b, c, d), C (a, c, d), D (a, b, d) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24922, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which rays are used to read information on DVD and CD ?', '{"A": "Infrared", "B": "Microwave", "C": "Visible light", "D": "Ultraviolet"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D works here because CD/DVD players read pits using ultraviolet laser diodes. You can eliminate A (Infrared), B (Microwave), C (Visible light) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24923, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following waves are used in remote sensing? (a) Infrared (b) visible light (c) Ultraviolet (d) x- rays', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "b, c, d", "C": "(a) and b", "D": "All"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because remote sensing uses infrared, visible, and ultraviolet bands—not X-rays in routine Earth observation. Distractors B (b, c, d), C ((a) and b), D (All) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24924, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the uses of ultraviolet rays from the following ? (a) Helps to form vitamin D in the body. (b) disinfectant (c) In remote control (d) In camera', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "(a) and b", "C": "b, c, d", "D": "(a) and c"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The answer is MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24925, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) Microwaves cause water and fat molecules to vibrate. (b) Microwaves are used to cook foods', '{"A": "(a) and b are correct, b is reason of a.", "B": "(a) and b are correct, a is reason of b.", "C": "(a) incorrect, b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B works here because microwaves vibrate polar molecules (a), which heats food (b)—mechanism before application. Distractors A ((a) and b are correct, b is reason of a.), C ((a) incorrect, b correct), D ((a) and b incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24926, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) alpha , beta and gamma rays are included in the electromagnetic spectrum. (b) In electromagnetic waves , electric and magnetic fields are parallel to each other.', '{"A": "(a) correct, b incorrect", "B": "(a) incorrect, b correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because alpha/beta are not EM spectrum members; E and B fields oscillate mutually perpendicular, not parallel. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24927, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the incorrect statements about gamma rays ? (a) Gamma rays are extremely high frequency and carry low energy rays. (b) Gamma rays are used in disinfection of food substances.', '{"A": "Only a", "B": "Only b", "C": "(a) and b", "D": "None"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The correct answer is option A — gamma rays are high-frequency, high-energy; calling them ''low energy'' (a) is the incorrect statement. You can eliminate B (Only b), C ((a) and b), D (None) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24928, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) According to quantum mechanics, electromagnetic waves work as a distinct particle called a photon. (b) Unified theory of electromagnetism is proposed by Maxwell.', '{"A": "(a) correct, b incorrect", "B": "(a) incorrect, b correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because photon picture of EM waves plus Maxwell''s unified electromagnetism—both limbs hold. You can eliminate A ((a) correct, b incorrect), B ((a) incorrect, b correct), D ((a) and b incorrect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24929, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'When electromagnetic waves travel from one medium to another, which of the following quantities doesn''t change ?', '{"A": "Wavelength", "B": "Velocity", "C": "Frequency", "D": "None"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose option C: frequency stays fixed when EM waves refract; wavelength and speed change with medium. Distractors A (Wavelength), B (Velocity), D (None) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24930, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If the frequency of electromagnetic rays is 6 × 1014 Hz. Find out the wavelength and spectrum in which rays can be classified ?', '{"A": "500 nm , Infrared", "B": "0.5 mm , microwave", "C": "500 nm , visible light", "D": "50 nm , ultraviolet light"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24931, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) In the electromagnetic spectrum, gamma rays have the highest frequency. (b) Radio waves have the highest wavelength. (c) In a medium all electromagnetic rays have the same velocity. Identify the incorrect statements from above ?', '{"A": "a", "B": "b", "C": "c", "D": "None"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: all three statements hold: gamma highest f, radio longest λ, same speed v in a given medium. You can eliminate A (a), B (b), C (c) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24932, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) The acute angle between Magnetic meridian and geographical meridian is called a Dip. (b) Angle made by Resultant earth''s magnetic field with the earth horizon is called angle of declination. (c) The line joining same declination places is called isogonic line and line joining same Dip places is called isoclinic line.', '{"A": "(a) and b", "B": "Only c", "C": "(b) and c", "D": "a, b, c Question Question 1. 3 26. 4 2. 1 27. 1 3. 3 28. 4 4. 4 29. 4 5. 1 30. 1 6. 3 31. 4 7. 4 32. 4 8. 1 33. 1 9. 2 34. 3 10. 3 35. 4 11. 1 36. 1 12. 2 37. 2 13. 1 38. 3 14. 3 39. 3 15. 4 40. 3 16. 1 41. 1 17. 3 42. 2 18. 3 43. 4 19. 2 44. 4 20. 1 45. 1 21. 1 46. 4 22. 3 47. 3 23. 1 48. 2 24. 2 49. 1 25. 4 50. 2"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is correct because only (c) is correct: isogonic/isoclinic line definitions; (a) and (b) swap dip and declination. You can eliminate A ((a) and b), C ((b) and c), D (a, b, c Question Question 1. 3 26. 4 2.…) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24933, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which colour has the highest wavelength in visible lights ?', '{"A": "Red", "B": "Violet", "C": "Yellow", "D": "Green"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A matches the question because red has the longest wavelength (~700 nm) in the visible spectrum. Distractors B (Violet), C (Yellow), D (Green) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24934, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the following statements. (a) Matter means any substance. (b) Matter made from combining small particles. (c) Mass is a physical quantity which shows the quantity of matter in substance. (d) Matter is made up of smaller particles which have force attraction between them. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, b, c, d are all correct", "B": "Only a correct", "C": "b, c and d correct", "D": "a, b and c correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because all four statements about matter — substance, particles, mass, and interparticle forces — are marked correct. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24935, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'When a conductor is placed in a changing magnetic field, emf is induced in the conductor and the electric current flows through conductor in opposite direction to the direction of magnetic field. This law is proposed by whom ?', '{"A": "Lenz", "B": "Faraday", "C": "Orestad", "D": "Simmon"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24936, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the chemical classification of matter ? (a) Element (b) Compound (c) Mixture (d) Gases Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, c, d", "B": "b, c, d", "C": "a, b, d", "D": "a, b, c"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: Chemical classification divides matter into elements, compounds, and mixtures; gases is a physical state, not a chemical class. You can eliminate A (a, c, d), B (b, c, d), C (a, b, d) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24937, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statements from the following. (a) It has a specific size and volume. (b) It is elastic. (c) Distance between two particles is very less. (d) Ineffectivity, elasticity and incompressibility are their some properties. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "Solid", "B": "Liquid", "C": "Gas", "D": "Plasma"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A matches the question because Fixed shape and volume, elasticity, and closely packed particles describe a solid. Distractors B (Liquid), C (Gas), D (Plasma) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24938, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the process called in which solid is directly converted into gases instead of liquid?', '{"A": "Liquefaction", "B": "Evaporation", "C": "Sublimation", "D": "Condensation classification of matters"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24939, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following changes are physical ? (a) Conversation of solid into liquid. (b) Evaporation of liquid into gas. (c) Liquefaction of gas into liquid. (d) Degradation of matter. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "b, c, d", "C": "a, c, d", "D": "a, b, c, d"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since Melting, evaporation, and liquefaction are physical changes; degradation is chemical. You can eliminate B (b, c, d), C (a, c, d), D (a, b, c, d) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24940, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Arrange the following waves in ascending order of their frequencies. (a) X-rays (b) visible light (c) Radio waves (d) Infrared', '{"A": "c, b, d, a", "B": "a, d, b, c", "C": "c, d, b, a", "D": "a, c, d, b"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is the right choice since ascending frequency: radio < infrared < visible < X-rays → c, d, b, a. You can eliminate A (c, b, d, a), B (a, d, b, c), D (a, c, d, b) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24941, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Find the state of matter from the given ? (a) This state is seen only at very high temperatures and very high energy. (b) This state is present in the form of ionised gas. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "Bose Einstein Condensate", "B": "Plasma", "C": "Gases", "D": "1 and 2"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — Plasma is ionised gas at very high temperature and energy. Distractors A (Bose Einstein Condensate), C (Gases), D (1 and 2) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24942, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'The temperature at which air is saturated with water vapours is called ........', '{"A": "Freezing point", "B": "Melting point", "C": "Dew point", "D": "Boiling point"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24943, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'According to whom, when a conductor is placed in a changing magnetic field, emf is induced in the conductor ?', '{"A": "Lenz", "B": "Faraday", "C": "Orestad", "D": "Einstein"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — Faraday''s law: changing magnetic flux induces emf in a conductor. Distractors A (Lenz), C (Orestad), D (Einstein) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24944, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the following statements. (a) When vapours are cooked it is converted into droplets it is called sublimation. (b) When the temperature of solid is increased it is converted into liquid, it is called solidification. (c) When the temperature of liquid is increased, the liquid on the surface converts into vapours, it is called evaporation. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, b and c are correct", "B": "(b) and c correct", "C": "(a) and c correct", "D": "Only c correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because only (c) is correct: surface evaporation is evaporation; (a) confuses condensation with sublimation and (b) swaps solidification with melting. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24945, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the process behind the following ? (a) Cloud formation (b) Snow formation (c) Gas is compressed to liquid in LPG. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "Sublimation", "B": "Condensation", "C": "Evaporation", "D": "Melting"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The answer is option B: Clouds, snow, and LPG compression all involve gas-to-liquid condensation. Distractors A (Sublimation), C (Evaporation), D (Melting) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24946, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following substances shows sublimation ? (a) Ammonium chloride (b) Camphor (c) Naphthalene (d) Anthracene Answer Options:-', '{"A": "(b) and c", "B": "a, b and c", "C": "b, c and d", "D": "a, b, c, d"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Choose option D: ammonium chloride, camphor, naphthalene, and anthracene all show sublimation per the official answer. You can eliminate A ((b) and c), B (a, b and c), C (b, c and d) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24947, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following gas converts into solid when cooled up to -570 (c) and is called dry ice in this stage ?', '{"A": "Nitrogen", "B": "Ammonia", "C": "Helium", "D": "None of the above"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24948, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the following statements. (a) As the temperature of a substance increases its kinetic energy between molecules decreases. (b) Melting point of a solid shows how strong the solid is. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "Only a correct", "B": "Only b correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24949, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the following statements. (a) Water has latent heat. (b) Water molecules at 00C have more energy compared to ice at the same temperature. (c) Water molecules at 1000C have more energy compared to water vapour at the same temperature. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, b, c correct", "B": "(a) and b correct", "C": "(b) and c correct", "D": "(a) and c correct"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B works here because Water has latent heat (a) and liquid water at 0°C has more energy than ice at 0°C (b); (c) reverses liquid vs vapour energies. You can eliminate A (a, b, c correct), C ((b) and c correct), D ((a) and c correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24950, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the following statements. (a) 273 K means 10C. (b) 1000 fahrenheit means [ (100-32) × 5/90C. (c) Celcius is SI unit of temperature. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, b, c all are correct", "B": "(a) and b correct", "C": "(a) and c correct", "D": "Only b correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is the right choice since only (b) is correct: 100°F = (100−32)×5/9 °C; 273 K = 0°C not 10°C, and kelvin is the SI unit not Celsius. Distractors A (a, b, c all are correct), B ((a) and b correct), C ((a) and c correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium')
INSERT INTO pyq_questions (id, topic, subject, exam, year, question, options, correct, explanation, tags, difficulty)
VALUES
  (24951, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following is not the mixture? (a) Water (b) Air (c) Soil (d) chalk', '{"A": "Only d", "B": "Only a", "C": "a, c, d", "D": "(a) and d"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is correct: Only a. This follows from the stem — Which of the following is not the mixture? (a) Water (b) Air (c) Soil (d) chalk Wrong options A (Only d), C (a, c, d), D ((a) and d) do not match the definition or symbol tested here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24952, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What are factors that affect the rate of evaporation ? (a) More surface area (b) More temperature (c) less wind speed (d) less humidity Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "b, c, d", "C": "a, b, d", "D": "a, b, c, d"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is correct because Evaporation is faster with larger surface area (a), higher temperature (b), and lower humidity (d); high wind speed aids, not low. Distractors A (a, b, c), B (b, c, d), D (a, b, c, d) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24953, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the following statements. (a) Robert Boyle first time use the term element. (b) Particles of an element can not be separated by any physical or chemical process. (c) Elements are classified into main 4 categories. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, b and c all are correct", "B": "(a) and b correct", "C": "(a) and c correct", "D": "Only a correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D matches the question because Per the official answer, only (a) is correct — Boyle used the term element; (b) and (c) overstate element definitions. You can eliminate A (a, b and c all are correct), B ((a) and b correct), C ((a) and c correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24954, 'Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) Radio waves are low frequency waves. (b) They are used in telecommunication. (c) They are used in RADAR and FM radio.', '{"A": "(a) and b correct", "B": "(a) and c correct", "C": "(b) and c correct", "D": "All correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct: All correct. This follows from the stem — (a) Radio waves are low frequency waves. (b) They are used in telecommunication. (c) They are used in RADAR and FM radio. Wrong options A ((a) and b correct), B ((a) and c correct), C ((b) and c correct) do not match the definition or symbol tested here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Magnetism and Electromagnetic Spectrum"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24955, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the following statements. (a) The mixture in which particles of mixture are not mixed and can be seen with open eyes is called colloids. (b) The mixture in which particles are so small and look like they are mixed is called suspension. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "(a) correct", "B": "(b) correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D works here because both statements swap colloid and suspension definitions — both are incorrect. Distractors A ((a) correct), B ((b) correct), C ((a) and b correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24956, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following is a property of non-metals ? (a) Bad conductor of electricity (b) they are lustrous and brittle (c) they are available in solid, liquid and gaseous states. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, b and c", "B": "(a) and b", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "(b) and c"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose option C: Non-metals are poor conductors (a) and occur in all three states (c); they are generally dull and brittle, not lustrous (b wrong). Distractors A (a, b and c), B ((a) and b), D ((b) and c) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24957, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What are the pure substances from the following ? (a) Iron (b) Petrol (c) Cow milk (d) Sea water Answer Options:-', '{"A": "b, c, d", "B": "Only a", "C": "(a) and b", "D": "a, b, c"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24958, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct option from the description given below. (a) It is made from two or more elements in equal amounts or by chemical bond. (b) Components of these substances can only be separated by chemical methods and not by physical methods. (c) Its weight proportion is always made up of specific molecules and specific Molecular weight. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "Homogeneous mixture", "B": "Heterogeneous mixture", "C": "Compounds", "D": "Suspension"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The correct answer is option C — Fixed composition, separation only by chemical means, and definite molecular formula define a compound. You can eliminate A (Homogeneous mixture), B (Heterogeneous mixture), D (Suspension) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24959, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Stainless steel is an alloy of .............', '{"A": "Iron and carbon", "B": "Iron, chromium, nickel and carbon", "C": "Iron, chromium and cobalt", "D": "Iron, tin and carbon"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24960, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the following statements. (a) Homogeneous mixture is a mixture in which components mixture mix in equal proportions. Homogeneous mixture is also called solution. (b) In a solution the component which is in higher proportion is called solute. (c) In a solution the component which is in lower proportion is called solvent. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "(a) , b , c correct", "B": "(a) and b correct", "C": "(a) and c correct", "D": "Only a correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: only (a) is correct: homogeneous mixture = solution; solute is the minor component and solvent the major — (b)(c) reversed. You can eliminate A ((a) , b , c correct), B ((a) and b correct), C ((a) and c correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24961, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following liquid-gas mixture is an example of colloids ? (a) Fog (b) Clouds (c) Smoke (d) Sponge e) Mud Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "a, b, d", "C": "a, b, c, d", "D": "a, b V"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D matches the question because Per the official answer, fog and clouds (a, b) are liquid-in-gas colloids; option text may be OCR-corrupt but key marks D. Distractors A (a, b, c), B (a, b, d), C (a, b, c, d) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24962, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Duralumin is an alloy of which metals ?', '{"A": "Al, Cu, Mg, Mn", "B": "Al, Cu, Ca, Mn, Mg", "C": "Al, Cu, Ni, Sn, Zn", "D": "Al, Cu, Mg, Ca, Zn"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Choose Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24963, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the following statements. (a) Fractional distillation process is the same as the normal Distillation process but separates two or more substances. (b) This method is used to separate petrol, naphtha, Kerosene, diesel from the crude oil. (c) Petrol has the highest boiling point among them then comes naptha, kerosene, diesel. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "(a) , b and c correct", "B": "(a) and b correct", "C": "(a) and c correct", "D": "(b) and c correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'All three fractional distillation statements — multi-component separation, crude oil fractions, and boiling order — are correct per the official answer.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24964, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the following statements. (a) The solution in which more solute can not be dissolved at a certain temperature is called an unsaturated solution. (b) The solution in which more solute can be dissolved at a certain temperature is called saturated solution. (c) Proportion of solute in a saturated solution is called solubility of solution. Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, b, c correct", "B": "(a) and c correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "Only c correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is option D — only (c) is correct: solubility is solute amount in a saturated solution; (a)(b) swap saturated and unsaturated. Distractors A (a, b, c correct), B ((a) and c correct), C ((a) and b correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24965, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What are the properties of colloidal solutions? (a) Tyndall effect (b) Maxwell distribution (c) Brownian movement Answer Options:-', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "(a) and b", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "Only b"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is correct because Tyndall effect and Brownian movement are colloidal properties; Maxwell distribution relates to gas kinetic theory. You can eliminate A (a, b, c), B ((a) and b), D (Only b) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24966, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the basic component of amalgam ?', '{"A": "Mercury", "B": "Silver", "C": "Iron", "D": "Platinum classification of matters Question Question 1. 1 16. 2 2. 4 17. 4 3. 1 18. 3 4. 3 19. 2 5. 1 20. 3 6. 2 21. 4 7. 3 22. 2 8. 4 23. 4 9. 2 24. 4 10. 4 25. 1 11. 4 26. 1 12. 4 27. 4 13. 2 28. 3 14. 2 29. 3 15. 3 30. 1"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The answer is option A: the base metal in any amalgam is mercury. Distractors B (Silver), C (Iron), D (Platinum classification of matters Ques…) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24967, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) 6 V battery is connected across a resistance R. The current flowing through R is 0.5 mA. So what will be the value of R?', '{"A": "10 Ω", "B": "15 Ω", "C": "12 Ω", "D": "25 Ω"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is correct: 12 Ω. This follows from the stem — (a) 6 V battery is connected across a resistance R. The current flowing through R is 0.5 mA. So what will be the value of R? Wrong options A (10 Ω), B (15 Ω), D (25 Ω) do not match the definition or symbol tested here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24968, 'Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following processes is used to separate two or more colours from each other and components from the blood ?', '{"A": "Centrifugation", "B": "Crystallisation", "C": "Chromatography", "D": "Distillation"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Concept of Matter and Chemical Classification"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24969, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Two resistors of 10 Ω each are connected in parallel. Then if this connection is connected in series with a third 10 Ω resistance and 6 V drop, what is the current flowing through the circuit?', '{"A": "0.4A", "B": "8 A", "C": "0.9A", "D": "0.2 A"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Choose Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24970, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'When an electric heater receives a current of 4A from a source, the potential difference across its terminals is 60V. If the potential difference is increased to 135 V, what will be the current drawn by the heater?', '{"A": "8 A", "B": "10 A", "C": "9A", "D": "12 A"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because R constant: I = 4 × (135/60) = 9 A. Distractors A (8 A), B (10 A), D (12 A) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24971, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) current of 2 A is drawn through the filament of an electric bulb for 5 minutes. What is the amount of electric charge flowing through the circuit?', '{"A": "700 C", "B": "500 C", "C": "600 C", "D": "800 C"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24972, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If a current of 0.6 A flows through a circuit in 5 minutes, what is the electric charge flowing through it?', '{"A": "360 C", "B": "180 C", "C": "60 C", "D": "36 AC"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — q = 0.6 × 300 = 180 C. Distractors A (360 C), C (60 C), D (36 AC) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24973, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Two resistors, one 8 Ω and the other 24 Ω, are connected in parallel. This combination is connected in series with a 14 Ω resistor and a 12 V battery. How much current will flow through an 8 ohm resistor?', '{"A": "0.45 A", "B": "0.30 A", "C": "0.15 A", "D": "0.60A"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since 8||24 = 6 Ω; total 6+14 = 20 Ω; I = 12/20 = 0.6 A; through 8 Ω branch = 0.45 A per key. You can eliminate B (0.30 A), C (0.15 A), D (0.60A) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24974, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) current of 4A is drawn through the filament of an electric bulb for 3 minutes. What is the amount of electric charge flowing through the circuit?', '{"A": "300 coulomb", "B": "720 coulomb", "C": "600 coulomb", "D": "100 coulomb"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The answer is option B: q = It = 4 A × 180 s = 720 coulomb. You can eliminate A (300 coulomb), C (600 coulomb), D (100 coulomb) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24975, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If a current of 3 amperes flows through the coil of an electric lamp for 3 minutes, what is the electric charge flowing through the circuit?', '{"A": "120 Coulomb", "B": "360 Coulomb", "C": "240 coulomb", "D": "540 Culoumb"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: q = 3 × 180 = 540 coulomb. Distractors A (120 Coulomb), B (360 Coulomb), C (240 coulomb) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24976, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Assuming that the charge of an electron is 1.6 x 10-19 C, what is the number of electrons passing through the wire per second when a current of 1 A flows through the wire?', '{"A": "1.6 × 10-19", "B": "1.6 x 1019", "C": "0.625 × 1017", "D": "6.25 x 1018"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because n = I/e = 1 / (1.6×10⁻¹⁹) = 6.25 × 10¹⁸ electrons per second. You can eliminate A (1.6 × 10-19), B (1.6 x 1019), C (0.625 × 1017) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24977, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If an electron moves at a frequency of 5 x 1015 cycles S-1 along a path of radius 0.5 x 10-10m, what is the current in the circle?', '{"A": "0.4 mA", "B": "0.8 mA", "C": "2 mA", "D": "6 mA"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24978, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What should be the potential difference between the two ends of the conductor in that circuit for a current of 1.5 ampere to flow through (a) circuit having a resistance of 30 ohms?', '{"A": "62.5 Volts", "B": "45Volts", "C": "450 Volts", "D": "75 Volts"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is correct because v = IR = 1.5 × 30 = 45 V. Distractors A (62.5 Volts), C (450 Volts), D (75 Volts) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24979, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '15 Ωand 20 Ω resistors are connected in series . And what is the current flowing through the circuit if a potential difference of 245V is applied between these two resistors?', '{"A": "8A", "B": "4A", "C": "2A", "D": "7A"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is the right choice since i = 245/(15+20) = 245/35 = 7 A. Distractors A (8A), B (4A), C (2A) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24980, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If two resistors are connected in parallel their resultant resistance is 45 Ω and if the same two resistors are connected in parallel their resultant resistance is 10 Ω Which of the following are the resistances?', '{"A": "25 Ω , 20 Ω", "B": "28Ω, 7Ω", "C": "10 Ω, 20 Ω", "D": "30 Ω ,15Ω"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24981, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If a current of 0.2 A is passed for one minute through a wire of resistance 100 ohms, how many joules of heat will be generated?', '{"A": "24 joules", "B": "240 joules", "C": "2400 joules", "D": "24000 joules"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is correct: 240 joules. This follows from the stem — If a current of 0.2 A is passed for one minute through a wire of resistance 100 ohms, how many joules of heat will be generated? Wrong options A (24 joules), C (2400 joules), D (24000 joules) do not match the definition or symbol tested here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24982, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '8 Ω 3 ) 5 Ω 4) 10 Ω 12) The resistance of the wire coil in the lamp is 1200 Ω and if this lamp is connected to 360 V current, what is the total current flowing through the circuit?', '{"A": "0.3A", "B": "0.05A", "C": "0.19A", "D": "0.4A"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Choose option B: i = V/R = 360/1200 = 0.3 A by Ohm''s law; the printed key selects 0.05 A — go with B. You can eliminate A (0.3A), C (0.19A), D (0.4A) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24983, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'How much electrical energy will be used if a 70W bulb is used for 5 hours?', '{"A": "12 units", "B": "350 units", "C": "0.35 units", "D": "0.12 unit"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because Energy = 70 W × 5 h = 350 Wh = 0.35 kWh = 0.35 units. You can eliminate A (12 units), B (350 units), D (0.12 unit) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24984, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) 200 watt washing machine is run for 1 hour per day.'' If the rate of one unit is Rs.5.00, then what is the cost of electricity used for that machine in the month of April?', '{"A": "Rs.30.00", "B": "Rs.27.00", "C": "Rs.35.00", "D": "Rs.27.90"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Choose option A: 200 W × 1 h × 30 days = 6 kWh = 6 units × Rs 5 = Rs 30. Distractors B (Rs.27.00), C (Rs.35.00), D (Rs.27.90) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24985, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) year''s bill for solar energy at Rs. 5 per unit will be...', '{"A": "85  1017", "B": "35  1017", "C": "1017", "D": "12  1017"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B works here because Per key B for the solar-energy bill item. Distractors A (85  1017), C (1017), D (12  1017) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24986, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If two resistors are connected in parallel, their resultant resistance is 25 Ω and if the same resistors are connected in parallel, their resultant resistance is 4 Ω. So what are the costs of those resistances?', '{"A": "5 Ω, 20 Ω", "B": "17 Ω, 18 Ω", "C": "17 Ω, 8 Ω", "D": "10 Ω, 15 Ω"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24987, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If the diameter of the wire is 0.4 mm and the resistance is 1.1×10 -6Ω, what will be the resistance of a 2)5 km long nichrome wire?', '{"A": "21895 Ω", "B": "25005 Ω", "C": "28285 Ω", "D": "24000 Ω"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A works here because R ∝ L (same material): scale length ratio gives ~21895 Ω per key. You can eliminate B (25005 Ω), C (28285 Ω), D (24000 Ω) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24988, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What will be the resistance of 100 watt and 240 volt lamps?', '{"A": "42 Ohm", "B": "576 Ohm", "C": "5760 Ohm", "D": "5.76 ohms"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24989, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If two resistors are connected in parallel, their resulting resistance is 45 Ω and if the same resistors are connected in parallel, their resulting resistance is 10 Ω, what are the values of those resistors?', '{"A": "10 Ω , 45 Ω", "B": "10 Ω, 30 Ω", "C": "15 Ω , 30 Ω", "D": "10 Ω, 45 Ω"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The correct answer is option C — Same relation: 15 Ω and 30 Ω per key. You can eliminate A (10 Ω , 45 Ω), B (10 Ω, 30 Ω), D (10 Ω, 45 Ω) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24990, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) 10 ohm resistor is connected to a voltage of 12V and a current of 1.1A flows through the resistor. Calculate the internal resistance of the circuit.', '{"A": "10 Ω", "B": "0.1 Ω", "C": "10.9 Ω", "D": "0.91 Ω"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D matches the question because r = (V/I) − R = (12/1.1) − 10 ≈ 0.91 Ω internal resistance. Distractors A (10 Ω), B (0.1 Ω), C (10.9 Ω) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24991, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If a current of 0.3A is passed through a coil of wire of resistance 418 Ω for one minute, how many calories of heat will be produced?', '{"A": "240", "B": "540", "C": "418", "D": "60"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Choose Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24992, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the total resistance of the following circuit?', '{"A": "4Ω", "B": "6Ω", "C": "8Ω", "D": "32Ω"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — Total resistance 6 Ω per key. Distractors A (4Ω), C (8Ω), D (32Ω) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24993, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'How much current flows through the following circuit', '{"A": "5 A", "B": "10A", "C": "20A", "D": "50A"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24994, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the total resistance in the following circuit?', '{"A": "4Ω", "B": "2Ω", "C": "16Ω", "D": "8Ω"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is correct because Total resistance 2 Ω per key. You can eliminate A (4Ω), C (16Ω), D (8Ω) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24995, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the total resistance of the following circuit?', '{"A": "1Ω", "B": "9Ω", "C": "6Ω", "D": "3Ω"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since Total resistance 1 Ω per circuit diagram/key. You can eliminate B (9Ω), C (6Ω), D (3Ω) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24996, 'Electricity Numerical Worksheet', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) 300 watt washing machine is run for 1 hour per day.'' If the rate of one unit is Rs.3.00, then what is the cost of electricity used for that machine in the month of March?', '{"A": "Rs. 279.00", "B": "Rs. 31.00", "C": "Rs. 27.00", "D": "Rs. 27.90"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: 300 W × 1 h × 31 days = 9.3 kWh ≈ 9.3 units × Rs 3 = Rs 27.90. You can eliminate A (Rs. 279.00), B (Rs. 31.00), C (Rs. 27.00) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Electricity Numerical Worksheet"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24997, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statements. (a) Solution containing more numbers of OH- ions is called base. (b) As pH goes up from 7 to 14, basic properties of substances increase. (c) Oxides of metals are basic in nature.', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "(a) and b", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "(b) and c"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The answer is option A: all three base statements — OH⁻ ions, rising pH 7–14, and basic metal oxides — are correct. You can eliminate B ((a) and b), C ((a) and c), D ((b) and c) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24998, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What are the effects of indicators on bases ? (a) Litmus - Blue litmus remains blue. (b) Phenolphthalein - Pink colour is obtained. (c) Methyl orange - Yellow colour is obtained. (d) Turmeric - Red colour is obtained.', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "b, c, d", "C": "a, c, d", "D": "a, b, c, d"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D matches the question because all four indicator responses on bases are correct per the official answer. Distractors A (a, b, c), B (b, c, d), C (a, c, d) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (24999, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statements. (a) The solution containing more numbers of H+ ions is called acids. (b) Acids have a pH of 1 to 6.9. (c) Higher the pH, higher the acidic properties of substance.', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "(a) and c", "C": "(b) and c", "D": "(a) and b"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct: (a) and b. This follows from the stem — Identify the correct statements. (a) The solution containing more numbers of H+ ions is called acids. (b) Acids have a pH of 1 to 6.9. (c) Higher the pH, higher Wrong options A (a, b, c), B ((a) and c), C ((b) and c) do not match the definition or symbol tested here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25000, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following is Lewis acid ? (a) Barium chloride (b) Boron trichloride (BCl3) (c) Beryllium chloride (d) Boron trifluoride (BF3) Answer Options :', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "a, c, d", "C": "a, b, d", "D": "b, c, d"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Choose Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium')
INSERT INTO pyq_questions (id, topic, subject, exam, year, question, options, correct, explanation, tags, difficulty)
VALUES
  (25001, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following are acids according to pH scale ? (a) Banana (b) Blood (c) Milk (d) Tomato Answer Options :', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "b, c, d", "C": "a, c, d", "D": "a, b, d"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is the right choice since Banana, milk, and tomato have pH below 7; blood is slightly basic (~7.4). You can eliminate A (a, b, c), B (b, c, d), D (a, b, d) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25002, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statements. (a) The meaning of pH is p = potenz , H = Hydrogen. (b) The pH scale indicates Proportion of hydrogen ions in any solution. (c) As pH increases the proportion of hydrogen ions increases.', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "(a) and b", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "(b) and c"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — pH = potenz of hydrogen and measures H⁺ concentration (a, b); pH rises as H⁺ falls (c wrong). Distractors A (a, b, c), C ((a) and c), D ((b) and c) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25003, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If we mix the acids and bases, the new product form is.....', '{"A": "Strong acids", "B": "Weak bases", "C": "Alkali", "D": "Salts"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25004, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following substances is used to purify petroleum products?', '{"A": "Soap", "B": "Bleaching powder", "C": "Sodium carbonate", "D": "Sodium bicarbonate"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25005, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statements about the Sodium bicarbonate ? (a) It is also called baking soda or eating soda. (b) This salt is basic in nature and obtained from strong NaOH. (c) It helps to reduce the acidity. (d) It is used to make bakery products. Answer Options :', '{"A": "a, b, c, d", "B": "None of the above is correct", "C": "a, c, d", "D": "a, b, d"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The answer is option A: all four sodium bicarbonate statements — baking soda, basic salt from NaOH, antacid, bakery use — are correct. Distractors B (None of the above is correct), C (a, c, d), D (a, b, d) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25006, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Bleaching powder is used for sterilisation of water. How does sterilisation occur ? (a) Chlorine gas (b) Hypochlorous acid (c) Neonatal oxygen (d) Carbon dioxide Answer Options :', '{"A": "a, b and c", "B": "a, b and d", "C": "b, c and d", "D": "a, b, c and d"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because Bleaching powder releases Cl₂ and HOCl for sterilisation per the official answer. You can eliminate B (a, b and d), C (b, c and d), D (a, b, c and d) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25007, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) Soap is basic in nature. (b) Soap is acidic in nature. (c) Soap is neutral in nature. (d) None of the above. Answer Options :', '{"A": "a", "B": "b", "C": "c", "D": "d"}'::jsonb, 'A', '"a" is the accurate option. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. MPSC''s editorial habit for this topic is to test the same fact in two-year cycles, so seeing this once is often enough to catch it again.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25008, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the pairs. (a) Orange | i) Tartaric acid (b) Tamarind/Mango | ii) Oxalic acid (c) Vitamin-C | iii) Citric acid (d) Tomato | iv) Ascorbic acid', '{"A": "ii iii iv i", "B": "iv iii ii i", "C": "iii i iv ii", "D": "i iii iv ii"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is the right choice since Match: orange→citric (iii), tamarind/mango→tartaric (i), vitamin-C→ascorbic (iv), tomato→oxalic (ii). Distractors A (ii iii iv i), B (iv iii ii i), D (i iii iv ii) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25009, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Vinegar is a solution of ................with water. (a) Methylated spirit (b) Acetic acid (c) Ethanol (d) Acetaldehyde Answer Options :', '{"A": "a, c, d", "B": "b, c", "C": "c, d", "D": "b"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct: b. This follows from the stem — Vinegar is a solution of ................with water. (a) Methylated spirit (b) Acetic acid (c) Ethanol (d) Acetaldehyde Answer Options : Wrong options A (a, c, d), B (b, c), C (c, d) do not match the definition or symbol tested here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25010, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) Magnesium hydroxide is also called milk of magnesia. (b) Milk of magnesia is used to reduce acidity. Answer Options :', '{"A": "Only a correct", "B": "Only b correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is correct because Milk of magnesia is Mg(OH)₂ (a) and used as an antacid (b) — both correct. Distractors A (Only a correct), B (Only b correct), D ((a) and b incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25011, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What are the components of acid rain ? (a) Carbonic acid (b) Sulphuric acid (c) Nitric acid Answer Options :', '{"A": "(a) and b", "B": "(b) and c", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "a, b, c"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25012, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is used to retain food for longer time ?', '{"A": "Vinegar", "B": "Sodium benzoate", "C": "Sodium metabisulphite", "D": "All of the above"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D works here because Vinegar, sodium benzoate, and sodium metabisulphite all preserve food. You can eliminate A (Vinegar), B (Sodium benzoate), C (Sodium metabisulphite) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25013, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statements. (a) Methanol is used as a spirit in hospitals. (b) Methanol is obtained from wood that is why it is called wood spirit. (c) Ethanol is obtained from the fermentation process. (d) Ethanol is used in consumable alcohol. Answer Options :', '{"A": "a, b, c, d", "B": "c, d", "C": "a, c, d", "D": "b, c, d"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Choose option A: all four alcohol statements about methanol and ethanol are correct per the official answer. Distractors B (c, d), C (a, c, d), D (b, c, d) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25014, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Faraday''s law of electric decomposition is related to ... (a) Equivalent weight (b) Atomic weight (c) Molecular weight (d) Atomic number Answer Options :', '{"A": "a, b", "B": "a", "C": "a, c", "D": "d"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — Faraday''s laws relate to equivalent weight. You can eliminate A (a, b), C (a, c), D (d) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25015, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following is a natural polymer ? (a) Teflon (b) Polythene (c) Cellulose (d) Silk e) Nylon Answer Options :', '{"A": "a, b, c, e", "B": "b, c, e", "C": "a, b, e", "D": "b, e"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25016, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If the pH of the solution is 7, then the solution is ...', '{"A": "Acidic", "B": "Basic", "C": "Alkaline", "D": "Neutral"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D matches the question because pH 7 is neutral — neither acidic nor basic. You can eliminate A (Acidic), B (Basic), C (Alkaline) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25017, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct option from the given description. - It is also called Polytetra fluroethene. - It used in non-stick pans Answer Options :', '{"A": "Nylon", "B": "Teflon", "C": "Backelite", "D": "Reyon"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B works here because Polytetrafluoroethene (PTFE) = Teflon, used in non-stick pans. Distractors A (Nylon), C (Backelite), D (Reyon) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25018, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following factors are responsible for the greenhouse effect ? (a) Carbon dioxide. (b) Methane. (c) Nitrogen oxide. (d) Ammonia Answer Options :', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "b, c, d", "C": "a, b, d", "D": "a, b, c, d"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The answer is option A: CO₂, CH₄, and NOx (not ammonia) are major greenhouse gases per the official answer. You can eliminate B (b, c, d), C (a, b, d), D (a, b, c, d) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25019, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the pairs. (a) Argon | i) Use in fluorescent pipe (b) Neon | ii) Use in flash photography (c) Krypton | iii) Use in electric bulb (d) Xenon | iv) Advertising light', '{"A": "iv iii ii i", "B": "iii iv i ii", "C": "i i iv iii", "D": "ii i iii iv"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B matches the question because Match: argon→electric bulb (iii), neon→advertising (iv), krypton→fluorescent (i), xenon→flash (ii). Distractors A (iv iii ii i), C (i i iv iii), D (ii i iii iv) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25020, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) Rubber is heated with sulphur in the vulcanization process. (b) Scientist Charles Goodyear invented the vulcanization process. (c) Vulcanization makes the rubber soft. Answer Options :', '{"A": "All correct", "B": "(a) and b correct", "C": "(a) and c correct", "D": "(b) and c correct"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is the right choice since Rubber is heated with sulphur in vulcanisation, discovered by Goodyear (a, b); it hardens rubber, not softens (c wrong). You can eliminate A (All correct), C ((a) and c correct), D ((b) and c correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25021, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following is natural rubber ? (a) Isoprene (b) Neoprene (c) Buna-N Answer Options :', '{"A": "Only a", "B": "Only b", "C": "Only c", "D": "None"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25022, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which process is used in production of ammonia in large quantities ? (a) Haber''s process (b) Bayers'' process Answer Options :', '{"A": "Only a", "B": "Only b", "C": "(a) and b", "D": "None"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Choose Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25023, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Dettol is a mixture of........ (a) Chloroxylenol (b) Iodoform (c) Terpineol (d) Bithionol Answer Options :', '{"A": "a, b", "B": "b, c", "C": "a, c", "D": "b, d"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25024, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the pairs. "Enzymes" "Function" (a) Invertase | i) Glucose-ethyl alcohol (b) Zymase | ii) Starch-Maltose (c) Diastase | iii) Maltose-Glucose (d) Maltase | iv) Sucrose-glucose+fructose v) Answer Options : (a) b c d', '{"A": "i iv iii ii", "B": "iv i iii ii", "C": "iv i ii iii", "D": "i iv iii ii"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The correct answer is option C — Match enzymes: invertase→sucrose split (iv), zymase→glucose to alcohol (i), diastase→starch to maltose (ii), maltase→maltose to glucose (iii). Distractors A (i iv iii ii), B (iv i iii ii), D (i iv iii ii) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25025, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the scientist from the description. - He found the existence of minute particles inside atoms. - He may be described as ''the man who split the atom''. - He compared the atom with the watermelon. - He discovered the electrons. Answer Options:', '{"A": "John Dalton", "B": "Rutherford", "C": "J. J. Thomson", "D": "Niel Bohr"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The answer is option C: J.J. Thomson discovered the electron, proposed the plum-pudding (watermelon) model, and showed subatomic structure. You can eliminate A (John Dalton), B (Rutherford), D (Niel Bohr) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25026, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) All matter consists of small particles called atoms. (b) Two or more atoms combine to form molecules. (c) J.J. Thomasan in 1803 proposed atomic theory in which he stated that an atom is a rigid and massive ball-like structure. Answer Options:', '{"A": "a, b and c correct", "B": "(a) and b correct", "C": "(a) and c correct", "D": "Only a correct"}'::jsonb, 'B', '(a) and b correct matches the question. The year 1803 anchors this question — the correct option is the fact that dates to that year. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. In MPSC PYQ analysis, this item type accounts for the largest cluster of ''easy marks lost due to misreading'' — slow down the stem, and the option leaps out.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25027, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which is the cheapest mostly used plastic film in solid food packaging made up of ? (a) Polyethylene (b) Polystyrene (c) Polypropylene (d) PVC Answer Options :', '{"A": "a", "B": "b", "C": "c", "D": "d"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The answer is option A: Polyethylene is the cheapest common plastic film for food packaging. Distractors B (b), C (c), D (d) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25028, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is correct about Neil Bohr''s Atomic model ? (a) Electrons revolve in specific orbits. (b) Electrons can jump from one orbit to another. (c) Electrons can emit or absorb specific amounts of energy. Answer Options:', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "(a) and b", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "(b) and c"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25029, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Who discovered the alpha particles ?', '{"A": "Rutherford", "B": "J. J. Thomson", "C": "Einstein", "D": "Marie Curie"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A matches the question because Rutherford identified and used alpha particles in his scattering experiment. Distractors B (J. J. Thomson), C (Einstein), D (Marie Curie) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25030, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the correct statement about the perfect structure of an atom? (a) Atoms contain a massive nucleus. (b) Atoms have empty space around the nucleus where electrons revolve. (c) Nucleus contains only protons (d) Atom has same numbers of protons and neutron, combinedly they called as ''nucleons''. Answer Options:', '{"A": "a, b, c, d", "B": "a, b, c", "C": "a, b", "D": "a, b, d"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is the right choice since atoms have a massive nucleus (a) and empty space with orbiting electrons (b); nucleus has protons and neutrons, not protons alone (c, d wrong). You can eliminate A (a, b, c, d), B (a, b, c), D (a, b, d) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25031, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What are the features of the hydrogen element ? (a) Hydrogen atom''s nucleus has a neutron. (b) Hydrogen atom''s have no neutrons. (c) Hydrogen nuclei don''t have any protons. (d) Hydrogen atoms have 2 electrons revolving around the nucleus. Answer Options:', '{"A": "Only a", "B": "Only b", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "(b) and d"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — Ordinary hydrogen has one proton and no neutron in the nucleus. Distractors A (Only a), C ((a) and c), D ((b) and d) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25032, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Atoms of molecules are combined by which forces ?', '{"A": "Intermolecular forces", "B": "Intramolecular forces", "C": "Dipole-dipole forces", "D": "Van der waals forces"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25033, 'Acid, Base and Salt', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Why is chlorine mixed with water ?', '{"A": "It increases oxygen level in water.", "B": "It kills microbes in water.", "C": "It removes dirt.", "D": "It removes insoluble impurities."}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is correct because Chlorine in water kills microbes by disinfection. You can eliminate A (It increases oxygen level in water.), C (It removes dirt.), D (It removes insoluble impurities.) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Acid, Base and Salt"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25034, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What are the elements called with different atomic numbers and atomic mass numbers but have the same number of neutrons ? (a) Isotones (b) Isotopes (c) Isobars (d) Isomer Answer Options:', '{"A": "a", "B": "b", "C": "(a) and d", "D": "(b) and c"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since Isotones have the same number of neutrons but different Z and A. Distractors B (b), C ((a) and d), D ((b) and c) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25035, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the following statements. (a) Goldstein has discovered the protons. (b) Thomson has discovered the neutrons. (c) Neutrons have a negative charge.', '{"A": "(a) and c correct", "B": "(a) and b correct", "C": "(b) and c correct", "D": "Only a correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because only (a) is correct: Goldstein discovered protons; Chadwick found neutrons; neutrons are neutral. You can eliminate A ((a) and c correct), B ((a) and b correct), C ((b) and c correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25036, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is an atomic number?', '{"A": "Numbers of protons.", "B": "Numbers of neutrons.", "C": "Numbers of protons + numbers of neutrons.", "D": "Numbers of protons + electrons + neutrons."}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25037, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statements. (a) The total accumulated mass inside an atom is known as atomic mass. (b) As the mass of a neutron is negligible, so while calculating atomic mass only the mass of protons and electrons is considered. (c) Atomic mass is measured in the Dalton [u] unit. Answer Options:', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "(a) and b", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "Only a"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The answer is option C: atomic mass is total nuclear mass (a) measured in dalton/u (c); neutrons contribute significantly — (b) wrong. Distractors A (a, b, c), B ((a) and b), D (Only a) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25038, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What are the isotopes of elements ? (a) Same atomic number (b) Different mass number (c) Different atomic number (d) Same mass number Answer Options:', '{"A": "(a) and d", "B": "(c) and d", "C": "(a) and b", "D": "(c) and b"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose option C: Isotopes have the same atomic number but different mass number. You can eliminate A ((a) and d), B ((c) and d), D ((c) and b) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25039, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'How many isotopes of oxygen are available in nature ?', '{"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "4", "D": "1"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B works here because Three natural isotopes of oxygen exist: ¹⁶O, ¹⁷O, ¹⁸O. You can eliminate A (2), C (4), D (1) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25040, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statements from below. (a) The combining power of an element is called valency. (b) By combining means, elements try to achieve stability. (c) Having 8 electrons in the last orbit means the octet state, also called stability state. (d) To maintain the octet, the number of electrons given or received is called valency of the atom. Answer Options:', '{"A": "a, b, c, d", "B": "a, b, c", "C": "b, c, d", "D": "a, c, d"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct: a, b, c, d. This follows from the stem — Identify the correct statements from below. (a) The combining power of an element is called valency. (b) By combining means, elements try to achieve stability. Wrong options B (a, b, c), C (b, c, d), D (a, c, d) do not match the definition or symbol tested here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25041, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) Sodium has 11 electrons means valency of sodium is one. (b) Chlorine has 17 electrons means valency of chlorine is one. Answer Options:', '{"A": "Only a correct", "B": "Only b correct", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because Sodium (11 e⁻, valency 1) and chlorine (17 e⁻, valency 1) — both statements correct per key. You can eliminate A (Only a correct), B (Only b correct), D ((a) and b incorrect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25042, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) Atomic mass of element is denoted above the symbol. Eg. 23Na (b) Atomic number of elements is denoted below the symbol. Eg. 11Na (c) Atomic mass is denoted by the letter ''Z''. (d) Atomic number is denoted by letter ''A''. Answer Options:', '{"A": "a, b, c, d are all correct", "B": "(a) and b correct", "C": "(c) and d correct", "D": "All are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is correct because Mass number is written above and atomic number below the symbol (a, b); A is mass number and Z is atomic number — (c)(d) swap letters. Distractors A (a, b, c, d are all correct), C ((c) and d correct), D (All are incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25043, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following compounds or compounds have extended the octate in the valence shell of the atom ? (a) BrF3 (b) BrF5 Answer Options:', '{"A": "Only a", "B": "Only b", "C": "(a) and b", "D": "None"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25044, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) The closest orbit to the nucleus has 4 electrons. (b) These orbits are called K, L, M, N ............ orbits respectively. (c) Specific number of electrons are present in a particular orbit. It is denoted by the 2n2 formula. Answer Options:', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "(a) and b", "C": "(b) and c", "D": "Only b"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The official Set A key selects Only b. The Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. Add this correct option to your active-recall flashcard deck; MPSC repeats the underlying fact in some form every two to three prelim cycles.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25045, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the molecular formula of magnesium chloride if valency of magnesium is 2 and chlorine is 1 ?', '{"A": "Mg2Cl2", "B": "MgCl", "C": "MgCl2", "D": "MgCl0"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose option C: Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻ give MgCl₂. Distractors A (Mg2Cl2), B (MgCl), D (MgCl0) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25046, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statements. (a) HF and H2O are polar compounds. (b) CH4 and CO2 are not polar compounds. (c) CO2 and SO2 are not polar compounds. Options -', '{"A": "(a) and b", "B": "(b) and c", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "a, b, c"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A works here because HF and H₂O are polar; CH₄ and CO₂ are non-polar; SO₂ is polar so (c) is wrong. Distractors B ((b) and c), C ((a) and c), D (a, b, c) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25047, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a) When a living thing dies, the amount of carbon 12 in its body starts decreasing slowly but the amount of carbon 14 remains the same. (b) When a living thing dies, the amount of carbon 14 in its body starts decreasing slowly but the amount of carbon 12 remains the same. (c) Age of dead organisms is calculated by comparing the proportion of C14 and C12. Answer Options:', '{"A": "(a) and c", "B": "(b) and c", "C": "Only a", "D": "Only c"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — after death C-14 decays while C-12 stays constant (b); age is found from their ratio (c). You can eliminate A ((a) and c), C (Only a), D (Only c) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25048, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Find the percentage of carbon in a molecule of glucose if the molecular weight of a glucose molecule is 180 units ? Answer Options:', '{"A": "40 %", "B": "45 %", "C": "50 %", "D": "55 %"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25049, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct statements. (a) The table form by arranging the elements according to their properties is called periodic table. (b) Doberianer''s triad and Newland''s octaves depend upon atomic mass. (c) Mendeleev''s periodic table depends upon atomic number. Options -', '{"A": "a, b, c", "B": "(a) and b", "C": "(a) and c", "D": "Only a"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The answer is option B: Periodic table arranges by properties (a); Dobereiner and Newlands used atomic mass (b); Mendeleev also used mass, not number (c wrong). You can eliminate A (a, b, c), C ((a) and c), D (Only a) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25050, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) In 1913, the British chemist Mendeleev found that atomic mass is the f undamental property of an element, not the atomic number. (b) Mendeleev''s periodic table is called the modern periodic table. Answer Options:', '{"A": "Only a", "B": "Only b", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect V"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The right answer is (a) and b incorrect V (option D). The year 1913 anchors this question — the correct option is the fact that dates to that year. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. The three wrong options are the standard distractor set MPSC uses for this topic — one is a chronological neighbour, one is a thematic near-miss and one is a plausible-sounding invention.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium')
INSERT INTO pyq_questions (id, topic, subject, exam, year, question, options, correct, explanation, tags, difficulty)
VALUES
  (25051, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about periodic tables. (a) As we go down along the groups, the number of orbits increases and so does the size of the atom. (b) As we go from left to right along the period, the size of the atom decreases. Answer Options:', '{"A": "Only a", "B": "Only b", "C": "(a) and b correct", "D": "(a) and b incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The correct answer is option C — atomic size increases down a group (a) and decreases across a period left to right (b). Distractors A (Only a), B (Only b), D ((a) and b incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25052, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the pairs of Mendeleev''s predicted elements and actual elements. (a) Eka Boron | i) Gallium (b) Eka Aluminium | ii) Germanium (c) Eka Silicon | iii) Scandium', '{"A": "i ii iii", "B": "ii iii i", "C": "i iii ii", "D": "iii i ii"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D matches the question because Eka-boron→scandium (iii), eka-aluminium→gallium (i), eka-silicon→germanium (ii). Distractors A (i ii iii), B (ii iii i), C (i iii ii) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25053, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Modern periodic table depends upon ............. (a) Properties (b) Existence of elements triad (c) Atomic mass (d) none of the above Answer Options:', '{"A": "a", "B": "b", "C": "c", "D": "d"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because Modern periodic law is based on atomic number — none of (a)(b)(c) alone is the answer per key. You can eliminate A (a), B (b), C (c) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25054, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If Doberianer''s triad has Ca and Ba , what is the third element ?', '{"A": "Be", "B": "Mg", "C": "Sr", "D": "I"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25055, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'The one mole of substance means ?', '{"A": "6.022×1022", "B": "6.022×1023", "C": "6.022×1024", "D": "6.022×1021 Question Question 1. 2 16. 1 2. 3 17. 3 3. 1 18. 3 4. 3 19. 3 5. 3 20. 2 6. 2 21. 1 7. 1 22. 1 8. 4 23. 2 9. 3 24. 4 10. 2 25. 3 11. 3 26. 4 12. 1 27. 3 13. 4 28. 3 14. 2 29. 4 15. 2 30. 2"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The answer is option B: One mole = 6.022 × 10²³ particles (Avogadro''s number). Distractors A (6.022×1022), C (6.022×1024), D (6.022×1021 Question Question 1. 2 16. 1…) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25056, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Swelling of raisins after keeping them in water is........... action and reduction in size of grapes in sugar water is........... action.', '{"A": "Exosmosis, Endosmosis", "B": "Endosmosis, Exosmosis", "C": "Endosmosis, Reverse Osmosis", "D": "None of these."}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B matches the question because Pure water has higher water potential than the raisin cell sap, so water enters the grape tissue by endosmosis and the fruit swells. Concentrated sugar solution lowers external water potential; water leaves the grape by exosmosis and it shrinks. Swapping the terms (A) or inserting reverse osmosis (C) confuses direction of solvent flow with active pressure-driven filtration. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25057, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements about Diffusion and choose the option correct is not incorrect. (a) Inhibition is the attraction of water by a substance having water attraction. (b) Diffusion is the movement of atoms from a more concentrated solution to a less concentrated solution. (c) No energy is expended in diffusion.', '{"A": "Only (a) and (b)", "B": "Only (b) and (c)", "C": "Only (a) and (c)", "D": "All of the above are correct."}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: Read ''not incorrect'' as ''correct''. (a) uses the garbled label ''Inhibition'' for imbibition—the passive uptake of water by dry colloids such as seeds and wood; that definition is accepted in this paper set. (b) states diffusion moves particles from higher to lower concentration along the gradient. (c) marks diffusion as passive—no metabolic ATP spend. All three limbs survive together, so rejecting any single pair overlooks the double-negative framing. You can eliminate A (Only (a) and (b)), B (Only (b) and (c)), C (Only (a) and (c)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25058, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements and choose the correct statement option. (a) Like animals, plants have respiration, nutrition, excretion but lack of coordinated action. (b) Human has circulatory system while plant has transport system. (c) Plants require more energy than animals. Choose the correct statement/s.', '{"A": "(b) only", "B": "(a) and (b) only", "C": "(a) and (c) Only", "D": "All of the above correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Statement (a) overreaches: plants do carry out respiration, nutrition, and excretion, but they also show coordinated responses through hormones, tropisms, and turgor-driven movements—so ''lack of coordinated action'' is syllabus-inaccurate. Statement (c) reverses the energy picture: animals are far more metabolically active per unit mass than sessile plants. Statement (b) cleanly contrasts human circulatory flow with the plant vascular (xylem–phloem) transport system—the only limb that stands alone.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25059, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Macronutrients | i) Carbon, Hydrogen (b) Critical elements | ii) Sulphur, magnesium (c) Micronutrients | iii) Nitrogen, Potassium (d) Framework elements | iv) Iron, magnesium.', '{"A": "(ii) (iii) (i) (iv)", "B": "(i) (iv) (ii) (iii)", "C": "(ii) (iii) (iv) (i)", "D": "(iv) (ii) (iii) (iv)"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Lock one anchor: framework elements are C, H, O—the structural skeleton of organic matter (i). Macronutrients needed in larger amounts include N and K (iii); S and Mg also sit in the macro set (ii). Micronutrients such as Fe appear in trace doses (iv)—note Mg appears in both macro and micro lists in some texts, but the correct grid is a→ii, b→iii, c→iv, d→i. Any option placing C,H under micronutrients or Fe under framework is incorrect the pairing logic.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25060, 'Atom and its Structure', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about periodic tables. (a) Elements in the same period can have different orbits. (b) As we go from left to right in periods, metallic properties decrease and non-metallic properties increase. (c) As we go down along the group, metallic properties increase and non- metallic properties decrease. Answer Options:', '{"A": "a, b, c correct", "B": "(a) and b correct", "C": "(b) and c correct", "D": "(a) and c correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The definitive answer is (b) and c correct. The Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. The MPSC pattern for questions of this type is to plant one exact fact and three plausible-looking alternatives; the fact-based option is always the answer.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","Atom and its Structure"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25061, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'The steps of light reactions are given in the following options. Put those alternatives in the correct order in the light reaction. (a) Absorption of light (b) Dissociation of oxygen from water. (c) Formation of high energy intermediates (d) Dissociation of water molecule.', '{"A": "a-b-c-d", "B": "d-b-a-c", "C": "a-d-b-c", "D": "a-b-d-c"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Light reactions begin with pigment absorption of photons (a), then photolysis splits water (d), releasing O₂ (b), while high-energy intermediates (ATP, NADPH) form (c). Sequences that photolyse before absorption or place intermediate formation before water split break the Z-scheme chronology memorised for MPSC botany.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25062, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Arnon''s and Stout (1939) criteria for essential nutrients. (a) Deficiency of essential nutrient elements makes it impossible for plant to complete the vegetative or reproductive stage. (b) Deficiency can be corrected only by supplying that particular element. (c) Deficiency can be corrected by supplying other similar element. (d) Must have direct influence on plant nutrition and metabolism.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) and (d) are correct, (c) is incorrect", "B": "Only (a), (b) and (c) are correct, (d) is incorrect", "C": "Only (a), (c) and (d) are correct, (b) is incorrect", "D": "All of the above is correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since arnon and Stout (1939) require that deficiency blocks normal life-cycle completion (a), that only the missing element cures the disorder—not a substitute (b), and that the element act directly in nutrition/metabolism (d). Statement (c) violates the specificity rule: similar elements cannot replace a true essential. Partial triplets that retain (c) break the classical criteria list. You can eliminate B (Only (a), (b) and (c) are correct, (d) …), C (Only (a), (c) and (d) are correct, (b) …), D (All of the above is correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25063, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Plants use......... method for short distance transport of the element they need and......... method for long distance transport.', '{"A": "Translocation, diffusion", "B": "Diffusion, Translocation", "C": "Translocation, osmosis", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The answer is option B: Short-distance movement across cell membranes and cell walls uses passive/active diffusion and osmosis at the cellular scale. Long-distance bulk transport of water, minerals, and assimilates runs through xylem and phloem—translocation. Reversing the pair (A) or substituting osmosis for vascular translocation (C) collapses two distinct spatial scales. Distractors A (Translocation, diffusion), C (Translocation, osmosis), D (None of these) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25064, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements and choose the correct option. (a) Plants release water in the form of water vapor through the minute pores on their leaves, this is called transpiration. (b) Water transport in the plant due to transpiration and root pressure. (c) Phenyl mercuric acetate (PMA) and abasic acid (AB A) are artificial anti- evaporation agent. (d) Silicone emulsion, colorless plastic resins are natural anti-evaporation agents.', '{"A": "(a), (b), (c) correct, (d) incorrect", "B": "(b), (c), (d) correct, (a) ncorrect", "C": "(a) and (b) correct, (c) and (d) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct."}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Transpiration is foliar water-vapour loss through stomata (a), and the transpiration pull couples with root pressure in ascent of sap (b)—both stand. PMA and ABA are cited here as anti-transpirants (c). Silicone emulsions and colourless plastic resins are synthetic/antitranspirant coatings, not ''natural'' agents (d)—that swap is the deliberate trap.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25065, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements and choose the option which is not correct. (a) Flower is the functional unit of sexual reproduction in plants. (b) Carpel and stamen are sexual organs between female plant and male plant. (c) Meosis and fertilization are the main methods of sexual reproduction in plants.', '{"A": "Only (b), (c) is incorrect", "B": "Only (a) is incorrect", "C": "Only (a), (c) Incorrect", "D": "None of these."}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D works here because the question asks which option is not correct—i.e., all statements are correct per key D. (a) Flower is the angiosperm reproductive organ. (b) Carpel (pistil) is female; stamen is male—both are floral sexual whorls. (c) Meiosis in sporogenous tissue plus double fertilisation defines sexual reproduction. Options marking any limb incorrect contradict the correct ''none wrong'' stand. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25066, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Hygroscopic water | i) In the form of oxides (b) Combined water | ii) In small tubes / cavities (c) Gravitational water | iii) Firmly bound to soil particles (d) Capillary water | iv) Sinks deeper due to gravity', '{"A": "(i) (iii) (iv) (ii)", "B": "(ii) (i) (iv) (iii)", "C": "(i) (iii) (ii) (iv)", "D": "(iii) (i) (iv) (ii)"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is the right choice since Hygroscopic water is held so tightly on soil particle surfaces that roots cannot extract it (iii). Combined water is locked in soil minerals as oxides/hydroxides (i). Gravitational water drains downward beyond field capacity (iv). Capillary water occupies pore spaces and is the plant-available pool (ii). The d→iii, a→i, b→iv, c→ii grid is internally consistent; other permutations mis-assign availability. Distractors A ((i) (iii) (iv) (ii)), B ((ii) (i) (iv) (iii)), C ((i) (iii) (ii) (iv)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25067, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements regarding the use of Auxin. (a) Root and stem growth is promoted by auxin. (b) Auxins help in the formation of vegetative reproduction. (c) A artificial auxin called 2-4D is mainly used to control dicot weeds in mono- legume fields. (d) Auxin reduces the rate of photosynthesis by promoting senescence.', '{"A": "All of the above are correct", "B": "(b), (c), (d) correct, (a) Incorrect", "C": "(a), (b), (c) correct, (d) incorrect", "D": "None of these."}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is correct because auxin at low concentration promotes root initiation and stem elongation (a), triggers adventitious/rooting in vegetative propagation (b), and synthetic 2,4-D serves as a selective dicot herbicide in cereal fields (c). Claiming auxin reduces photosynthesis via senescence (d) belongs to ABA/ethylene physiology, not auxin''s primary syllabus role—so (d) is the outlier. Distractors A (All of the above are correct), B ((b), (c), (d) correct, (a) Incorrect), D (None of these.) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25068, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements regarding glycolysis. (a) Glycolysis is a series of biochemical reactions. (b) Glycolysis is a reaction that takes place in the nucleus and requires oxygen (c) Glycolysis is the only activity of respiration in aerobic respirating animals. (d) In this process six carbon glucose is converted to three carbon pyruvate.', '{"A": "(a), (d) only", "B": "(b), (c) only", "C": "(a), (b), (c) only", "D": "(a) only"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because Glycolysis is a cytosolic biochemical pathway (a) splitting one six-carbon glucose into two three-carbon pyruvate molecules (d). It is not nuclear (b), not oxygen-dependent at the glycolytic step itself, and not the sole respiration stage even in aerobes—Krebs cycle and ETS follow. You can eliminate B ((b), (c) only), C ((a), (b), (c) only), D ((a) only) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25069, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements and choose the option which is not incorrect. (a) The movement of solute from a solution of lower concentration to a solution of higher concentration through a membrane is called osmosis. (b) osmosis was termed by Abi Nollate. (c) No energy is expended in osmosis.', '{"A": "(a), (b) correct (c) incorrect", "B": "(b), (c) correct (a) incorrect", "C": "(a), (c) correct (b) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Double-negative again: pick the assessment that is not wrong. The paper treats (a) and (b) as correct and (c) as incorrect—key A. Coach note: exam keys sometimes garble osmosis wording; here solvent movement and Nollet''s historical naming are accepted as stated, while disputing (c) matches the correct split. Options elevating (c) over (a) or (b) miss the paper''s chosen partition.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25070, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Parasitic plants. | i) Pitcher plant (b) Saprotrophic plants | ii) cuscuta, Reflesia (c) Insectivorous | iii) Lichen (d) Symbiotic plants | iv) Penicillium', '{"A": "(i) (ii) (iv) (iii)", "B": "(ii) (i) (iv) (iii)", "C": "(i) (iii) (ii) (iv)", "D": "(ii) (iv) (i) (ii)"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D matches the question because Parasitic dodder (Cuscuta) and Rafflesia live on host plants (ii). Insectivorous pitcher plant traps insects (i)—not saprotroph Penicillium (iv). Saprotrophic decomposers include moulds such as Penicillium (iv). Lichen is fungus–alga symbiosis (iii). Key D: a→ii, b→iv, c→i, d→iii; options placing Cuscuta with pitcher or Penicillium with lichen break nutrition-mode logic. You can eliminate A ((i) (ii) (iv) (iii)), B ((ii) (i) (iv) (iii)), C ((i) (iii) (ii) (iv)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25071, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements regarding Diffusion and choose the incorrect statement option. (a) Diffusion pressure is the ability of a liquid, gas or solid to move from an environment of higher concentration to an environment of lower concentration. (b) Diffusion continues even after equilibrium has been reached. (c) Diffusion pressure of pure water is lowest.', '{"A": "(a), (b) only", "B": "(b), (c) only", "C": "(a), (c) Only", "D": "None of the above."}'::jsonb, 'B', 'You want the incorrect statements. (a) correctly defines diffusion down a concentration gradient—keep it. (b) is incorrect: at equilibrium net diffusion stops even if micro-scale exchange continues—''continues after equilibrium'' is false. (c) is incorrect: pure water has the highest diffusion/osmotic potential, not the lowest. Option B isolates exactly those two wrong limbs.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25072, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following substances is affected by mosaic virus? (a) Tobacco (b) Potato (c) Cucumber (d) Rice (e) Cowpea f) Alpha-alpha', '{"A": "Only (a), (b), (d), (c)", "B": "Only (a), (c), (e), (f)", "C": "Only (a), (b), (d), (e), (f)", "D": "All of the above"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The answer is option B: Tobacco mosaic virus (TMV) classically infects tobacco (a), cucumber (c), cowpea (e), and alfalfa/lucerne (f)—the correct B set. Potato and rice appear in other virus stories (PVY, RTV) but not in this paper''s TMV host list. Over-inclusive', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25073, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Statement- Absxisic acid is called antigibberelins hormone. Cause- Absxisic acid inhibits gibberellin-activated growth.', '{"A": "(a) and (b) are correct and (b) is the cause of (a).", "B": "(a) and (b) are correct and (b) is not a reason for (a).", "C": "(a) correct (b) incorrect", "D": "Both are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ABA antagonises GA-promoted growth—stem elongation, enzyme mobilisation in germination—earning the label ''anti-gibberellin'' (a). Statement (b) gives the mechanism: ABA inhibition of gibberellin-activated pathways. Cause–effect pairing is valid; denying the link (B/C) or rejecting both (D) misses textbook hormone opposition.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25074, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) Photoautotrophs use light as energy to produce living food. (b) Chemotrophs produce food using energy produced by living chemical reactions. (c) Scientists Ruben and Kamen proved that the oxygen released in photosynthesis is obtained from water.', '{"A": "(a), (b) correct, (c) incorrect", "B": "(b), (c) correct, (a) incorrect", "C": "(a), (c) correct, (b) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is All of the above are correct. Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose; the overall equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, driven by chlorophyll in chloroplasts. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. This is a classic MPSC ''anchor-word'' item — the stem contains one specific term (a proper noun, a date, an article number) that maps directly to only one option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25075, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the correctness of the statements about growth related movements in plants and select the correct statement option. (a) In balsam and castor plants, the fruit bursts at the right time and the seeds spread everywhere. (b) Pitcher plant, venus fly trap These plants are completely dependent on other plants for food. (c) Cuscuta and Bandagul meet their nitrogen requirement by feeding on insects. Choose the correct option.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b)", "B": "Only (a), (b), (c)", "C": "Only (b), (c)", "D": "Only (a)"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Choose option D: Explosive dehiscence in balsam (Impatiens) and castor scatters seeds mechanically (a)—true. Pitcher plant and Venus flytrap are insectivorous autotrophs supplementing nitrogen; they are not wholly dependent on other plants for food (b). Cuscuta is parasitic, not insectivorous; ''Bandagul'' here points to bladderwort/Utricularia-type traps, not nitrogen from insects in Cuscuta (c). Only (a) survives. Distractors A (Only (a), (b)), B (Only (a), (b), (c)), C (Only (b), (c)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25076, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the elements and their deficiency diseases. (a) Nitrogen | i) Leaf spotting (b) Sulfur | ii) Production of chlorophyll (c) Potassium | iii) Late flowering (d) Iron | iv) Activates nitrogen metabolism enzymes (e) Molybdenum. v) Participation in photosynthesis and respiration', '{"A": "(a) - (i), (b) - (ii), (c) - (iii), (d) - (iv), (e) - (v)", "B": "(a) - (iii), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (v), (e) - (iv)", "C": "(a) - (iii), (b) - (i), (c) - (v), (d) - (ii), (e) - (iv)", "D": "(a) - (v), (b) - (iv), (c) - (iii), (d) - (ii), (e) - (i)"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The answer is option C: N deficiency → stunted growth and delayed/late flowering (iii). S deficiency → leaf chlorosis/spots (i). K participates in stomatal opening, enzyme activation, and photosynthesis-linked processes (v). Fe is essential for chlorophyll synthesis and enzyme systems (ii). Mo activates nitrate reductase and nitrogen metabolism (iv). Key C: a-iii, b-i, c-v, d-ii, e-iv. You can eliminate A ((a) - (i), (b) - (ii), (c) - (iii), (d)…), B ((a) - (iii), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d)…), D ((a) - (v), (b) - (iv), (c) - (iii), (d)…) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25077, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the correctness of the following statements and select the correct statement/s regarding vessels in plants.. (a) Transport of water and minerals in root plants takes place through Phloem. (b) Water and minerals flow only in unidirectional. (c) Transport of organic compounds and other fluids takes place in many directions.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "None of these."}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Xylem—not phloem—carries water and dissolved minerals upward from roots; statement (a) assigns the wrong tissue. Mineral mass flow in xylem is essentially unidirectional (roots → shoot), while phloem translocation of organic solutes is multidirectional (source to sink). Options keeping (a) as correct mis-map the two vascular highways.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25078, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements and choose the option of the correct statement. (a) The process of transporting food to each part of a plant is called translocation. (b) Translocation is unidirectional. (c) ATP is required for translocation as energy is expended. (d) Excess food except amino acids is stored in roots, fruits, seeds.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b), (c) correct (d) incorrect", "B": "Only (a), (c), (d) correct (b) incorrect", "C": "Only (a), (b), (d) correct (c) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B matches the question because Translocation is phloem transport of assimilates to sinks (a). It is multidirectional—root storage, fruit filling, spring remobilisation—not strictly one-way (b is wrong). Active loading/unloading spends ATP (c). Excess carbohydrate is stored as starch in roots, fruits, and seeds—not ''except amino acids'' phrasing aside, storage organs hold non-protein reserves (d). B keeps a,c,d and drops unidirectional (b). Distractors A (Only (a), (b), (c) correct (d) incorrect), C (Only (a), (b), (d) correct (c) incorrect), D (All of the above are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25079, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements regarding fertilization. (a) Each pollen tube contains two male gametes. (b) A male gamet unites with an ovum to form a zygote. (c) The endosperm is formed by the fusion of the second male gamete with the secondary nucleus. (d) The zygote develops on the stigma. Choose the correct statement/s', '{"A": "All of the above are correct", "B": "(b), (c), (d) correct, (a) incorrect", "C": "(a), (c), (d) correct, (b) incorrect", "D": "(a), (b), (c) correct, (d) incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is option D — Pollen tubes carry two male gametes (a). One fuses with the egg → zygote (b). The second fuses with polar nuclei → triploid endosperm (c). Zygote develops inside the ovule/embryo sac, not on the stigma (d)—stigma is only the landing pad. All except (d) are standard double-fertilisation facts. You can eliminate A (All of the above are correct), B ((b), (c), (d) correct, (a) incorrect), C ((a), (c), (d) correct, (b) incorrect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25080, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements regarding photosynthesis and select the correct statement option. (a) Photosynthesis requires respiration of energy in the form of light rays. (b) Synthesis is highest under red light. (c) Photosynthesis does not occur in yellow and infrared.', '{"A": "(a), (b) only", "B": "(b), (c) only", "C": "(a), (c) only", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Photosynthesis is a light-driven endergonic process (a). Red wavelengths (~660 nm) peak in chlorophyll absorption and show high quantum yield (b). Yellow and far-red/infrared are poorly absorbed by chlorophyll—minimal photosynthesis (c). All three limbs align with action-spectrum facts; splitting them mis-reads the spectrum chart.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25081, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Roots use the ........... water in the soil.', '{"A": "Hygroscopic water", "B": "Gravitational water", "C": "Combined water", "D": "Capillary water"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Roots absorb capillary water held in soil pore spaces at field capacity—the intermediate pool between hygroscopic (too tight) and gravitational (drains away). Hygroscopic and combined water are unavailable; gravitational water moves too fast for sustained uptake.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25082, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) No energy is expended in diffusion. (b) Energy is expended in plant osmosis. (c) No energy is expended in osmosis processes in the cells of the human body.', '{"A": "All of the above are correct", "B": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "C": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "D": "Only (a), (c) are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Diffusion is passive—no ATP (a). Active transport energises many plant osmotic adjustments at membranes, so ''energy expended in plant osmosis'' (b) is accepted in this item. Human cell osmosis across erythrocyte membranes is likewise passive at the basic level (c). Denying (b) or (c) splits what the paper bundles as all correct.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25083, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements regarding reproduction in plants and choose the option which is not incorrect. (a) Plants reproduce both sexually and asexually. (b) Root, stem, leaf, bud are involved in vegetative reproduction in plants. (c) Plants undergo meosis like animals.', '{"A": "(a), (b) only", "B": "(b), (c) only", "C": "(a), (c) only", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is option D — Plants reproduce sexually (seed/flower) and asexually (vegetative organs) (a). Roots (sweet potato), stems (potato, ginger), leaves (Bryophyllum), buds all propagate vegetatively (b). Meiosis precedes spore/pollen/egg formation in the sexual cycle—parallel to animals (c). All three stand; denying meiosis in plants (common trap) is incorrect angiosperm life-cycle basics. Distractors A ((a), (b) only), B ((b), (c) only), C ((a), (c) only) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25084, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements regarding cytokinin. (a) School and Miller discovered cytokinin while they were culturing tobacco plant tissue. (b) Cytokinin is derived from the purine thiamine. (c) Cytokinin is found in plants where cell division takes place.', '{"A": "(a), (c) correct, (b) incorrect", "B": "(b), (c) correct, (a) incorrect", "C": "(a), (b) correct, (c) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Skoog and Miller discovered cytokinin from autoclaved herring sperm/coconut milk during tobacco tissue culture (a). Cytokinin is an adenine/purine derivative—not thiamine (b is wrong). It concentrates in meristems, young fruits, and dividing tissues (c). Key A: accept (a,c), reject mis-named precursor (b).', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25085, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements regarding Kreb’s series and select the correct statement option. (a) Sir Hans Kreb was awarded the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1953 for his discovery of Kreb’s series. (b) Kreb’s cycle is also known as TCA cycle. (c) Transport of H+ ions across the cell membrane is essential for the formation of ATP.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) correct, (c) incorrect", "B": "Only (b), (c) correct, (a) incorrect", "C": "Only (a), (c) correct, (b) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: Hans Krebs elucidated the citric-acid cycle—TCA/Krebs cycle—in mitochondrial matrix (b); Nobel recognition for metabolism work is syllabus-linked to him (a). Proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane drives chemiosmotic ATP synthesis (c)—also true in ETS context. All three are accepted here; cherry-picking only two drops valid bioenergetics. Distractors A (Only (a), (b) correct, (c) incorrect), B (Only (b), (c) correct, (a) incorrect), C (Only (a), (c) correct, (b) incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25086, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements and choose the option which is not correct. (a) Photochemical phase takes place in the thalloid in chlorophyll. (b) Energy is basically converted in a light reaction. (c) Dark reaction takes place in stroma in chlorophyll. (d) Proteins are produced in dark reaction reactions.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b), (c) Correct, (d) incorrect", "B": "Only (b), (c), (d) Correct, (a) incorrect", "C": "Only (a), (c), (d) Correct, (b) incorrect", "D": "Only (a), (b), (d) Correct, (c) incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Photochemical phase occurs in thylakoid/granal membranes (a). Light reactions convert radiant energy to chemical carriers (b). Calvin cycle runs in stroma (c). Dark reactions fix CO₂ into sugars—not protein manufacture (d); proteins need ribosomal translation. Key A: a,b,c stand; (d) is the incorrect limb about protein synthesis in Calvin cycle.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25087, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Hosts on branches | i) Orbanche, Reflecia (b) Hosts on roots (ii) Santalum album (c) Incomplete host on branches (iii) Amarvel (d) Incomplete fertilization on roots. (iv) Mistletoe', '{"A": "(iii) (i) (iv) (ii)", "B": "(iii) (iv) (i) (ii)", "C": "(iii) (i) (ii) (iv)", "D": "(iv) (iii) (ii) (ii)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because amarvel (Cuscuta) twines on branches (iii). Root parasites include Orobanche and Rafflesia (i). Mistletoe (Viscum) is a partial stem parasite on branches (iv). Sandalwood (Santalum) is a root hemiparasite (ii). Key A: a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii. Options swapping sandalwood to branch host or Amarvel to root is wrong parasitism ecology. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25088, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements and select the correct statement option. (a) Wheat, rice, barley are examples of C4 plant. (b) Maize, sugarcane, sorghum are examples of C3 plants. (d) Pineapple, onion, garlic are examples of CAM plants.', '{"A": "All are incorrect except (a)", "B": "All are incorrect except (b)", "C": "All are incorrect except (c)", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose option C: Wheat, rice, barley are classic C3 plants—statement (a) is false. Maize, sugarcane, sorghum are C4—(b) is false. Pineapple, onion, garlic use CAM (crassulacean acid metabolism)—(d) is the sole correct line. Option C (''all incorrect except c'') matches; note the paper labels CAM as statement (d) even though (c) is missing from the stem text. You can eliminate A (All are incorrect except (a)), B (All are incorrect except (b)), D (All of the above are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25089, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Radical | i) Hair like threads at root tip (b) plumule | ii) Part growing towards the ground (c) Tap-root | iii) Sub-roots to roots (d) Root hair | iv) Above ground growing part', '{"A": "(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)", "B": "(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)", "C": "(a)- (i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)", "D": "(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is the right choice since Radicle is the embryonic root that grows downward (ii). Plumule is the shoot apex rising above ground (iv). Tap-root system forms the primary root with branches (iii). Root hairs are epidermal extensions for absorption (i). Key D: a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i. Options pairing plumule with downward growth or root hair with aerial parts invert seedling geography. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25090, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statements from the following- (a) The force of attraction between two similar molecules is called adhesive force. (b) The force between different types of molecules is called cohesive force. (c) Adhesive force exists between cell wall and water molecules.', '{"A": "Only (c)", "B": "Only (a), (b)", "C": "Only (a), (c)", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A works here because Cohesive force binds like molecules (water–water); adhesive force binds unlike molecules (water–cell wall). Statements (a) and (b) swap the definitions. (c) correctly notes adhesion between cellulose cell walls and water menisci in capillary ascent—the only accurate limb alone. You can eliminate B (Only (a), (b)), C (Only (a), (c)), D (All of the above are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25091, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements regarding the process of water transport in plants and choose the incorrect statement. (a) Root pressure is preferably operative during the day. (b) Transpiration in plants takes place only at night. (c) 98% of the total water absorbed is released through transpiration.', '{"A": "Only (a), (c)", "B": "Only (b), (c)", "C": "Only (a), (b)", "D": "None of these."}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Root pressure shows best at night when transpiration pull is low—not ''preferably during the day'' (a is false). Transpiration peaks in daylight through open stomata—not ''only at night'' (b is false). About 98% of absorbed water is lost via transpiration (c is true). The item wants the pair of incorrect statements: (a) and (b) only—option C.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25092, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct pair option from the following pairs. (a) Iron-Kewda disease (b) Zinc-Cauliflower whiptail (c) Molybdenum - Khairya disease. (d) Copper-exanthema', '{"A": "(a) only", "B": "(a), (b) only", "C": "(a), (d) only", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Iron deficiency in betel/kewda (Piper) causes ''Kewda disease'' (a). Zinc whiptail hits cauliflower (b). Molybdenum ''Khaira'' is a rice disorder (c)—not paired here. Copper ''exanthema'' is a citrus copper-toxicity/deficiency name (d). Key C accepts (a) and (d) only; options adding (b) or (c) import wrong crop–element duos for this paper.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25093, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements about plant breeding and choose the correct statement option. (a) Beet, Rose plants have vegetative reproduction in the stem. (b) In sweet potato, carrot, radish there is vegetative reproduction from stem. (c) Vegetative reproduction occurs from leaves in Bryophyta, Audiantum.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) correct, (c) incorrect", "B": "Only (a), (c) correct (b) incorrect", "C": "Only (b), (c) Correct, (a) incorrect", "D": "All of these are correct"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Beet and rose propagate by stem tubers/runners/cuttings (a). Sweet potato, carrot, radish are root modifications—vegetative reproduction from root, not stem (b is wrong). Bryophyllum (Kalanchoe) and Adiantum (fern) reproduce via leaf or spore-bearing fronds/leaves (c). Key B: a and c stand; stem claim for root crops (b) is the trap.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25094, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about auxin. (a) Auxin is the first hormone to be discovered and named as Auxin by F.W. Went. (b) Auxin is produced by the plant itself. (c) Auxin is found in the growing parts of plants. (d) It was F. W. Went who discovered Auxin from the plant oat.', '{"A": "(a), (b), (c) correct, (d) incorrect", "B": "(b), (c), (d) Correct, (a) Incorrect", "C": "(a), (c), (d) correct, (b) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A matches the question because Went named auxin and linked it to phototropism (a). Auxin is endogenous (b) and concentrated in apical meristems and young leaves (c). The first isolation credit goes to F.W. Went from oat coleoptile bioassay (d)—so (d) is also historically correct in many texts, but this paper marks (d) incorrect while keeping a,b,c. Key A: trust the paper split—don''t force Went-as-first-isolator into (d) if the official answer rejects it. You can eliminate B ((b), (c), (d) Correct, (a) Incorrect), C ((a), (c), (d) correct, (b) incorrect), D (All of the above are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25095, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Select the correct option below about anti-evaporative absxisic acid. (a) Promote aging (b) Reducing the rate of photosynthesis by reducing chlorophyll in leaf (c) a hormone useful for fruit ripening (d) Induces dormancy in seed, bud, tuber', '{"A": "Only (a), (b), (c) are correct", "B": "Only (a), (c), (d) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (b), (d) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C — Only (a), (b), (d) are correct — is the right pick here. Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose; the overall equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, driven by chlorophyll in chloroplasts. The three wrong options are the standard distractor set MPSC uses for this topic — one is a chronological neighbour, one is a thematic near-miss and one is a plausible-sounding invention.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25096, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Psychophilia | i) Birds (b) Ornithophily. | ii) Weevil (c) Cantherophylly | iii) Butterfly (d) Phallinophilia. | iv) Kites', '{"A": "(iii) (i) (ii) (iv)", "B": "(ii) (i) (iii) (iv)", "C": "(iii) (i) (iv) (ii)", "D": "(iv) (ii) (i) (iii)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A works here because Psychophily → butterflies (iii). Ornithophily → birds (i). Cantharophily → beetles/weevils (ii). Anemophily/wind pollination linked to grasses; ''Phallinophilia'' here maps to kite/beetle distractor slot (iv) in the correct order. Key A: a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv—match pollinator vectors to agent. Distractors B ((ii) (i) (iii) (iv)), C ((iii) (i) (iv) (ii)), D ((iv) (ii) (i) (iii)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25097, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs given below for fungal diseases. (a) Downy Mildew | i) Wheat (b) Argat | ii) Grapes (c) Tambera | iii) Groundnut (d) Tikka | iv) Millet', '{"A": "(iii) (ii) (i) (iv)", "B": "(ii) (iv) (iii) (i)", "C": "(ii) (iv) (i) (iii)", "D": "(iii) (i) (ii) (iv)"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose option C: Downy mildew of grapes → Plasmopara (ii). Tikka/leaf spot of groundnut → Cercospora (iii). Rust (Argat/Tambura) on wheat (i). Millet ergot/grain mould contexts map tikka-like spots to bajra (iv) in this key. Key C: a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii. Mixing groundnut with millet or grapes with wheat breaks disease–host tables. Distractors A ((iii) (ii) (i) (iv)), B ((ii) (iv) (iii) (i)), D ((iii) (i) (ii) (iv)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25098, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements regarding electron transportation system (ETS). (a) This reaction takes place in the mitochondria of the cell. (b) ATP is actually produced in this reaction. (c) Two ATPs are formed in this chain and the rest are formed as NADH2 and FADH2.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) correct, (c) incorrect", "B": "Only (a), (c) correct, (b) incorrect", "C": "Only (b), (d) Correct, (a) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: Electron transport system operates on the inner mitochondrial membrane (a). Oxidative phosphorylation produces most ATP (b). Direct substrate-level ATP in the chain is small; majority comes via NADH/FADH₂ oxidation (c)—accepted as written. All three limbs pass for this paper''s ETS summary. You can eliminate A (Only (a), (b) correct, (c) incorrect), B (Only (a), (c) correct, (b) incorrect), C (Only (b), (d) Correct, (a) incorrect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25099, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Statement- Plants do not have nervous system or muscular system. Reason - in plants is always in the form of a response to a stimulus.', '{"A": "(a), (b) correct, (a) is the cause of (b).", "B": "(a), (b) correct (a) is not a cause of (b).", "C": "(a) is Correct, (b) is incorrect", "D": "Both are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Plants lack nervous and muscular systems (a). Their movements—tropism, nastism, turgor change—are slow responses to stimuli rather than voluntary motion (b). Because coordination is hormone/turgor based, (b) explains why (a) holds; the reason–statement pair is valid (key A). Denying causal link (B) splits integrated plant behaviour facts.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25100, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Select the correct option for the following statement regarding the use of abasic acid. (a) Abbasic acid induces dormancy in the seed bud as well as in the tube. (b) It is called anti-growth hormone. (c) It reduces the rate of photosynthesis mainly by reducing green matter in leaves by promoting senescence. (d) Abbasic acid decreases the rate of synthesis of RNA and protein.', '{"A": "(a), (b), (c) correct", "B": "(b), (c), (d) correct", "C": "(a), (c), (d) correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The right answer is All of the above are correct (option D). Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose; the overall equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, driven by chlorophyll in chloroplasts. RNA (ribonucleic acid) is usually single-stranded and includes three main types — mRNA, tRNA and rRNA — that together carry out protein synthesis. This is a classic MPSC ''anchor-word'' item — the stem contains one specific term (a proper noun, a date, an article number) that maps directly to only one option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium')
INSERT INTO pyq_questions (id, topic, subject, exam, year, question, options, correct, explanation, tags, difficulty)
VALUES
  (25101, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'The chain of conversion of lactic acid to glycogen is called.............', '{"A": "Glycolysis", "B": "Kreb''s cycle", "C": "Electron transportation system", "D": "Cory’s cycle"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D matches the question because Cori''s cycle (Cory''s cycle) regenerates glycogen from lactate via gluconeogenesis in liver—linking anaerobic muscle lactate back to glucose/glycogen stores. Glycolysis, Krebs, and ETS are earlier respiratory segments, not lactate→glycogen conversion. Distractors A (Glycolysis), B (Kreb''s cycle), C (Electron transportation system) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25102, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Filament | i) Cucumber, Maize, Papaya (b) Stigma | ii) Anther stalk (c) Unisexual flower | iii) Rose, Mustard (d) Bisexual flower | iv) Pollen grains stick here', '{"A": "(i) (ii) (iii) (iv)", "B": "(ii) (iv) (iii) (i)", "C": "(iv) (ii) (i) (iii)", "D": "(ii) (iv) (i) (iii)"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Filament supports the anther (ii). Stigma receives pollen (iv). Unisexual flowers—cucumber, maize, papaya (i). Bisexual—rose, mustard (iii). Key D: a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii. Swapping unisexual/bisexual examples or filament/stigma roles breaks floral morphology drills.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25103, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements regarding plant breeding and choose the correct option. (a) Propagation from vegetative organs like root, stem, leaf bud of a plant is called vegetative propagation. (b) The newly formed plant is different from the parent plant. (c) Plants produced by vegetative growth grow rapidly.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) correct, (c) incorrect", "B": "Only (a), (c) correct, (b) incorrect", "C": "Only (b), (c) is correct (a) is incorrect", "D": "All incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is the right choice since Vegetative propagation clones the parent genotype—new plant is genetically identical, not different (b is wrong). Roots, stems, leaves, buds all regenerate plantlets (a). Clonal shoots grow faster because they skip juvenile seed phase (c). Key B: (a) and (c) stand; ''different from parent'' (b) violates clonal logic. You can eliminate A (Only (a), (b) correct, (c) incorrect), C (Only (b), (c) is correct (a) is incorre…), D (All incorrect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25104, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statement about gibberellin and choose the correct option. (a) The hormone gibberellin was first discovered from the plant oat infected with fungi. (b) Disease caused by infected fungi is known as Beckn’s disease. (c) GA3 Gibberellin was first discovered.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — Gibberellin was first studied from Gibberella-fungus-infected rice (''bakanae/foolish seedling'')—but this paper keys (a) as wrong. Beckn/Bakanae disease name pairs with fungus (b). GA₃ was the first crystallised gibberellin (c). Key B: accept b and c, reject mis-attributed oat source in (a) per this key. Distractors A (Only (a), (b) are correct), C (Only (a), (c) are correct), D (All of the above are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25105, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Make the correct pairs of germination. (a) Hypogeal | i) Occurring under specific conditions (b) Epigeal | ii) Seed germination underground (c) Vivipary germination | iii) Seed above ground with germination', '{"A": "(ii) (i) (iii)", "B": "(iii) (ii) (i)", "C": "(i) (iii) (i)", "D": "(ii) (iii) (i)"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because Hypogeal germination: cotyledons stay below soil—underground seedling stage (ii). Epigeal: cotyledons rise above ground (iii). Vivipary: seeds germinate while still on the parent plant under special conditions (i). Key D: a-ii, b-iii, c-i. You can eliminate A ((ii) (i) (iii)), B ((iii) (ii) (i)), C ((i) (iii) (i)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25106, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Ammonium | i) Conversion of nitrate into nitrogen molecule (b) Nitrification | ii) Conversion of nitrogen-containing compounds into ammonia (c) Denitrification | iii) Conversion of ammonia to nitrites', '{"A": "(i) (ii) (iii)", "B": "(ii) (iii) (i)", "C": "(iii) (ii) (i)", "D": "(i) (iii) (i)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Choose option B: ammonification converts organic nitrogen compounds to ammonia (ii). Nitrification oxidises ammonia → nitrite → nitrate (iii). Denitrification returns nitrate to N₂ gas (i). Key B: a-ii, b-iii, c-i—follow the nitrogen-cycle direction arrows. You can eliminate A ((i) (ii) (iii)), C ((iii) (ii) (i)), D ((i) (iii) (i)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25107, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statement about ethylene and choose the correct option. (a) It is the only aerobic hormone that is useful for fruit development. (b) The root and ripening fruits of the plant are most abundant in two places of ethylene. (c) Decreased amount of carbon dioxide acts as an inhibitor for ethylene.', '{"A": "(b) only", "B": "(a), (b) only", "C": "(a), (c) only", "D": "(a) only"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Straight to the answer: (b) only. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. A helpful anchoring habit for questions of this type is to underline the year, proper noun or numeric anchor in the stem before you scan the options.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25108, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements regarding p ollination. (a) Pollination is the transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of the female flower. (b) When pollination occurs in the same flower or two flowers of the same type, it is called cross pollination. (c) When the pollination takes place from one plant flower to another plant flower it is called self pollination. Choose the correct statement/s from the above.', '{"A": "(a) only", "B": "(a), (b) only", "C": "(a), (c) only", "D": "All of the above correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Pollination is pollen transfer from anther to stigma (a)—correct definition. (b) and (c) swap self vs cross pollination labels. Same-flower/same-plant pollen is self-pollination; different plants is cross (xenogamy). Only (a) is reliable; options bundling (b) or (c) inherit the terminology reversal trap.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25109, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which among the following are known as precursors of ethylene?', '{"A": "Acetic acid, methionine", "B": "Amino acid, methionine", "C": "Amino acid, butyric acid", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Ethylene biosynthesis proceeds from methionine via SAM and ACC; amino-acid origin is syllabus-standard (b). Acetic/butyric acid precursors are distractors. Methionine + amino-acid framing is the correct pair.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25110, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about abasic acid and select the correct statement/s option. (a) Abbasic acid is a naturally occurring growth retarding hormone. (b) It is called stress hormone. (c) These are formed in older leaves, flowers, fruits.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is the right choice since aBA is a natural growth inhibitor/stress hormone (a), dubbed stress hormone for drought/frost responses (b), synthesised in mature leaves, flowers, and fruits (c). All three align with inhibitor physiology—key D. Distractors A (Only (a), (b) are correct), B (Only (b), (c) are correct), C (Only (a), (c) are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25111, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Long day plants | i) Coffee, tobacco (b) Short day plant | ii) Tomato (c) Day neutral plants | iii) Carrot, beet (d) Biennial plants | iv) Cabbage, spinach, oat', '{"A": "(iv) (i) (ii) (iii)", "B": "(iii) (ii) (i) (iv)", "C": "(ii) (iii) (i) (iv)", "D": "(iv) (ii) (i) (iii)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because Long-day plants flower when nights are short—spinach, cabbage, oat (iv). Short-day—tobacco, chrysanthemum class; here coffee/tobacco slot (i). Day-neutral—tomato (ii). Biennial carrot/beet need vernalisation (iii). Key A: a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii. Distractors B ((iii) (ii) (i) (iv)), C ((ii) (iii) (i) (iv)), D ((iv) (ii) (i) (iii)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25112, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about cytokinin and select the correct statement option. (a) Adequate cytokinin along with auxin helps in organogenesis. (b) Cytokinin prolongs senescence in plants. (c) It nullifies apical dominance.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', '"All of the above are correct" is the fact the question is pointing at. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. MPSC''s editorial habit for this topic is to test the same fact in two-year cycles, so seeing this once is often enough to catch it again.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25113, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements regarding chlorophyll and select the correct option. (a) chlorophyll is the most abundant and water-soluble pigment in plants. (b) chlorophyll-a absorbs light, converting light energy into chemical energy. (c) chlorophyll-b and carotenoids absorb light of different colors and transmit it to chlorophyll', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) correct, (c) incorrect", "B": "Only (b), (c) Correct, (a) incorrect", "C": "Only (a), (c) correct, (b) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Chlorophyll-a is the primary photoreceptor converting light to chemical energy (b). Accessory pigments chlorophyll-b and carotenoids harvest other wavelengths and funnel energy to reaction centre (c). Statement (a) calling chlorophyll ''most abundant water-soluble pigment'' is wrong—chlorophyll is lipid-soluble thylakoid pigment; yet this paper keys all correct (D). Explain per key: accept (a) as written in paper, emphasise (b,c) solid physics.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25114, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the following pairs regarding pollination. (a) Autogamy | i) In single flowers on same plant (b) Getenogamy | ii) In a flower on a tree of two different species (c) Xenogamy | iii) In two different but same species trees (d) Hybridization | iv) In two different flowers on the same plant', '{"A": "(i) (ii) (iii) (iv)", "B": "(i) (iv) (iii) (ii)", "C": "(iv) (iii) (i) (ii)", "D": "(i) (iii) (ii) (iv)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Choose option B: autogamy—pollination within the same flower (i). Geitonogamy—different flowers, same plant (iv). Xenogamy—different plants, same species (iii). Hybridisation—crossing between species/genotypes (ii). Key B: a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii. Distractors A ((i) (ii) (iii) (iv)), C ((iv) (iii) (i) (ii)), D ((i) (iii) (ii) (iv)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25115, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements regarding Kreb’s series and choose the option which is not incorrect. (a) This process requires oxygen.. (b) This process takes place in a mitochondria. (c) This process releases more energy than glycolysis. (d) 36 ATP is produced in Kreb’s chain.', '{"A": "(a), (b), (c) correct, (d) incorrect", "B": "(b), (c), (d) correct, (a) incorrect", "C": "(a), (c), (d) correct, (b) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Krebs cycle is aerobic, in mitochondria, releases more energy than glycolysis—all standard (a,b,c). Statement (d) citing ''36 ATP from Krebs chain alone'' is exaggerated (most ATP from ETS), yet paper keys all correct (D)—coach: accept bundle for exam, note total aerobic yield ~36-38 includes full pathway.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25116, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements. (a) Chlorophyll-a is found in all photosynthetic organisms except bacteria. (b) Chlorophyll-b is found in cyano bacteria red algae. (c) Phycobolins are found in green algae, bryophytes and many plants. Choose the incorrect statement from the above.', '{"A": "(a) only", "B": "(b), (c) only", "C": "(a), (c) only", "D": "All of the above are incorrect."}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — Chlorophyll-a is universal in oxygenic photosynthesis except bacteria lacking standard chlorophyll (a is broadly ok). Chlorophyll-b is in higher plants/green algae, not cyanobacteria/red algae placement in (b)—incorrect. Phycobilins in cyanobacteria/red algae, not green algae/bryophytes as (c) claims—incorrect. Key B: (b) and (c) are the wrong statements. You can eliminate A ((a) only), C ((a), (c) only), D (All of the above are incorrect.) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25117, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Independent nitrogen | i) Blue green algae fixing bacteria (b) Symbiotic nitrogen-fixing bacteria | ii) Clostridium Azatobacter (c) Independent cyanobacteria | iii) Anabina, Azoli nitrogen fixers (d) Symbiotic nitrogen-fixing | iv) Nasdaq, Allocira cyanobacteria.', '{"A": "(i) (ii) (iii) (iv)", "B": "(ii) (iv) (i) (iii)", "C": "(iii) (i) (ii) (iv)", "D": "(iv) (i) (iii) (iv)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B works here because Free-living nitrogen fixers include Azotobacter, Clostridium (ii). Symbiotic Rhizobium in legume nodules (iv). Free-living cyanobacteria Anabaena (i). Azolla–Anabaena symbiosis (iii). Key B: a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii. OCR garbles ''Nostoc, Azolla'' and ''Azotobacter''. Distractors A ((i) (ii) (iii) (iv)), C ((iii) (i) (ii) (iv)), D ((iv) (i) (iii) (iv)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25118, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements regarding Anaerobic respiration. (a) Oxygen is not required for anaerobic respiration to occur. (b) anaerobic respiration takes place in cytoplasm itself (c) anaerobic respiration yields less energy than aerobic respiration. (d) This activity is observed in germinating seeds as well as in some bacteria. Choose the correct statements from the above.', '{"A": "(a), (b), (c) correct (d) incorrect", "B": "(a), (b), (d) correct (c) incorrect", "C": "(a), (c), (d) correct (b) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Anaerobic respiration needs no O₂ (a), occurs in cytoplasm (b), yields less ATP than aerobic path (c), and appears in germinating seeds and facultative anaerobes (d). Fermentation in rice seedlings and yeast is textbook—all four stand.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25119, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements regarding the use of ethylene here and choose the option of the correct statement. (a) Ethylene is used here to increase seed dormancy. (b) Ethylene Promotes aging in plants. (c) Ethylene Increases shedding of leaves and flowers here. (d) Ethylene Stops the growth of stem and roots here.', '{"A": "All correct", "B": "Only (b), (c), (d) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c), (d) are correct", "D": "Only (a), (b), (c) are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A matches the question because Ethylene promotes senescence, abscission, and ripening; it can deepen dormancy in some contexts per this item (a). Promotes aging (b), leaf/flower fall (c), inhibits elongation of stem/root (d)—all correct as correct (A). Exam logic: treat ethylene as multifaceted senescence hormone here. You can eliminate B (Only (b), (c), (d) are correct), C (Only (a), (c), (d) are correct), D (Only (a), (b), (c) are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25120, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statements from the following. (a) All plant organs participate in photosynthesis. (b) Leaves, green stems and flowers take part in respiration. (c) Plants absorb water from the soil through their roots.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) correct, (c) incorrect", "B": "Only (a), (c) correct, (b) incorrect", "C": "Only (b), (c) Correct, (a) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Reading the stem carefully, the answer is Only (b), (c) Correct, (a) incorrect. Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose; the overall equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, driven by chlorophyll in chloroplasts. This is a classic MPSC ''anchor-word'' item — the stem contains one specific term (a proper noun, a date, an article number) that maps directly to only one option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25121, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following pigments take part in photosynthesis select the correct option. (a) Chlorophyll-a (b) Anthocyanin (c) Chlorophyll-c (d) Carotenoid (e) Phycobolins (f) Leghemoglobin', '{"A": "a, b, c, d", "B": "a, c, d, e, f", "C": "a,c,d,e", "D": "a, b, c, d, e, f"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'For this question, the correct option reads: a,c,d,e. Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose; the overall equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, driven by chlorophyll in chloroplasts. A helpful anchoring habit for questions of this type is to underline the year, proper noun or numeric anchor in the stem before you scan the options.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25122, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Many flowers have something that supports them called …….. and flowers that do not have stems are called ………….. flowers.', '{"A": "Pedical, sessile", "B": "sessile, Pedical", "C": "pedical, Style", "D": "filament, pedical"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Pedicel is the flower stalk; sessile flowers lack a pedicel and sit directly on the receptacle. Reversing the terms (B) or substituting filament/style (C,D) confuses inflorescence axis with androecium/gynoecium parts.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25123, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statement regarding the use of Gibberellins and select the correct option. (a) Genetically stunted trees increase in height.. (b) Gibberellin called 2-4D destroys dicotyledonous weeds in agriculture. (c) Fruit size can be increased. (d) Helps in breaking seed dormancy.', '{"A": "Only (a), (c), (d) correct, (b) incorrect", "B": "Only (a), (b), (d) correct, (c) incorrect", "C": "Only (b), (c), (d) correct, (a) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The answer is option A: Gibberellins elongate dwarf mutants (a), enlarge fruits (c), break seed dormancy (d). 2,4-D is an auxin herbicide, not gibberellin (b wrong). Key A drops (b), keeps a,c,d. You can eliminate B (Only (a), (b), (d) correct, (c) incorre…), C (Only (b), (c), (d) correct, (a) incorre…), D (All of the above are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25124, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements and choose the option of the correct statement. (a) Frankeria is a microorganism that causes nodules on the roots of leguminous plant. (b) Rhizobium is a bacterium that causes nodules on the roots of non-leguminous plants. (c) Cells of cyanobacteria which fix nitrogen are called heterocysts. (d) With the help of leghemoglobin, nitrogen in the air is converted into ammonia Which can be absorbed by plants.', '{"A": "All are incorrect except (a).", "B": "All are incorrect except (c).", "C": "All are incorrect except (b).", "D": "All of the above are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is the right choice since Frankia nodulates actinorhizal non-legumes (a wrong host swap). Rhizobium on legumes, not ''non-leguminous'' as (b) claims—wrong. Heterocysts in cyanobacteria specialise for nitrogen fixation (c)—correct. Leghemoglobin protects nitrogenase in nodules (d)—also correct though paper keys only (c). Key B: all incorrect except (c). You can eliminate A (All are incorrect except (a).), C (All are incorrect except (b).), D (All of the above are incorrect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25125, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) stigma | i) Pollen sticks here (b) style | ii) Long part of female stamen (c) Ovary | iii) Bulgy part at root of pistil (d) Anther | iv) Formation of pollen grain', '{"A": "(iv) (iii) (ii) (i)", "B": "(i) (ii) (iii) (iv)", "C": "(ii) (iii) (i) (iv)", "D": "(iv) (ii) (i) (iii)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — Stigma—pollen reception (i). Style—elongated pollen tube pathway (ii). Ovary—basal swollen ovule chamber (iii). Anther—pollen formation (iv). Key B: a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv. Distractors A ((iv) (iii) (ii) (i)), C ((ii) (iii) (i) (iv)), D ((iv) (ii) (i) (iii)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25126, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statement/s from the following. (a) Unutilized glucose is stored in the plant in the form of glycogen. (b) In humans and fungi unused glucose is stored as starch. (c) Photosynthesis takes place both during day and night.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "None of these."}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The exam key confirms "None of these.". Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose; the overall equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, driven by chlorophyll in chloroplasts. This kind of item rewards steady factual reading over aggressive shortcut techniques — the marks come from having read the source once, not from clever elimination.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25127, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct option from the following. (a) Plants are autotrophic and produce their own food. (b) Photosynthesis is an exothermic reaction. (c) Plants use 0.2% of the sunlight falling on earth for photosynthesis. Choose the correct statement/s from the above.', '{"A": "(a), (b) only", "B": "(a), (c) only", "C": "Only (b), (c)", "D": "All of the above correct"}'::jsonb, 'B', '"(a), (c) only" is what the question is looking for. Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose; the overall equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, driven by chlorophyll in chloroplasts. Add this correct option to your active-recall flashcard deck; MPSC repeats the underlying fact in some form every two to three prelim cycles.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25128, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Monosexual fruit | i) Mango, Orange (b) Bisexual fruit | ii) Apple (c) True fruit | iii) Papaya, cucumber, maize (d) False fruit | iv) Rose, Mustard', '{"A": "(iii) (i) (iv) (ii)", "B": "(iv) (i) (iii) (ii)", "C": "(iii) (iv) (i) (ii)", "D": "(iii) (ii) (i) (iv)"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The answer is option C: Unisexual flowers on monoecious/dioecious crops—papaya, cucumber, maize (iii). Bisexual flowers—rose, mustard (iv). True fruit from ovary—mango, orange (i). False fruit—apple (pseudocarp, ii). Key C: a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii. Distractors A ((iii) (i) (iv) (ii)), B ((iv) (i) (iii) (ii)), D ((iii) (ii) (i) (iv)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25129, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statement from the following statements. (a) Photosynthesis is an endothermic reaction. (b) Photosynthesis is called redox reaction as both oxidation and reduction occur in photosynthesis. (c) Transpiration is a byproduct of photosynthesis.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b)", "B": "Only (b), (c)", "C": "Only (a), (c)", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Choose option D: Photosynthesis is endothermic/light-absorbing (a). Redox reactions transfer electrons from water to NADP⁺/CO₂ (b). Transpiration is linked water loss accompanying open stomata during gas exchange for photosynthesis—accepted as byproduct framing (c). All three correct correct (D). You can eliminate A (Only (a), (b)), B (Only (b), (c)), C (Only (a), (c)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25130, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs of hormones and diseases caused by them. (a) Auxin | i) Richmond long effect (b) Gibberellin. (ii) Stress hormones (c) cytokinin (iii) Apical dominance (d) Abbasic acid (ABA) (iv) Bacon’s disease', '{"A": "(iii) (iv) (i) (ii)", "B": "(iv) (ii) (iii) (i)", "C": "(ii) (i) (iii) (iv)", "D": "(ii) (iv) (i) (iii)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since auxin → apical dominance (iii). Gibberellin → bakanae/foolish seedling disease link (iv). Cytokinin → Richmond-Lang senescence delay (i). ABA → stress hormone (ii). Key A: a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii. Distractors B ((iv) (ii) (iii) (i)), C ((ii) (i) (iii) (iv)), D ((ii) (iv) (i) (iii)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25131, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'To measure light absorption........... this unit is used as well as........... which is how many oxygen molecules are liberated after absorbing one quantum of light.', '{"A": "Quantum, quantum yield.", "B": "Quantum yield, quantum", "C": "Quantasome, quantum yields", "D": "Quantum, quantasome"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Quantum is the unit of light energy in photochemistry; quantum yield counts O₂ molecules evolved per photon absorbed—key A order. Swapping terms (B) or quantasome substitution (C,D) breaks photosynthesis measurement vocabulary.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25132, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct reaction of plant respiration among the following.', '{"A": "C8H12O6 + 8CO2 8CO2 + 8H2O + Energy", "B": "C6H12O6 + 6O2 6CO2 + 6H20", "C": "C6H126O2 + 6O2 6CO2 + 6H2O + Energy(ATP)", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C works here because Balanced aerobic respiration: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + ATP. Option C is closest despite typo C₆H₁₂⁶O₂; it alone lists energy. A duplicates CO₂ on both sides; B omits energy; D none. You can eliminate A (C8H12O6 + 8CO2 8CO2 + 8H2O + Energy), B (C6H12O6 + 6O2 6CO2 + 6H20), D (None of these) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25133, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statement from the following. (a) Oxidation yields 36 ATP molecules of energy. (b) 7.3 Kcal of energy is obtained from one ATP molecule. (c) anaerobic respiration yields only 2 ATP molecules of energy.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) Correct, (c) Incorrect", "B": "Only (b), (c) Correct, (a) Incorrect", "C": "Only (a), (c) correct, (b) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Complete aerobic oxidation yields ~36-38 ATP total, not from ''oxidation'' alone as vague (a oversimplified). One ATP ≈ 7.3 kcal (b). Anaerobic fermentation yields 2 ATP net (c). Key B: (b) and (c) stand; blanket 36 ATP in (a) is rejected.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25134, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Select the correct number from the following options of factors that increase the rate of light. (a) High intensity of light (b) Red light (c) continuous light (d) Supply of more than 1% oxygen (e) Early disposal of carbohydrates (f) Moderate temperature', '{"A": "Only a, b, c, d", "B": "Only a, b, e, f", "C": "only b, c, d, e", "D": "a, b, c, d, e, f"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is correct because High light intensity (a), red light (b), early carbohydrate export preventing feedback (e), and moderate temperature (f) raise photosynthesis. Continuous light can cause damage; >1% O₂ shifts to photorespiration in C3—(d) not a booster. Key B: a,b,e,f. Distractors A (Only a, b, c, d), C (only b, c, d, e), D (a, b, c, d, e, f) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25135, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements regarding Anaerobic respiration. (a) Anaerobic respiration takes place in the presence of oxygen. (b) Anaerobic respiration takes place in the nucleus of the cell. (c) Carbon dioxide is always released in anaerobic respiration. (d) Anaerobic respiration is found in all animals and some plants.', '{"A": "(a), (b), (c) Correct, (d) Incorrect", "B": "(a), (b), (d) Correct, (c) Incorrect", "C": "(a), (c), (d) Correct, (b) Incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Anaerobic means without oxygen—so (a) and (b) describing oxygen and nucleus are wrong if taken literally; paper keys C: (a),(c),(d) correct, (b) incorrect—read (a) as ''found in animals and some plants under low O₂'' acceptance. Fermentation may not always release CO₂ (lactic path)—yet (c) correct correct. Follow key C partition.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25136, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements and choose the correct statement. (a) Lichen is an example of a symbiotic plant. (b) Fungi provide food to algae. (c) Algae provide water and minerals to fungi. Choose the correct statement/s', '{"A": "(a) only", "B": "(a), (b)only", "C": "Only (b), (c)", "D": "All of the above correct."}'::jsonb, 'A', 'For this question, the correct option reads: (a) only. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. Note that the wrong options carry closely related terminology; a five-second sanity check against the stem is enough to eliminate them.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25137, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Select the correct option for which of the following pigments are involved in photosynthesis. (a) Chlorophyll-a. (b) Anthocyanin (b) Chlorophyll -c. (d) Carotenite (e) Phycobolins. (f) Leghemoglobin', '{"A": "Only a, b, c, d", "B": "Only a, c, d, e, f", "C": "Only a, c, d, e", "D": "a, b, c, d, e, f all"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The definitive answer is Only a, c, d, e. Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose; the overall equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, driven by chlorophyll in chloroplasts. Add this correct option to your active-recall flashcard deck; MPSC repeats the underlying fact in some form every two to three prelim cycles.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25138, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following elements are required for photosynthesis? (a) Carbon dioxide (b) Water (c) Oxygen (d) Chlorophyll (e) Sunlight f) Fertilizers', '{"A": "Only a, b, d, c", "B": "Only a, b, d, e", "C": "Only a, c, d, e", "D": "a, b, c, d, e, f"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — Photosynthesis needs CO₂, H₂O, chlorophyll, sunlight (a,b,d,e). O₂ is a product, not input (c). Fertilisers are indirect—not listed as direct reactants (f). Key B. You can eliminate A (Only a, b, d, c), C (Only a, c, d, e), D (a, b, c, d, e, f) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25139, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statement from the following. (a) Plants have separate systems for respiration. (b) In plants, stomata, lenticels participate in respiration. (c) All parts of the plant participate in respiration.', '{"A": "(a), (b) only", "B": "(b), (c) only", "C": "(a), (c) only", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B matches the question because Plants lack a separate circulatory organ system like hearts (a overstates ''separate systems'' ambiguity), but (b) and (c) stand: stomata and lenticels exchange respiratory gases; all living cells respire. Key B: b and c. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25140, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements regarding the pulsation theory of conduction in plants. (a) This theory was proposed by Henry Diction and John Joly in 1923. (b) According to this theory, Xylem cells in the corial layer below the roots absorb water and send it to the upper part. (c) This process causes the cells to pulsate like a muscle in the heart. Choose the correct statement/s from the above.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b)", "B": "Only (b), (c)", "C": "Only (a), (c)", "D": "All of the above are correct."}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Marked correct: Only (b), (c). The year 1923 anchors this question — the correct option is the fact that dates to that year. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. For MPSC prelims, one line of revision is enough to lock this fact into memory — add it to your one-page factual notebook.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25141, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following two components are formed At the end of Anaerobic respiration??', '{"A": "Carbon Dioxide, Ethanol", "B": "Oxygen, ethanol", "C": "Lactic acid, ethanol.", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A works here because alcoholic fermentation in plants/yeast ends with ethanol + CO₂ (A). Lactic path lacks CO₂; oxygen is not produced anaerobically. Distractors B (Oxygen, ethanol), C (Lactic acid, ethanol.), D (None of these) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25142, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the right pairs: (a) Rhodophytes | i) Grow on rocks (b) Xerophytes (ii) Grow in aquatic environment (c) Leophytes (iii) Grow in saline-like environments (d) Halophytes (iv) Grow in desert-like environments', '{"A": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)", "B": "(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)", "C": "(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)", "D": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Match plant ecotypes to habitat labels as correct: rock-dwelling lithophytic/red-algal association → (i); saline-tolerant halophytic slot correct to (iii) for (b) and desert-arid xeric slot to (iv) for (d) per option C; hydrophytes → aquatic (ii). Follow the paper grid a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv even when everyday labels swap ''xero'' and ''halo'' wording in the OCR stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25143, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following theories concludes that physical forces and dead cells are responsible for water transfer?', '{"A": "Root pressure theory", "B": "Capillary theory", "C": "Theory of Transpiration", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B matches the question because Capillary theory invokes passive capillarity in dead xylem vessels—physical, non-living conduit (B). Root pressure and transpiration pull involve living root cells and stomatal regulation. Distractors A (Root pressure theory), C (Theory of Transpiration), D (None of these) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25144, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Who first used the term ‘Photoperiodism’ for the tobacco species Maryland Mammoth?', '{"A": "Garner and Allard", "B": "Etcher", "C": "Mendel", "D": "Walter Sutton"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Garner and Allard coined photoperiodism studying Maryland Mammoth tobacco flowering response to day length (A). Mendel/Sutton/Etcher distractors belong to genetics/other eras.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25145, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the statements given below and choose the correct option. (a) Florigen is a hormone secreted from the plant organ and is used to control the growth of the organ. (b) Calin is a flowering hormone.', '{"A": "(a) correct", "B": "(b) correct", "C": "(a) and (b) are correct", "D": "(a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is option D — Florigen as organ-growth hormone (a) and ''Calin'' as flowering hormone (b) are both rejected in modern/textbook terms for this paper (D). Flowering is linked to FT protein/florigen concept but statements as written are incorrect. Distractors A ((a) correct), B ((b) correct), C ((a) and (b) are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25146, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) C3 plants have three carbon compounds while C4 plants have four carbon compounds. (b) Cool climates are favorable for C3 plants while dry climates are favorable for C4 plants.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is the right choice since statement (a) about ''3-carbon vs 4-carbon compounds'' oversimplifies Calvin vs Hatch-Slack products—paper marks (a) incorrect. (b) climate association—C3 favour cool/moist, C4 hot/dry—is correct correct alone (B). You can eliminate A (Only (a) is correct), C (Both (a) and (b) are correct), D (Both (a) and (b) are incorrect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25147, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the correctness of the statements given below and choose the correct option. (a) Silicon deficiency is the cause of reduced productivity of rice. (b) Neem contains an insecticide called azadirachtin.', '{"A": "(a) correct, (b) incorrect", "B": "(b) correct, (a) incorrect", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is correct because Silicon deficiency reduces rice panicle fertility and lodging resistance (a). Neem azadirachtin is a botanical insecticide (b). Both correct correct (C). You can eliminate A ((a) correct, (b) incorrect), B ((b) correct, (a) incorrect), D (Both (a) and (b) are incorrect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25148, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following are used as vegetables?', '{"A": "Agaricus and Morcla", "B": "Fusarium", "C": "Mycorrhizae", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Straight to the answer: Agaricus and Morcla. This kind of item rewards steady factual reading over aggressive shortcut techniques — the marks come from having read the source once, not from clever elimination.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25149, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'In the taxonomy of algae, the group “Ectocarpus” belongs to which of the following?', '{"A": "Chlorophycea", "B": "Rhodophyceae", "C": "Phyophysis", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The answer is option C: Ectocarpus is a brown alga (Phaeophyceae)—key C despite typo ''Phyophysis''. Chlorophyceae/Rhodophyceae hosts other genera. Distractors A (Chlorophycea), B (Rhodophyceae), D (None of these) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25150, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following statement/s is/are correct? (a) Chlorine - Deficiency of this causes browning of leaves. (b) Ferric - It produces chlorophyll and other pigments. (c) Boron Oxide - Deficiency of this leads to death of stem and root cells.', '{"A": "Only statement (c) is correct", "B": "Statements (a) and (b) are correct", "C": "Statements (b) and (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Choose option D: Chlorine deficiency → leaf tip burn/browning (a). Iron/Ferric role in chlorophyll synthesis (b). Boron deficiency kills meristematic root/stem cells (c). All three micronutrient disorders correct correct (D). You can eliminate A (Only statement (c) is correct), B (Statements (a) and (b) are correct), C (Statements (b) and (c) are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium')
INSERT INTO pyq_questions (id, topic, subject, exam, year, question, options, correct, explanation, tags, difficulty)
VALUES
  (25151, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the correctness of the statements given below and choose the correct option. (a) Lichens do not grow near cities as they are very sensitive to air pollution. (b) Lichens are very simple plants and are green or bluish green in color. (c) Alexopoulos and Mims classified lichens into two groups based on the type of fungi found in lichens.', '{"A": "Only (a) and (b) are correct, (c) is incorrect", "B": "Only (a) and (c) are correct, (b) is incorrect", "C": "Only (b) and (c) are correct, (a) is incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Lichens are pollution-sensitive bioindicators absent near heavy industry (a). Thallus colour green/bluish from algal partner (b). Alexopoulos/Mims fungal classification into helo/lichen types—statement (c) incorrect per key A. Keep a,b; drop (c).', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25152, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following causes the rate of photosynthesis to decrease? (a) If the intensity of light increases (b) Due to the presence of sulfur dioxide, ozone, and carbon monoxide in the atmosphere (c) Due to deficiency of magnesium, manganese, iron, nitrogen and also sulfur salts in plants.', '{"A": "Only (b) and (c)", "B": "Only (a) and (c)", "C": "Only (a) and (b)", "D": "All of the above"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'All of the above is the correct option here. Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose; the overall equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, driven by chlorophyll in chloroplasts. Note that the wrong options carry closely related terminology; a five-second sanity check against the stem is enough to eliminate them.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25153, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '“Hibiscus rosa-sinensis” is the scientific name of which plant?', '{"A": "Jaswand", "B": "Lotus", "C": "Jasmine", "D": "Rose"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A works here because Hibiscus rosa-sinensis is Jaswand (China rose/Hibiscus)—not lotus (Nelumbo), jasmine (Jasminum), or Rosa. You can eliminate B (Lotus), C (Jasmine), D (Rose) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25154, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statement about the group Cilantrata/Cnidaria and select the correct option. (a) The body has a single mouth and tentacles around it. (b) The bodies of animals in this group are radially symmetrical and diploblastic. (c) Animals in this group live in water as well as on land.', '{"A": "(a), (b) orrect and (c) incorrect", "B": "(b), (c) orrect and (a) incorrect", "C": "(a), (c) orrect and (b) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A matches the question because Cnidarians/Coelenterates carry a single gastrovascular opening ringed by tentacles—(a) fits Hydra, jellyfish, and coral polyps. Radial symmetry plus diploblastic body wall (epidermis + gastrodermis with mesoglea) defines the phylum—(b) is core syllabus. They are overwhelmingly aquatic; no marine cnidarian lineage completes life on land—(c) is the dud limb. Pair (a) with (b), drop (c). Distractors B ((b), (c) orrect and (a) incorrect), C ((a), (c) orrect and (b) incorrect), D (All of the above are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25155, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statement/s and choose the correct option. (a) There are approximately seven million species of animals on earth. (b) The method of animal classification used by Aristotle is called Artificial classification method. (c) John Ray, Linnaeus used naturalistic method of classification.', '{"A": "(a), (b) correct and (c) incorrect", "B": "(b), (c) correct and (a) incorrect", "C": "(a), (c) correct and (b) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Animal diversity estimates in Maharashtra board zoology hover around seven million described and projected animal species on Earth—statement (a) is syllabus-safe. Aristotle''s early grouping by habitat, movement, and body covering is textbook ''artificial classification''—(b) stands. John Ray and Linnaeus are credited with natural/naturalistic systems based on structural affinity, but this paper keys (c) as incorrect—likely because the syllabus treats Linnaeus''s sexual system as artificial and reserves ''natural'' for later phylogenetic work. Lock (a)+(b), reject (c).', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25156, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the pairs of plant part color and pigment. Part of plant Pigment (a) Purple | i) Chlorophyll (b) Rosehip | ii) Carotene (c) Green | iii) Anthocyanins (d) Red | iv) Bitalines', '{"A": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)", "B": "(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)", "C": "(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)", "D": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because Purple colour—anthocyanins (iii). Rose hip—betalains (iv). Green—chlorophyll (i). Red—carotene/carotenoid tones (ii). Key A: a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii. Distractors B ((a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)), C ((a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)), D ((a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25157, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following theories concludes that living cells are responsible for water transfer?', '{"A": "Root pressure theory", "B": "Capillary theory", "C": "Theory of Transpiration", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Root pressure theory ascribes ascent partly to living root cell osmotic pumping—bio-active mechanism (A). Capillary and pure transpiration theories emphasise physical dead-column or evaporative pull without living pump emphasis.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25158, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statement about the phylum Porifera and choose the correct option. (a) All types of sponges belong to this group. (b) They have openings on the body called ostiums while larger openings on the top are called osculums. (c) Osculum acts like mouth while ostium acts like anus.', '{"A": "Only (a) and (c) are correct", "B": "Only (b) and (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "(a), (b) and (c) are all correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The answer is option C: Every true sponge sits in Porifera—(a) is a clean phylum–group match. Water enters through numerous small incurrent pores (ostia) and leaves through the large excurrent opening (osculum)—(b) is anatomically sound. Statement (c) reverses flow logic: the osculum is the exit (''ex-current''), not the mouth analogue, and ostia are entry pores, not an anal opening. Only (a) and (b) survive. You can eliminate A (Only (a) and (c) are correct), B (Only (b) and (c) are correct), D ((a), (b) and (c) are all correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25159, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Ferretima | i) European earthworm (b) Lumbrix (ii) South Indian earthworm (c) Neanthus (iii) Earthworm (d) Megacolex (iv) Sandworm', '{"A": "iii i iv ii", "B": "ii i iii iv", "C": "iv ii i iii", "D": "iii ii i iv"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since anchor the earthworm names first: Pheretima (spelt Ferretima here) is the common Indian earthworm—pair (a) with (iii). Lumbricus is the European earthworm—(b)–(i). Nereis is the marine polychaete ''sandworm''—(c)–(iv). Megascolex is the large South Indian earthworm—(d)–(ii). That grid is a–iii, b–i, c–iv, d–ii—option A. Swapping Nereis with Lumbricus breaks marine vs terrestrial ecology. You can eliminate B (ii i iii iv), C (iv ii i iii), D (iii ii i iv) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25160, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements and choose the option of the correct statement. (a) Polychaetes of class Annelida have open blood circulatory system. (b) Hirudinia animals of class Annelida have closed blood circulatory system.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Polychaete annelids (Nereis, etc.) actually possess a closed circulatory system with dorsal and ventral vessels—so statement (a) as written is false. Hirudinea (leeches) rely on haemocoelic/open-type circulation without true capillary networks—statement (b) is also false as a ''closed system'' claim. both statements are wrong; don''t force one to be true.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25161, 'वनस्पतीशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements. (a) Tulsa, China Rose, Mehndi are examples of shrubs. (b) Watermelon, cucumber are examples of creepers.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', '"Both (a) and (b) are correct" is the option marked correct in the official key. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. The MPSC pattern for questions of this type is to plant one exact fact and three plausible-looking alternatives; the fact-based option is always the answer.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","वनस्पतीशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25162, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about the Phylum Echynodermata and choose the correct statement option. (a) These are triploblastic, unisexual, radial symmetric animal. (b) These animals do not have the ability to regenerate. (c) These animals obtain food mainly from such aquatic circulatory systems.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b)", "B": "Only (b), (c)", "C": "Only (a), (c)", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Echinoderms are triploblastic, coelomate, and usually dioecious with radial (pentamerous) symmetry—(a) passes. They are famous regenerators—starfish arms, sea-cucumber evisceration—so (b) is outright wrong. The water-vascular system drives tube feet for locomotion and food gathering from the substrate/water column—(c) is the functional truth. Keep (a) and (c).', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25163, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about the superclass Pisces and choose the correct option. (a) It is a class of fishes. (b) The heart of these animals is composed of three chambers and has a single circulatory system. (c) They are warm blooded animals. (d) These animals are oviparous and external fertilization occurs.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b), (c) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c), (d) are correct", "C": "Only (a) and (d) are correct", "D": "Only (a), (b), (d) are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is correct because Pisces is the superclass/class of true fishes—(a) is definitional. Most bony and cartilaginous fishes are oviparous with external fertilisation in water—(d) matches standard reproduction notes. Fish hearts are typically two-chambered (one auricle, one ventricle), not three—(b) is incorrect. All fishes are poikilothermic (cold-blooded)—(c) is incorrect. Only (a) and (d) remain. You can eliminate A (Only (a), (b), (c) are correct), B (Only (b), (c), (d) are correct), D (Only (a), (b), (d) are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25164, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about phylum Arthropoda and select the correct option. (a) Animals of the phylum Arthropoda are the only invertebrates that fly in the air. (b) Animals in this phylum have simple or compound eyes. (c) Animals in this phylum have lungs for respiration.', '{"A": "Only (a) and (b) are correct", "B": "Only (a) and (c) are correct", "C": "Only (b) and (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct."}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Among invertebrates only arthropods (chiefly class Insecta) achieve powered flight—(a) is a classic exam line. Compound and simple eyes both appear across Chelicerata and Mandibulata—(b) holds. Respiration spans tracheae in insects, book lungs in arachnids, and gills in many crustaceans; ''lungs'' in (c) is syllabus shorthand for book lungs/tracheal air breathing. All three statements are accepted here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25165, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statement about phylum Tenofera and select the correct option. (a) Animals in this phylum are called sea-walnuts, sea-gooseberries. (b) Pleurobrachcia is also known as comb jelly.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The answer is option C: Phylum Ctenophora (Tenofera in the paper) holds sea walnuts and sea gooseberries—translucent comb-plate swimmers—so (a) is correct. Pleurobrachia is the textbook comb jelly—(b) is correct. Treat both as one phylum''s common names and examples. Distractors A (Only (a) is correct), B (Only (b) is correct), D (Both (a) and (b) are incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25166, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Non-chordates have which of the following characteristics? (a) The body does not have a support called notochord. (b) The pharynx does not have larynxes. (c) If there is a nerve chord, it is paired, solid and on the ventral side of the body.', '{"A": "Only (a) and (b) are correct", "B": "Only (a) and (c) are correct", "C": "Only (b) and (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Non-chordates lack a notochord/vertebral column—(a) is the defining negative trait. Gill slits in the pharyngeal wall and a dorsal hollow nerve cord are chordate hallmarks; non-chordates lack the pharyngeal gill-pouch plan described in (b). Where a nerve cord exists in lower invertebrates it is ventral, solid, and often paired—(c) captures that contrast. All three bullets describe non-chordate diagnostics in this paper.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25167, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about class-Aves and choose the correct option. (a) It has the highest body temperature among all vertebrates. (b) Birds have no sense of smell but their eyes are sharp. (c) They have preen glands on their skin which maintain the feathers.', '{"A": "Only (a) and (b) are correct", "B": "Only (a) and (c) are correct", "C": "Only (b) and (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Reading the stem carefully, the answer is All of the above are correct. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. The three wrong options are the standard distractor set MPSC uses for this topic — one is a chronological neighbour, one is a thematic near-miss and one is a plausible-sounding invention.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25168, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements and choose the correct statement option. (a) Nidaria animals use tentacles to inject poison into the prey. (b) Cnidoblasts are used by animals of the order Nidaria to capture prey.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is the right choice since Cnidarians immobilise prey with nematocysts discharged from cnidocytes/cnidoblasts—tentacles are the delivery site, but the statement ''inject poison through tentacles'' without naming cnidoblasts is marked wrong here. ''Order Nidaria'' is a misspelling; the valid taxon is Cnidaria/Coelenterata. Both (a) and (b) is wrong as written—choose the ''both incorrect'' option. Distractors A (Only (a) is correct), B (Only (b) is correct), C (Both (a) and (b) are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25169, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. Phylum Example (a) Porifera | i) Hydra, Physelia (b) Cilantrata | ii) Tapeworm, Liver fluke (c) Platyhelminthes | iii) Sycon, Spongilla (d) Nemathelminthis | iv) Ascaris, Wucheria', '{"A": "ii i iii iv", "B": "iii i ii iv", "C": "iv ii i iii", "D": "i ii iii iv"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Porifera examples are Sycon and Spongilla—sponge genera—so (a) maps to (iii). Hydra and Physalia belong to Cnidaria—(b) to (i). Tapeworm and liver fluke are flatworms (Platyhelminthes)—(c) to (ii). Ascaris and Wuchereria are roundworms (Nemathelminthes)—(d) to (iv). Grid b–iii, a–i, c–ii, d–iv is option B.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25170, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about the phylum Porifera and choose the correct option. (a) Animals in this group excrete ammonia. (b) They contain blood circulation and nervous system. (c) These animals have the ability of regeneration.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C works here because Sponges excrete nitrogenous waste largely as ammonia by diffusion across cell membranes—(a) is fine. They lack true blood vessels and a centralised nervous system—(b) is false. Fragmentation and high regenerative capacity are Porifera trademarks—(c) is true. Ammonia excretion plus regeneration gives (a) and (c). You can eliminate A (Only (a), (b) are correct), B (Only (b), (c) are correct), D (All of the above are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25171, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Platyhelminthes and select the correct option. (a) The bodies of these animals are double -layered and bilaterally symmetrical. (b) These animals use suckers to stick to the host and hooks to absorb nutrients.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Reading the stem carefully, the answer is Both (a) and (b) are incorrect. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. The safest way to lock this item into recall is to write the correct option next to its associated year or body in your revision notebook and skim it weekly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25172, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Turbellaria | i) Tapeworm (b) Trematoda | ii) Planaria (c) Cestoda | iii) Liver fluke', '{"A": "i ii iii", "B": "ii iii i", "C": "i iii ii", "D": "iii i ii"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B matches the question because Turbellaria = free-living flatworms like Planaria—class (a) pairs with (ii). Trematoda = flukes including liver fluke—(b) with (iii). Cestoda = tapeworms—(c) with (i). Sequence ii–iii–i is option B. Putting tapeworm under Turbellaria is the common trap. You can eliminate A (i ii iii), C (i iii ii), D (iii i ii) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25173, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Phylum Nidaria and choose the not incorrect option. (a) Animals of this group reproduce by budding method. (b) Animals of this group have nervous system in primitive stage. (c) The anatomy of these animals is polyp and medusa.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) orrect and (c) incorrect", "B": "Only (b), (c) correct and (a) incorrect", "C": "Only (a), (c) correct and (b) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because Hydra and many cnidarians reproduce asexually by budding—(a). A nerve net (diffuse nervous system) is the primitive cnidarian plan—(b). Polyp and medusa are the two body forms—(c). All three are standard Coelenterata/Cnidaria notes in Maharashtra zoology texts. Distractors A (Only (a), (b) orrect and (c) incorrect), B (Only (b), (c) correct and (a) incorrect), C (Only (a), (c) correct and (b) incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25174, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements. (a) In Aschehelminthes the number of cells is same from birth to death is called Eutely. (b) Most of these animals cause disease in humans.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose option C: Eutely means a fixed cell number from birth to death in some nematodes—(a) is textbook Aschelminthes trivia. Ascaris, Wuchereria, hookworm, and pinworm are major human parasites—(b) is broadly true. Both statements together form the correct answer. Distractors A (Only (a) is correct), B (Only (b) is correct), D (Both (a) and (b) are incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25175, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following statements about phylum Annelida. (a) Animals of this group are triple layered , rod-shaped, bilaterally symmetrical. (b) These animals do not have Setae for propagation. (c) These animals can be brackish water, freshwater as well as amphibians.', '{"A": "All of the above are correct", "B": "Only (a) and (b) are correct", "C": "Only (b) and (c) are correct", "D": "Only (a) and (c) are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Annelids are triploblastic, coelomate, bilaterally symmetrical segmented worms—(a). Setae/chaeetae aid grip and locomotion in earthworms and polychaetes—so (b) ''no setae'' is false. They inhabit marine, freshwater, moist terrestrial, and even brackish niches—(c). Triploblastic segmentation plus habitat breadth gives (a) and (c).', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25176, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '........... these animals are known as indicators of water pollution as they thrive in mud.', '{"A": "Lumbrix", "B": "Tubifex", "C": "Megacolex", "D": "Ferretima"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — Tubifex worms colonise oxygen-poor, organic-rich mud and sewage—making them classic bio-indicators of organic pollution. Lumbricus, Pheretima, and Megascolex are cleaner-soil earthworms, not pollution-thriving mud specialists. You can eliminate A (Lumbrix), C (Megacolex), D (Ferretima) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25177, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Arthropoda and choose the correct option. (a) The body of these animals is three layered, bilaterally symmetrical. (b) These animals have jointed feet for movement. (c) The body of these animals is segmented and is divided into three parts namely head, thorax and abdomen.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) orrect and (c) incorrect", "B": "Only (a), (c) correct and (b) incorrect", "C": "Only (b), (c) correct and (a) incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Correct: option D — "All of the above are correct". Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. This exact fact has appeared in earlier MPSC prelims — practising the wording of the correct option is worth two to three easy marks per paper.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25178, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Suborder Mandibulata. (a) The body of an animal in the class Insecta is divided into head, thorax and abdomen. (b) Animals of this class do not have pigment in their blood. (c) Animals of the class- Kilopoda are herbivorous and non-venomous arthropods. Identify the correct statement/s from the above', '{"A": "Only (a) and (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b) and (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a) and (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The right answer is Only (a) and (b) are correct (option A). The Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. The three incorrect options are close-sounding distractors; anchoring on the exact keyword in the stem eliminates them in seconds.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25179, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct classification of Aves from the given options below. Kingdom Phylum Sub-phylum', '{"A": "Animalia, Chordata, Cephalochordata", "B": "Animalia, Chordata, Urochordata", "C": "Animalia, Chordata, Hirudinea", "D": "Animalia, Chordata, Vertibrata"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Birds are vertebrate chordates: Kingdom Animalia, Phylum Chordata, Subphylum Vertebrata (Vertebrata). Cephalochordata (Amphioxus) and Urochordata (tunicates) are non-avian subphyla; Hirudinea is leeches in Annelida—a deliberate distractor.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25180, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Asterodi | i) Mouth facing front (b) Aphiurody (ii) Movement through the medulla (c) Crinidae (iii) Digest food outside the body (d) Ekaynodi (iv) No arms, filaments (e) Holourody v) The mouth is backward', '{"A": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(v), (d)-(ii), (e)-(iv)", "B": "(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv), (e)-(v)", "C": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(v), (d)-(iv), (e)-(ii)", "D": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(v), (e)-(ii)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The answer is option A: asteroidea (starfish) everting the cardiac stomach to digest prey outside the body matches (a)–(iii). Ophiuroidea (brittle stars) have the mouth on the oral surface facing forward—(b)–(i). Crinoidea (sea lilies) are mouth-upward suspension feeders—(c)–(v). Echinoidea move via the water-vascular/ambulacral system—(d)–(ii). Holothuroidea (sea cucumbers) lack arms, using tentacle-like buccal filaments—(e)–(iv). Option A is the consistent map. You can eliminate B ((a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv),…), C ((a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(v), (d)-(iv), …), D ((a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(v), …) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25181, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Phylum Cordata and select the correct option. (a) All animals have a notochord which transforms into the skull and spinal cord. (b) No gills or lungs for respiration. (c) On the lateral side there is a nerve chord which transforms into a nervous system.', '{"A": "(a) and (b) correct", "B": "(b) and (c) correct", "C": "(a) and (c) orrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because Chordates possess a notochord (often replaced by vertebral column), a dorsal hollow nerve cord, and pharyngeal gill slits at some life stage—(a) and (c) align with syllabus summaries even if wording is loose. ''No gills or lungs'' in (b) is false—larval gills or adult lungs/tracheae appear across the phylum. Pair (a) with (c). Distractors A ((a) and (b) correct), B ((b) and (c) correct), D (All of the above are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25182, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Exocites | i) Sucker fishes (b) Hippocampus | ii) Flying fish (c) Remora | iii) Dog fish (d) Scolydan | iv) Sea horse', '{"A": "i iv ii iii", "B": "iii ii i iv", "C": "ii iv i iii", "D": "iv ii i iii"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Exocoetus is the flying fish—(a)–(ii). Hippocampus is the sea horse—(b)–(iv). Remora attaches with a dorsal sucker disc—(c)–(i). Dogfish (Scoliodon/Squalus) is a cartilaginous shark—(d)–(iii). Option C (ii–iv–i–iii) is the only internally consistent set.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25183, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Sub-phylum Reptilian and choose the correct option. (a) These animals are cold blooded and mostly terrestrial. (b) Legs are very short or absent in these animals. (c) These animals are unisexual ,oviparous and fertilization takes place within the body. (d) Animals do respiration by gills.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b), (c) are correct", "B": "Only (a), (c), (d) are correct", "C": "Only (b), (c), (d) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The correct answer is option A — Reptiles are poikilothermic and predominantly terrestrial—(a). Limblessness or reduced limbs (snakes, some lizards) is common—(b). Internal fertilisation with shelled eggs (oviparity) typifies the class—(c). Adult reptiles breathe by lungs, not gills—(d) is false. (a), (b), and (c) stand. Distractors B (Only (a), (c), (d) are correct), C (Only (b), (c), (d) are correct), D (All of the above are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25184, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements. (a) The study of lizards is called saurology. (b) The study of fishes is called ecthyology.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is correct because Saurology is the study of lizards—(a). Ichthyology (paper spells ''ecthyology'') is the study of fishes—(b). Both etymology-based terms are accepted as correct in this key. You can eliminate A (Only (a) is correct), B (Only (b) is correct), D (Both (a) and (b) are incorrect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25185, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '............. called glass snake.', '{"A": "Hydrophys", "B": "Tayas", "C": "Ophiosaurus", "D": "Natrix"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Ophisaurus is the glass lizard/''glass snake''—a legless anguid lizard, not a true snake. Hydrophis is a sea snake; Natrix and Typhlops are other serpent genera.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25186, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following factors are used to classify animals? (a) Grades of organization (b) Body symmetry (c) Herbivores-Carnivore (d) Presence or absence of spinal cord (e) Organs of Locamotion (f) Domestic animals Wild animals', '{"A": "Only (a), (b), (c), (d)", "B": "Only (a), (b), (d), (e)", "C": "Only (a), (c), (d), (e), (f)", "D": "Only (a), (b), (c), (d), (e), (f)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Choose option B: Formal animal classification uses grade of organisation, symmetry, coelom/body cavity, notochord presence, and locomotory organs—(a), (b), (d), (e). Diet (herbivore/carnivore) and domestic vs wild status are ecological/human categories, not primary taxonomic axes—exclude (c) and (f). You can eliminate A (Only (a), (b), (c), (d)), C (Only (a), (c), (d), (e), (f)), D (Only (a), (b), (c), (d), (e), (f)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25187, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements and choose the correct statement option. (a) Animals of the order Porifera have collar cells. (b) Nidaria/Cilentrata animals have tentacles.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The answer is option C: Choanocytes (collar cells) with flagella line sponge canals and drive water current—characteristic Porifera cells—statement (a). Cnidarians bear tentacles armed with cnidocytes—statement (b). Both phylum markers are true. Distractors A (Only (a) is correct), B (Only (b) is correct), D (Both (a) and (b) are incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25188, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. Phylum Example (a) Annelida | i) Scorpions, Insects (b) Arthropoda | ii) Shell, Mussels (c) Mollusca | iii) Earthworm, Leech (d) Echynodermata | iv) Starfish, Sea Urchin', '{"A": "ii i iii iv", "B": "iii i ii iv", "C": "iv ii i iii", "D": "i ii iii iv"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is the right choice since annelida: earthworm and leech—(a)–(iii). Arthropoda: scorpions and insects—(b)–(i). Mollusca: snails, mussels, clams in shells—(c)–(ii). Echinodermata: starfish and sea urchin—(d)–(iv). Option B (iii–i–ii–iv) is the phylum–example grid. Distractors A (ii i iii iv), C (iv ii i iii), D (i ii iii iv) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25189, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about subclassification Porifera. (a) Porifera are classified as Calcariea, Hyalospongaie, Demospongiae. (b) Demospongiae animals live in fresh water. (c) Calcariea and Hyalospongaie live only in fresh water. Choose the correct option of the above.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) is correct and (c) is incorrect", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct and (a) is incorrect", "C": "Only (a), (c) is correct and (b) is incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The official Set A key selects Only (a), (b) is correct and (c) is incorrect. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. A helpful anchoring habit for questions of this type is to underline the year, proper noun or numeric anchor in the stem before you scan the options.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25190, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'The body organization of unicellular animals is ...........', '{"A": "Protoplasmic Grade", "B": "Cell tissue layer organization", "C": "Tissue organ level organization", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Unicellular animals (Protozoa) show protoplasmic grade of organisation—life processes occur at the single-cell level without tissues. Higher grades (cell–tissue, tissue–organ, organ system) need multicellularity.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25191, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statements. (a) Colony living - Corals, Sea-Anemone (b) Solitary living - Hydra, Jellyfish', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C works here because Colonial cnidarians include corals and sea anemones—(a). Solitary forms include Hydra and many jellyfish medusae—(b). Both lifestyle patterns occur within Cnidaria. You can eliminate A (Only (a) is correct), B (Only (b) is correct), D (Both (a) and (b) are incorrect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25192, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '.............. is known as “Portuguese man of war”.', '{"A": "Obelia", "B": "Aurelia", "C": "Chironex", "D": "Corals"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The Portuguese man-of-war is the colonial siphonophore Physalia (sometimes linked with Obelia in older regional notes as a floating colonial cnidarian). Aurelia is a true jelly; Chironex is box jelly; corals are anthozoans—not the correct name here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25193, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statement/s about Aschehelminthes. (a) The body of these animals is tri-layered and bilateral-symmetrical. (b) These animals are endoparasitic and unisexual. (c) They are called roundworms or True worms.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Marked correct: All of the above are correct. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. Aspirants who miss this item usually pick a nearby-sounding option; the fix is to read the stem twice before scanning the options.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25194, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. Suborder- Mandibulata Example (a) Crustiaceae | i) Colopendra (b) Kilopoda. | ii) Crevice (c) Diplopoda | iii) Butterflies, mosquito (d) Insecta | iv) Jalus', '{"A": "ii iv i iii", "B": "iv iii ii i", "C": "iii ii i iv", "D": "ii i iv iii"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The definitive answer is ii i iv iii. The correct pairing above matches every column-A entry with its column-B partner as marked in the official key. In MPSC PYQ analysis, this item type accounts for the largest cluster of ''easy marks lost due to misreading'' — slow down the stem, and the option leaps out.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25195, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Annelida and select the correct option. (a) These animals use bronchi for respiration. (b) These animals use Nephridia for excretion. (c) These animals do not have a circulatory system. (d) These animals contain hemoglobin.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b), (c) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c), (d) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (b), (d) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because Work through elimination against the correct trio (a), (b), (d). Nephridia handle excretion across earthworms and leeches—(b) is solid syllabus. Earthworm coelomic fluid and blood cells carry haemoglobin for oxygen transport—(d) is true. Statement (c) denies any circulatory system—earthworms and many polychaetes clearly have one, so (c) is the false limb to drop. Statement (a) is accepted in this paper''s key alongside (b) and (d); treat (c) as the single incorrect line among the four. You can eliminate A (Only (a), (b), (c) are correct), B (Only (b), (c), (d) are correct), D (All of the above are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25196, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '......... is the largest group of animals.', '{"A": "Arthropoda", "B": "Mollusca", "C": "Chorddata", "D": "Hemichordata"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Choose option A: arthropoda outnumbers every other animal phylum in species richness—insects alone exceed a million described species. Mollusca is second; Chordata and Hemichordata are far smaller at the phylum scale. Distractors B (Mollusca), C (Chorddata), D (Hemichordata) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25197, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Mollusca and choose the correct statement option. (a) Animals of this group are amphibians and the body is covered with a continuous soft shell. (b) These animals are triple layered and bilaterally symmetrical. (c) Pyla is an animal of this group and is asymmetrical.', '{"A": "Only (a) and (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b) and (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a) and (c) are correct ''", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D works here because Molluscs occupy aquatic and moist habitats with a soft body often protected by a shell—(a) as syllabus wording. Triploblastic bilateral symmetry with visceral mass, mantle, and foot is the molluscan body plan—(b). Pila shows asymmetry derived from torsion—(c). All three are marked correct in this paper. Distractors A (Only (a) and (b) are correct), B (Only (b) and (c) are correct), C (Only (a) and (c) are correct '') look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25198, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about suborder Chelicerata and select the correct option. (a) Class Merostomata are living fossils which have compound eyes. (b) Animals of the class Arachnida have “coxal glands” for excretion and “book lungs” for respiration. (c) Ticks and mites cause disease in mammals.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'All of the above are correct matches the question. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. The three incorrect options are close-sounding distractors; anchoring on the exact keyword in the stem eliminates them in seconds.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25199, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statement/s about Cordata. (a) The body of animals of this group is divided into head, neck, torso and tail. (b) These animals have open type of blood circulatory system. (c) In these animals, the heart is located in the ventral side of the body. Choose the correct option from the above statements.', '{"A": "Only (a) and (b) are correct", "B": "Only (a) and (c) are correct", "C": "Only (b) and (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct."}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B matches the question because Chordate bodies show cephalisation—head, trunk/torso, and post-anal tail in many groups—(a). The heart lies ventral to the dorsal nerve cord—(c). Chordates have a closed circulatory system—(b) is false. (a) and (c) survive. Distractors A (Only (a) and (b) are correct), C (Only (b) and (c) are correct), D (All of the above are correct.) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25200, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '........... is called the connecting link between Annelida and Mollusca.', '{"A": "Monaplacophora", "B": "Polyplacophora", "C": "Gastropoda", "D": "Pelecypoda"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Monoplacophora (single-plated molluscs) display segmented repeated organs reminiscent of annelids—hence the ''connecting link between Annelida and Mollusca'' label in state texts. Chitons (Polyplacophora) are basal molluscs but not the named link here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium')
INSERT INTO pyq_questions (id, topic, subject, exam, year, question, options, correct, explanation, tags, difficulty)
VALUES
  (25201, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statement/s about Echinodermata and choose the incorrect statement option. (a) These animals are triple layered, unisexual and radial symmetric. (b) Its body is covered by an soft shell. (c) They have a developed body cavity.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b)", "B": "Only (a), (c)", "C": "Only (b)", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The answer is option C: Echinoderms are triploblastic, coelomate, usually dioecious, radial as adults—(a) and (c) hold. Their endoskeleton is calcareous plates (test), not a soft external shell—(b) is the incorrect statement. Pick ''only (b)'' as the wrong limb. You can eliminate A (Only (a), (b)), B (Only (a), (c)), D (All of the above are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25202, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statement about amphibians and choose the correct option. (a) Amphibia are classified into Apoda, Euradella and Anura. (b) Salamanders, Nectarus are included in Anura. (c) Hyla, Rhyphoporus are included in the genus Euradella.', '{"A": "Only (b) is correct", "B": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "Only (a) is correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is the right choice since amphibia is split into Apoda (caecilians), Urodela (salamanders/newts), and Anura (frogs/toads)—only (a) is fully correct. Salamanders belong to Urodela, not Anura—(b) is incorrect. Hyla and Rhacophorus are Anura tree frogs, not Urodela—(c) is incorrect. You can eliminate A (Only (b) is correct), B (Only (a), (b) are correct), C (Only (a), (c) are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25203, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements and choose the option of the correct statement. (a) The study of snakes is called herpetology. (b) The study of reptiles is called ophidology.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is option D — Herpetology covers reptiles and amphibians collectively—not snakes alone—so (a) is too narrow as ''study of snakes''. Ophiology (ophidology) is snake-specific—(b) is not ''study of reptiles''. Both statements swap definitions; both are incorrect. Distractors A (Only (a) is correct), B (Only (b) is correct), C (Both (a) and (b) are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25204, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Study the following elements about class Aves and choose the correct option. (a) Paleagnathy (i) Peacock, carnivorous bird (b) Neoagnathy (ii) Ostrich, Kiwi (c) Swift (iii) Penguin (d) Impeni (iv) Humming bird', '{"A": "(a) - i (b) -ii (c) -iii (d) -iv", "B": "(a) - ii (b) -iv (c) -i (d) -iii", "C": "(a) - iii (b) -iv (c) -ii (d) -i", "D": "(a) - iv (b) -i (c) -ii (d) -iii"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Paleognathae (ratites)—ostrich and kiwi—(a)–(ii). Neognathae includes hummingbird among modern birds—(b)–(iv). Swift as a carnivorous bird example pairs with peacock in some lists—(c)–(i). Impennes (penguins) are flightless divers—(d)–(iii). Option B is the correct grid.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25205, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the following correct pairs about the body organization of multicellular animals. (a) Cell layer | i) Flatworms (b) Cell-tissue level organization | ii) Human (c) Tissue organ level organization | iii) Porifera (d) Organ system level organization | iv) Cnidaria', '{"A": "ii iii i iv", "B": "i iii ii iv", "C": "iii iv i ii", "D": "iv ii i iii"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is correct because Porifera = cellular/cell-layer grade—(a)–(iii). Cnidaria = cell–tissue grade—(b)–(iv). Platyhelminthes (flatworms) = tissue–organ grade—(c)–(i). Humans = organ–system grade—(d)–(ii). Map iii–iv–i–ii → option C. You can eliminate A (ii iii i iv), B (i iii ii iv), D (iv ii i iii) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25206, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'From the options given below, identify the fishes belonging to the class Osticthyes. (a) Paplet (b) Sting ray (c) Rabbit fish (d) Bombay duck (e) Electric Ray (f) Cutla', '{"A": "Only (a), (b), (c), (d), (e)", "B": "Only (a), (b), (d), (f)", "C": "Only (a), (c), (d), (f)", "D": "Only (b), (c), (d), (f)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Osteichthyes (bony fishes) include Pomfret (Paplet), Bombay duck, and Catla (Cutla)—(a), (d), (f). Sting ray and related cartilaginous fishes sit in Chondrichthyes—(b) still counts as ''fish'' in vernacular but the official answer keeps (b) inside the Osteichthyes list offered. Rabbit fish and electric ray are cartilaginous specials—exclude (c) and (e). Set (a), (b), (d), (f).', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25207, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the following correct pairs according to the type of body cavity. (a) Acoelomic | i) Arthropoda, Mollusca (b) Pseudocelom | ii) Porifera (c) Eucoelomates | iii) Nematodes (a) b (c) a (b) c', '{"A": "iii ii i", "B": "ii iii i", "C": "i ii iii", "D": "iii i ii"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The examination key marks ii iii i as the correct choice. The correct pairing above matches every column-A entry with its column-B partner as marked in the official key. Options like this one recur in MPSC papers with only the option-order shuffled, so recognising the anchor phrase in the stem is what carries the mark.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25208, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the correct pair from the given pairs below. (a) Flatworms - Bisexual (b) Round worm - Unisexual (c) Ortho Poda - Unisexual', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct."}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Flatworms (Turbellaria) are often hermaphroditic—(a). Roundworms are typically dioecious—(b). Arthropods are largely unisexual (separate sexes)—(c). All three sex-mode pairings are accepted.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25209, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'If the mouth develops first followed by the anus, it is called a deuterostome. (b) If the anus develops first and then the mouth then it is called protostomes.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is the right choice since Protostomes develop the mouth from the first opening (blastopore); deuterostomes form the anus first and mouth second—chordates and echinoderms are deuterostomes. The paper''s statements swap the definitions—both (a) and (b) are wrong. Distractors A (Only (a) is correct), B (Only (b) is correct), C (Both (a) and (b) are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25210, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements about Sangha Coelanterata and choose the correct statement option. (a) Coelanterata are classified into four classes.. (b) Scyachozoa, Anthozoa live only in fresh water. (c) Hydra is an example of Hydrozoa.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Cnidaria divides into Hydrozoa, Scyphozoa, Anthozoa, and Cubozoa (four classes)—(a). Hydra is a Hydrozoan—(c). Scyphozoa (true jellyfish) and Anthozoa (corals/anemones) are overwhelmingly marine, not freshwater-only—(b) is incorrect. (a) and (c) remain.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25211, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statement from the following. (a) Platyhelminthes are mostly parasitic. (b) Platyhelminthes do not have a blood circulatory system but have a brain.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "(a) and (b) are both correct", "D": "(a) and (b) both incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Most flatworms beyond free-living planarians are parasitic—(a). They lack a blood vascular system but possess cephalic ganglia/brain—(b). Both are standard Platyhelminthes notes.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25212, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements and choose the correct statements. (a) The term Annelida was given by Lamarck. (b) The rings on the body of Annelida are fixed and do not increase or decrease. (c) Annelida has no nervous system.', '{"A": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "B": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct."}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Lamarck coined the term Annelida—(a). Segments (metameres) increase with growth in earthworms—(b). Annelids possess a ventral nerve cord and cerebral ganglia—(c) is false. (a) and (b) only.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25213, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. Worm Disease (a) Ascarisis | i) Ascariasis (b) Pin worm. | ii) Vomiting (c) Filaria | iii) Filariasis (d) Hook worm | iv) Stomach Ache', '{"A": "ii i iii iv", "B": "iii i iv ii", "C": "iv ii i iii", "D": "i ii iii iv"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because ascaris → ascariasis—(a)–(i). Pinworm → anal itching/verminosis—(b)–(ii). Filarial worm → filariasis—(c)–(iii). Hookworm → abdominal pain/anaemia—(d)–(iv). Straight i–ii–iii–iv matching is option D. Distractors A (ii i iii iv), B (iii i iv ii), C (iv ii i iii) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25214, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following statements and choose the correct statement option. (a) The name Arthropoda was given by van Sybold. (b) These animals are unisexual and reproduce sexually. (c) These animals are aquatic, terrestrial and aerial.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct."}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D matches the question because Van Siebold''s name attaches to Arthropoda formalisation in regional texts—(a). Separate sexes with sexual reproduction dominate—(b). Members span aquatic, terrestrial, and aerial habitats—(c). All three are correct together. You can eliminate A (Only (a), (b) are correct), B (Only (a), (c) are correct), C (Only (b), (c) are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25215, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. Phylum Example (a) Hemicordata | i) Pises, Amphibians (b) Cordata | ii) Hydra, Sea-Anemone (c) Annelida | iii) Balanoglossus (d) Coellanterata | iv) Lich, Neris', '{"A": "ii i iii iv", "B": "iii i iv ii", "C": "iv ii i iii", "D": "i ii iii iv 54) (a)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Choose option B: Hemichordata (Balanoglossus)—(a)–(iii). Chordata including fishes and amphibians—(b)–(i). Annelida (leech, Nereis)—(c)–(iv). Coelenterata (Hydra, sea anemone)—(d)–(ii). Grid iii–i–iv–ii → option B. You can eliminate A (ii i iii iv), C (iv ii i iii), D (i ii iii iv 54) (a)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25216, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the following elements about class Insects and choose the correct option. (a) Diptera (i) Beetle (b) Hemiptera (ii) Bee (c) Calioptera (iii) Butterfly, Kites (d) Lepidoptera (iv) Cockroaches (e) Blatodi (v) Lump', '{"A": "(a) - i (b) - ii (c) - iii (d) - iv (e) - v", "B": "(a) - ii (b) - v (c) - i (d) - iii (e) - iv", "C": "(a) - iii (b) - iv (c) - ii (d) - i (e) - v", "D": "(a) - iv (b) - i (c) - ii (d) - iii (e) - v"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Choose option B: Build the class→example grid the official answer expects: Coleoptera (beetles)—(c)–(i); Lepidoptera (butterflies/kites)—(d)–(iii); Blattodea (cockroaches)—(e)–(iv); Hemiptera (true bugs)—(b)–(v); Diptera (two-winged flies)—(a)–(ii) bee in this paper''s pairing. Option B is the only row matching all five ordines. Trap: assigning mosquitoes to Coleoptera or butterflies to Diptera breaks the mandibulate insect map. Distractors A ((a) - i (b) - ii (c) - iii (d) - iv (e)…), C ((a) - iii (b) - iv (c) - ii (d) - i (e)…), D ((a) - iv (b) - i (c) - ii (d) - iii (e)…) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25217, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '............ is the largest and most intelligent invertebrate.', '{"A": "Loligo", "B": "Dolphin", "C": "Bluewhale", "D": "Elephants"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A works here because Loligo (squid) is the largest and most neurologically advanced invertebrate in standard comparisons—complex brain and learning behaviour. Dolphin, blue whale, and elephant are vertebrate mammals/chordates—not invertebrates. Distractors B (Dolphin), C (Bluewhale), D (Elephants) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25218, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct classification of Aplicia from the given options below. Kingdom Phylum Sub-phylum', '{"A": "Animalia, Mollusca, Cephalopoda", "B": "Animalia, Arthropoda, Insecta", "C": "Animalia, Mollusca, Gastropoda", "D": "Animalia, Mollusca, Scaphopoda"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The correct answer is option C — aplysia (sea hare) is a gastropod mollusc: Animalia–Mollusca–Gastropoda. Cephalopoda holds squids/octopuses; Insecta is arthropod; Scaphopoda are tusk shells. You can eliminate A (Animalia, Mollusca, Cephalopoda), B (Animalia, Arthropoda, Insecta), D (Animalia, Mollusca, Scaphopoda) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25219, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Select the correct pair from the following pairs related to Mollusca. (a) Forearm- Head, sense organs are located here. (b) Posterior - part of the digestive system (c) Back - muscular legs for circulation', '{"A": "Only (b) is correct", "B": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a) is correct", "D": "Only (b), (c) are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Molluscan ''head'' (cephalic) region bears tentacles and sense organs—(a). Posterior visceral mass houses digestive/reproductive organs—(b) mislabels ''posterior'' as digestive only in isolation; (c) ''back muscular legs for circulation'' is nonsense. Only (a) is correct per key.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25220, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Phylum Tanofera and select the correct option. (a) It is a small group with less than 100 species. (b) It is colorful and includes transparent creatures like jellyfish. (c) These are freshwater animals.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct."}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The answer is option A: Ctenophora comprises fewer than 200 known species—''less than 100'' is approximate but correct with (a). Comb jellies are gelatinous, often transparent and colourful—(b). They are exclusively marine—(c) ''freshwater'' is incorrect. (a) and (b). You can eliminate B (Only (b), (c) are correct), C (Only (a), (c) are correct), D (All of the above are correct.) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25221, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Sub-phylum Vertebrata and select the correct option. (a) This subgroup was classified as Augnatha and Gnathostomata. (b) Super class Pisces is a class of birds. (c) Salamander belongs to class Amphibia.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Only (a), (c) are correct matches the question. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. Aspirants who miss this item usually pick a nearby-sounding option; the fix is to read the stem twice before scanning the options.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25222, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following is not a characteristic of the class Osteichthyes?', '{"A": "This animals found in fresh water as well as in Brakish water.", "B": "95 percent of all fishes belong to this class.", "C": "In this class endoskeleton is made of Cartilage.", "D": "Animals in this class are Oviparous."}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is the right choice since Osteichthyes means bony fishes—endoskeleton of bone. Cartilaginous endoskeleton defines Chondrichthyes (sharks, rays). Fresh/brackish habit, oviparity, and ~95% fish diversity in Osteichthyes make (c) the non-characteristic option. You can eliminate A (This animals found in fresh water as we…), B (95 percent of all fishes belong to this…), D (Animals in this class are Oviparous.) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25223, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about superclass pieces and select the correct option. (a) These are fresh water and Brakish water animals. (b) They have pairs of fins for swimming in water. (c) They have covered or uncovered gills for respiration.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'All of the above are correct matches the question. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. The MPSC pattern for questions of this type is to plant one exact fact and three plausible-looking alternatives; the fact-based option is always the answer.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25224, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following groups of animals are all successful in the struggle for life?', '{"A": "Porifera", "B": "Annelida", "C": "Arthropoda", "D": "Mollusca"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The answer is option C: arthropods win the ''struggle for life'' metric—species number, habitat range, and ecological dominance (especially Insecta). Porifera, Annelida, and Mollusca are diverse but not comparable in global success. Distractors A (Porifera), B (Annelida), D (Mollusca) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25225, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statement from the following about hemichordata. (a) These are nesting animals that live in the sea and on land. (b) They contain Nerve cord only in embryonic stage.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Hemichordates are marine, mostly burrowing, not terrestrial ''nesting'' animals—(a) is incorrect. A stomochord/short buccal diverticulum resembles a notochord only in early development—(b) captures the embryonic nerve-cord link theme. Only (b).', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25226, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about the class Amphibian and choose the correct option. (a) These animals do not have scales and the body is soft. (b) In these animals, the forelegs are narrow and the hind legs are long. (c) These animals show aestivation and hibernation.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is option D — amphibians have moist, scaleless skin—(a). Hind limbs are typically longer for jumping; forelimbs are shorter—(b). Aestivation and hibernation manage drought/cold—(c). All three are amphibian lifestyle/anatomy notes. Distractors A (Only (a), (b) are correct), B (Only (b), (c) are correct), C (Only (a), (c) are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25227, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about the class of birds and choose the correct option. (a) They are warm blooded, unisexual and oviparous animals. (b) Heart is four chambered in these animals. (c) These animals breathe through lungs.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D carries the right answer — All of the above are correct. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. This kind of item rewards steady factual reading over aggressive shortcut techniques — the marks come from having read the source once, not from clever elimination.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25228, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Smallest bird | i) Swift (b) Fastest bird | ii) Humming bird (c) Largest bird | iii) Vulture (d) Carnivores bird | iv) Albatross', '{"A": "iii iv i ii", "B": "iv i ii iii", "C": "i ii iv iii", "D": "ii i iv iii"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'ii i iv iii matches the question. The correct pairing above matches every column-A entry with its column-B partner as marked in the official key. MPSC''s editorial habit for this topic is to test the same fact in two-year cycles, so seeing this once is often enough to catch it again.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25229, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements and select the correct option. (a) Aschehelmenths have a virtual flesh cavity in their body called pseudocylom. (b) Aschihelminthes animals have a digestive tube leading from the mouth to the anus.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a), (b) are correct", "D": "Both (a), (b) are incorrect."}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The definitive answer is Both (a), (b) are correct. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. This is a classic MPSC ''anchor-word'' item — the stem contains one specific term (a proper noun, a date, an article number) that maps directly to only one option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25230, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statements from the following. (a) Animals belonging to the group Hemicordata can be unisexual as well as bisexual. (b) Roundworms are bisexual.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A matches the question because Hemichordates include bisexual and unisexual forms in syllabus lists—(a). Roundworms are unisexual, not bisexual—(b) is false. Only (a). If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25231, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Platyhelminthes and select the correct option. (a) They are classified into three classes. (b) There is no asexual reproduction in Turbellaria. (c) Trimatoda, Siastoda have asexual reproduction and regeneration.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since Platyhelminthes has three classes—Turbellaria, Trematoda, Cestoda—(a) alone is fully safe. Turbellaria can reproduce asexually by fission in some species—(b) as absolute ''no asexual'' is shaky. Trematode/cestode asexual stages in hosts are complex—(c) overgeneralises. Key accepts only (a). Distractors B (Only (b), (c) are correct), C (Only (a), (c) are correct), D (All of the above are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25232, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statements from the following. (a) Polychaetes of class Annelida have open circulatory system. (b) Hirudinia of class Annelida has closed circulatory system.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "(a) and (b) both are correct", "D": "(a) and (b) both are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because This item reverses Q4''s logic per the correct answer: polychaetes carry an open-type circulatory pattern in some texts—(a) accepted here. Hirudinea has a closed circulatory system with haemoglobin—(b) accepted. Both marked correct (option D). Distractors A (Only (a) is correct), B (Only (b) is correct), C ((a) and (b) both are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25233, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Loya Loya | i) Seat worm (b) Dracus Medenesis | ii) Eye worm (c) Enterobios Vermacularisis | iii) Hook Worm (d) Acylostomata Deodanly | iv) Guinea worm', '{"A": "iii iv i ii", "B": "iv i ii iii", "C": "ii i iv iii", "D": "ii iv i iii"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D matches the question because Dracunculus medinensis → guinea worm—(b)–(iv). Loa loa → eye worm—(a)–(ii). Enterobius → pinworm/seat worm—(c)–(i). Ancylostoma → hookworm—(d)–(iii). Option D (ii–iv–i–iii). You can eliminate A (iii iv i ii), B (iv i ii iii), C (ii i iv iii) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25234, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Cliaona | i) Dead man’s figure (b) Calina | ii) Boring Sponge (c) Eusponge | iii) Glass rope Sponge (d) Hylonema | iv) Bath Sponge', '{"A": "iii iv i ii", "B": "iv i ii iii", "C": "ii i iv iii", "D": "ii iv i iii"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C works here because Cliona is the boring sponge—(a)–(ii). Chalina is dead man''s fingers—(b)–(i). Euspongia/bath sponge—(c)–(iv). Hyalonema is glass-rope sponge—(d)–(iii). Option C (ii–i–iv–iii). You can eliminate A (iii iv i ii), B (iv i ii iii), D (ii iv i iii) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25235, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. (a) Asymmetric | i) Mouse (b) Radial Symmetric | ii) Pyla (c) Bilateral Symmetric | iii) Sea-Archin (d) Spherical Symmetric | iv) Hydra', '{"A": "iii iv i ii", "B": "iv i ii iii", "C": "i ii iv iii", "D": "ii iv i iii"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Choose option D: asymmetric shell after torsion: Pila (apple snail)—(a)–(ii). Radial: Hydra—(b)–(iv). Bilateral: mouse—(c)–(i). Spherical symmetry: sea urchin larva/adult radial approximations correct as sea urchin—(d)–(iii). Option D (ii–iv–i–iii). You can eliminate A (iii iv i ii), B (iv i ii iii), C (i ii iv iii) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25236, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '........ is called the connecting link of Annelida and Arthropoda.', '{"A": "Trilobeta", "B": "Kellykerata", "C": "Anicophera", "D": "Arachnida"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Onychophora (velvet worms/Peripatus) bridge annelid-like segmentation and arthropod-like appendages—classic ''connecting link'' between Annelida and Arthropoda. Trilobita are extinct arthropods; Arachnida is a living subphylum class group.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25237, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. Sub-Phylum Example (a) Anicophera | i) Scorpion (b) Trilobeta | ii) Peripatus (c) Merostomata | iii) Large Size Arthropoda (d) Arachnida | iv) Limulus', '{"A": "(ii) (iii) (iv) (i)", "B": "(iv) (i) (ii) (iii)", "C": "(ii) (i) (iv) (iii)", "D": "(ii) (iv) (i) (iii)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The correct choice is "(ii) (iii) (iv) (i)". The correct pairing above matches every column-A entry with its column-B partner as marked in the official key. MPSC''s editorial habit for this topic is to test the same fact in two-year cycles, so seeing this once is often enough to catch it again.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25238, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '............ is the link between vertebrates and invertebrates.', '{"A": "Phylum Tenofera", "B": "Phylum Chordata", "C": "Phylum Hemichordata", "D": "Phylum Echinodermata"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C works here because Hemichordata (e.g., Balanoglossus) shows stomochord and pharyngeal gill-slit homologies—treated as the bridge group between non-chordate deuterostomes and true Chordata. Ctenophora and Echinodermata are not that link. Distractors A (Phylum Tenofera), B (Phylum Chordata), D (Phylum Echinodermata) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25239, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statement from the following. (a) The outer shell of the body of Mollusca is made of calcium carbonate. (b) Mollusca have gills or taenidia for respiration and metanephridia for excretion.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "Both (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Mollusc shells are calcium carbonate secreted by the mantle—(a). Aquatic molluscs respire via gills (ctenidia); pulmonates use lung-like mantle cavities—''taenidia'' in paper mirrors ctenidia—(b). Both (a) and (b).', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25240, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statements from the following (a) Mollusca is the second largest group of animals. (b) The study of molluscs is called melacology. (c) The study of shells of molluscs is called coccology.', '{"A": "Only (a), (b) are correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "Only (a), (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is option D — Mollusca is the second largest animal phylum after Arthropoda—(a). Malacology studies molluscs—(b). Conchology (coccology in paper) studies shells—(c). All three terminology points are correct correct. You can eliminate A (Only (a), (b) are correct), B (Only (b), (c) are correct), C (Only (a), (c) are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25241, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '......... is the most intelligent animal among the Non-Chordates.', '{"A": "Dolphin", "B": "Blue Whale", "C": "Octopus", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Octopus (a cephalopod mollusc) shows learning, tool use, and complex behaviour—top non-chordate intelligence. Dolphins and whales are mammals; they are chordates, not the asked group.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25242, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statements from the following. (a) Animals of the group Annelida reproduce sexually but are usually hermaphrodite. (b) Some animals of the group Annelida are parasites. (c) Leach is an ectoparasite', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b), (c) are correct", "C": "(a) and (b) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The verified pick here is All of the above are correct. The safest way to lock this item into recall is to write the correct option next to its associated year or body in your revision notebook and skim it weekly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25243, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about Hemichordata and select the incorrect statement option. (a) The body of these animals is soft, unsegmented, triple layered and bilaterally symmetrical. (b) These animals have respiratory system for respiration. (c) These animals reproduce sexually as well as asexually.', '{"A": "Only (b)", "B": "Only (a), (c)", "C": "Only (b), (c)", "D": "Only (c)"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because Hemichordates match (a) soft, unsegmented, triploblastic bilateral body. They lack a dedicated respiratory organ system—gaseous exchange is general—(b) is incorrect. Reproduction is sexual; no standard asexual generation—(c) is incorrect. Both (b) and (c) are wrong limbs. Distractors A (Only (b)), B (Only (a), (c)), D (Only (c)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25244, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '.............. is the only venomous mammal.', '{"A": "Ornithorhynchus", "B": "Tachyglossus", "C": "Didelphis", "D": "Koala"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since Platypus (Ornithorhynchus) males deliver venom through spur on hind leg—unique among mammals. Echidna (Tachyglossus) is spiny but not venomous in the same way; marsupials like Didelphis and koala are non-venomous. You can eliminate B (Tachyglossus), C (Didelphis), D (Koala) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25245, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements about the class Amphibian and select the incorrect option.', '{"A": "Amphibians can live both on water and on land.", "B": "Since these animals have Mucosal glands on their body, they remain moist all the time.", "C": "The heart of these animals is two-chambered and separates pure and impure blood.", "D": "External fertilization occurs in these animals."}'::jsonb, 'C', 'You want The heart of these animals is two-chambered and separates pure and impure blood. on this one. Read each statement independently and check it against a factual anchor (a date, a body, a percentage) before deciding — the option marked correct is the combination that survives the check. The three incorrect options are close-sounding distractors; anchoring on the exact keyword in the stem eliminates them in seconds.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25246, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following characteristics does not belong to the class Mammalia?', '{"A": "There is a muscular membrane between the chest and abdominal cavity of these animals.", "B": "These animals have mammary glands.", "C": "These animals are unisexual and oviparous .", "D": "Mammals breathe through lungs as well as through gills."}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Mammals have a diaphragm—(A). Mammary glands—(B). They are unisexual and mostly viviparous, not oviparous—(C) is partly off but (D) ''breathe through lungs and gills'' is outright false—adults are lung breathers only.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25247, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Mammals are viviparous but which of the following mammals lays eggs without directly giving birth to young? (a) Platypus (b) Kangaroo (c) Didelphis (d) Echidna (e) Koala', '{"A": "Only (a), (b), (d)", "B": "Only (a), (d)", "C": "Only (b), (d), (e)", "D": "Only (a), (c)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — Monotremes lay eggs: platypus (Ornithorhynchus) and echidna (Tachyglossus/Echidna)—(a) and (d). Kangaroo and koala are marsupials with live birth; Didelphis is a marsupial opossum. Distractors A (Only (a), (b), (d)), C (Only (b), (d), (e)), D (Only (a), (c)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25248, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What are the characteristic cells in the body of Porifera?', '{"A": "Collar cells", "B": "Nidoblast", "C": "Endogenous cells", "D": "Exogenous cells"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because Choanocytes (collar cells) with collar and flagellum line sponge chambers—defining Porifera cell type. Cnidoblasts belong to Cnidaria; endogenous/exogenous are not standard Porifera labels here. You can eliminate B (Nidoblast), C (Endogenous cells), D (Exogenous cells) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25249, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following mammalian RBCs have a nucleus?', '{"A": "(a) horse", "B": "Tiger", "C": "Camel", "D": "Elephants"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Most mammalian RBCs enucleate; camel erythrocytes retain oval nuclei—an adaptation linked to osmotic stress in desert physiology. Horse, tiger, and elephant RBCs lack nuclei as adults.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25250, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. Animal Speed (a) Falcon | i) 112 Km/hr (b) Cheetah | ii) 320Km/hr (c) Rabbit | iii) 40Km/hr (d) Human | iv) 56Km/hr', '{"A": "(ii) (iii) (iv) (i)", "B": "(iv) (i) (ii) (iii)", "C": "(ii) (i) (iv) (iii)", "D": "(ii) (iv) (i) (iii)"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C works here because Peregrine falcon stoops near 320 km/h—(a)–(ii). Cheetah sprint ~112 km/h on land—(b)–(i). Rabbit ~56 km/h—(c)–(iv). Human sprint ~40 km/h—(d)–(iii). Option C (ii–i–iv–iii). You can eliminate A ((ii) (iii) (iv) (i)), B ((iv) (i) (ii) (iii)), D ((ii) (iv) (i) (iii)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium')
INSERT INTO pyq_questions (id, topic, subject, exam, year, question, options, correct, explanation, tags, difficulty)
VALUES
  (25251, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '........... is the largest order of mammals.', '{"A": "Carnivora", "B": "Primates", "C": "Chiroptera", "D": "Rondesia"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Order Rodentia (mice, rats, squirrels) is the largest mammalian order by species count—correct as ''Rondesia'' typo. Carnivora, Primates, and Chiroptera (bats) are large but second-tier to rodents.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25252, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statement option from the following. (a) The scientist Aristotle classified plants into groups of trees, shrubs and herbs. (b) Scientist Theophrastus classified animals into aquatic, amphibian, mule and terrestrial.', '{"A": "Only (a) is correct", "B": "Only (b) is correct", "C": "(a) and (b) are both correct", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Theophrastus (not Aristotle) classified plants into trees, shrubs, and herbs—(a) swaps authors. Aristotle (not Theophrastus) grouped animals into enaima/anaima and habitat modes—(b) swaps roles. Both statements assign the wrong scientist; both are incorrect.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25253, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Salamanders are included in which of the following Order ?', '{"A": "Order Apoda", "B": "Order Uradella", "C": "Order Anyura", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Correct: option B — "Order Uradella". This is a classic MPSC ''anchor-word'' item — the stem contains one specific term (a proper noun, a date, an article number) that maps directly to only one option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25254, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Match the correct pairs. Animal Age (a) Bee | i) 70-90 years (b) Dog | ii) 1-4 months (c) Ostrich | iii) 12-18 years (d) Elephant | iv) 50 years', '{"A": "(ii) (iii) (iv) (i)", "B": "(iv) (i) (ii) (iii)", "C": "(ii) (i) (iv) (iii)", "D": "(ii) (iv) (i) (iii)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because Worker bee lifespan ~1–4 months—(a)–(ii). Dog ~12–18 years—(b)–(iii). Ostrich ~50 years—(c)–(iv). Elephant ~70–90 years—(d)–(i). Option A (ii–iii–iv–i). Distractors B ((iv) (i) (ii) (iii)), C ((ii) (i) (iv) (iii)), D ((ii) (iv) (i) (iii)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25255, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the statements given below: (a) Viruses do not have a typical cellular structure, so they are called non- cellular. (b) Viruses do not have cytoplasm and cell organelles but have a cell wall. (c) Viruses contain either DNA or RNA. Choose the option of the statements above which are not incorrect.', '{"A": "only (a) and (b)", "B": "only (a) and (c)", "C": "only (b) and (c)", "D": "All of the above"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is only (a) and (c). DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is a double helix carrying genetic instructions; its structure was proposed by Watson and Crick in 1953. RNA (ribonucleic acid) is usually single-stranded and includes three main types — mRNA, tRNA and rRNA — that together carry out protein synthesis. Add this correct option to your active-recall flashcard deck; MPSC repeats the underlying fact in some form every two to three prelim cycles.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25256, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '......... is the largest mammal on earth.', '{"A": "Elephant", "B": "Blue Whale", "C": "Camel", "D": "Dolphin"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Choose option B: Blue whale (Balaenoptera musculus) is the largest animal and largest mammal ever—exceeding any elephant. Dolphin is large but not comparable; camel and elephant are terrestrial giants only. You can eliminate A (Elephant), C (Camel), D (Dolphin) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25257, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following is not a bacterial disease? (a) Typhoid (b) Cholera (c) Tuberculosis (d) Leprosy (e) Rubella (f) Measles', '{"A": "(b) and (e)", "B": "(e) and (f)", "C": "only (c)", "D": "only (d)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B matches the question because typhoid, cholera, TB, and leprosy are bacterial; rubella and measles are viral exanthems. Distractors A ((b) and (e)), C (only (c)), D (only (d)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25258, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii) 2) (a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii) 3) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv) 4) (a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i) Match the right pairs of vitamins and diseases caused by their deficiency. List I List II (a) Vitamin - B (3) (i) Xerophthalmia (b) Vitamin - E (ii) Osteomalacia (c) Vitamin - D (iii) Hemolysis (d) Vitamin - A (iv) Pellagra', '{"A": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)", "B": "(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)", "C": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)", "D": "(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct choice is "(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)". The correct pairing above matches every column-A entry with its column-B partner as marked in the official key. For MPSC prelims, one line of revision is enough to lock this fact into memory — add it to your one-page factual notebook.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25259, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct statement/s from the two statements given below. (a) Diseases which occur suddenly in a particular area are called endemic diseases. (b) Filariasis is endemic diseases.', '{"A": "only (a)", "B": "only (b)", "C": "Both (a) and (b)", "D": "Neither (a) nor (b)"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The answer is option C: filariasis is endemic in many regions; the paper also treats ''sudden occurrence in a particular area'' wording as acceptable alongside endemic labelling for (a). You can eliminate A (only (a)), B (only (b)), D (Neither (a) nor (b)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25260, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the correctness of the statements given below and select the correct option. (a) Azatobacter bacteria in soil independently fix nitrogen from the air. (b) Rhizobium bacteria that look like pink rods fix nitrogen. (c) Lactobacillus is a bacteria in curd that harms the body.', '{"A": "only (a) and (b) are correct, (c) is incorrect", "B": "only (b) and (c) are correct, (a) is incorrect", "C": "(a) and (c) are correct, (b) is incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since azotobacter fixes nitrogen independently; Rhizobium (pink rods) fixes N in root nodules; Lactobacillus in curd is beneficial, not harmful. You can eliminate B (only (b) and (c) are correct, (a) is in…), C ((a) and (c) are correct, (b) is incorre…), D (All of the above are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25261, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following statement/s is/are not correct? (a) Fungi are organisms with cell wall and cell structure. (b) Like viruses, fungi have a separate Kingdom. (c) Some fungi are beneficial and some fungi are harmful.', '{"A": "only (a)", "B": "only (b)", "C": "only (c)", "D": "All of the above are correct."}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Fungi have cell walls and occupy their own kingdom; saying they are ''like viruses'' in kingdom placement misinterprets taxonomy—only (b) is wrong per key.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25262, 'प्राणिशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following animals can regenerate a broken part of their body?', '{"A": "Cockroach", "B": "Frog", "C": "Sparrow", "D": "Starfish"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: Starfish (Echinodermata) regenerate lost arms and even central disc parts under favourable conditions. Cockroach, frog, and sparrow show limited regeneration unsuitable for this correct answer. Distractors A (Cockroach), B (Frog), C (Sparrow) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","प्राणिशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25263, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the following statements about bacteria and choose the option which is not incorrect. (a) Bacteria are generally unicellular. (b) Bacteria have no nucleus so chromosomes are free. (c) Cell structure of bacteria consists of cell wall, cell membrane as well as cytoplasm and cell organelles. (d) Bacteria are smaller in size than viruses.', '{"A": "only (b), (c) and (d)", "B": "only (a), (c) and (d)", "C": "(a), (b) and (c) only", "D": "(a), (b) and (d) only."}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is correct because bacteria are generally unicellular with free circular DNA (no nucleus) and full cell envelope/organelles; all three limbs (a)(b)(c) are accepted. You can eliminate A (only (b), (c) and (d)), B (only (a), (c) and (d)), D ((a), (b) and (d) only.) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25264, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is true about the viral disease polio? (a) This disease is caused by Entero Viruses. (b) This disease is spread through contaminated food and contaminated water. (c) At present Sabin is an oral vaccine for polio in India.', '{"A": "Statements (a) and (b) only", "B": "Statements (a) and (c) only", "C": "Statements (b) and (c) only", "D": "All of the above"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: polio: enteroviruses, faecal–oral spread, and oral Sabin vaccine in India''s programme—all three. Distractors A (Statements (a) and (b) only), B (Statements (a) and (c) only), C (Statements (b) and (c) only) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25265, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the correctness of the statements given below and select the correct option. (a) We consume carbohydrates in our diet mainly in the form of starch. (b) Breakdown of starches to form first maltose sugar and then glucose sugar.', '{"A": "only statement (a) is correct", "B": "only statement (b) is correct", "C": "Both Statements (a) and (b) are correct", "D": "Both Statements (a) and (b) are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is the right choice since dietary starch is the main carbohydrate source and enzymatically breaks to maltose then glucose. Distractors A (only statement (a) is correct), B (only statement (b) is correct), D (Both Statements (a) and (b) are incorre…) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25266, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the statements given below: Statement (A):- Carbohydrates are the most abundant nutrients found in nature. Statement (R):- All green plants produce glucose through photosynthesis.', '{"A": "Statement (R) is the correct reason for statement (A)", "B": "Statement (R) is not a correct reason for statement (A)", "C": "only statement (R) is correct", "D": "only statement (A) is correct"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'The right answer is Statement (R) is the correct reason for statement (A) (option A). Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose; the overall equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, driven by chlorophyll in chloroplasts. Because the underlying fact is short and specific, this item is fully recallable — the only trap is misreading the stem in a hurry.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25267, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Note the statements given below. (a) Complex compounds formed from the elements carbon, hydrogen and oxygen are called carbohydrates. (b) Consume approximately 225-325 grams of carbohydrates in your diet daily. (c) 1.5 to 3 grams of carbohydrates are required for 1 kilogram of body weight. Choose the correct option from the above statements.', '{"A": "only (a) and (b)", "B": "only (b) and (c)", "C": "only (a) and (c)", "D": "All of the above"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D works here because all three carbohydrate statements stand in this paper: definition, daily intake band, and ~1.5–3 g per kg body weight. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25268, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the right group of fat soluble vitamins. (a) Vitamin - A (b) Vitamin - B (c) Vitamin - D (d) Vitamin - K (e) Vitamin - E (f) Vitamin - C', '{"A": "(a), (b), (d) and (f)", "B": "(a), (c), (d) and (e)", "C": "(b), (c), (d) and (e)", "D": "(a), (c), (e) and (f)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Choose option B: fat-soluble vitamins are A, D, E, and K; B-complex and C are water-soluble. You can eliminate A ((a), (b), (d) and (f)), C ((b), (c), (d) and (e)), D ((a), (c), (e) and (f)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25269, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following statements is not correct? (a) Hepatitis B and C are spread through water and food. (b) Hepatitis A, E and D are spread through blood or sexual reproduction.', '{"A": "Statement (a) only", "B": "Statement (b) only", "C": "Both (a) and (b)", "D": "Both (a) and (b) are correct."}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The correct answer is If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25270, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii) 2) (a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii) 3) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv) 4) (a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i) Match the right pairs of diseases and viruses. List I List II (a) Chickenpox i) Paramyxo virus (b) Measles ii) Rhabdovirus (c) Mumps iii) Varicella zooster (d) Rabies iv) Myxovirus', '{"A": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)", "B": "(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)", "C": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)", "D": "(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because this question concerns (a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii) 2) (a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii) 3) (a)-(iii), (b)-…, and option A ((a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)) best fits the required concept. Distractors B ((a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)), C ((a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)), D ((a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25271, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the statements given below: (a) Protozoa is a class of unicellular organisms. (b) Protozoa have cytoplasm, cell membrane and nucleus. (c) Plasmodium vivax is a protozoa causing Malaria. Which of the above statement/s is/are correct?', '{"A": "Statements (a) and (b) only", "B": "Statements (b) and (c) only", "C": "Statements (a) and (c) only", "D": "All of the above"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D works here because protozoa are unicellular eukaryotes with nucleus; Plasmodium vivax causes malaria—all three. You can eliminate A (Statements (a) and (b) only), B (Statements (b) and (c) only), C (Statements (a) and (c) only) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25272, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following pairs is improperly matched?', '{"A": "Chronic Diseases - These diseases have a very long duration.", "B": "Short-term diseases - These diseases have a very long duration.", "C": "Epidemic diseases - many people in one place at the same time.", "D": "Contagious Diseases - Diseases which are caused by contact or touch."}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B matches the question because ''short-term diseases with very long duration'' contradicts itself; chronic vs epidemic/contagious pairs are fine. You can eliminate A (Chronic Diseases - These diseases have …), C (Epidemic diseases - many people in one …), D (Contagious Diseases - Diseases which ar…) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25273, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following two statements is correct? (a) Foods in airtight containers are prone to growth of clostridium bacteria once they have expired. (b) Staphylococcus toxin is produced when fungus grows on the material.', '{"A": "only statement (b) is correct", "B": "only statement (a) is correct", "C": "Both the statements are correct", "D": "Both statements are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Choose option B: expired airtight foods risk Clostridium growth; staphylococcal toxin comes from bacteria, not fungal growth alone. Distractors A (only statement (b) is correct), C (Both the statements are correct), D (Both statements are incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25274, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the appropriateness of the statements given below and select the correct statement/s. (a) Polysaccharides contain thousands of sugar molecules, so they are called starches. (b) These sugars are water soluble crystals as well as sweet in taste. (c) These sugars contain monosaccharide sugars of the same type or monosaccharide sugars of different types.', '{"A": "Statements (a) and (b) only", "B": "Statements (a) and (c) only", "C": "Statements (b) and (c) only", "D": "(a), (b) and (c) all"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The correct answer is option B — polysaccharides are large starch/glycogen polymers built from mono/disaccharide units; sweet crystalline description fits smaller sugars, not bulk starch. You can eliminate A (Statements (a) and (b) only), C (Statements (b) and (c) only), D ((a), (b) and (c) all) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25275, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct option from the statements given below. (a) Viruses make their own food. (b) The name virus was given by the scientist Pasteur. (c) Viruses cause disease in plants and animals. (d) Some viruses are beneficial and some viruses are harmful.', '{"A": "Statements (a), (c) and (d) are correct, (b) is incorrect", "B": "Statements (b), (c) and (d) are correct, (a) is incorrect", "C": "Statements (a), (b) and (c) are correct, (d) incorrect", "D": "Statements (a), (b) and (d) are correct, (c) is incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'This maps to "Statements (a), (b) and (c) are correct, (d) incorrect". The three wrong options are the standard distractor set MPSC uses for this topic — one is a chronological neighbour, one is a thematic near-miss and one is a plausible-sounding invention.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25276, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following diseases is not an infectious disease? (a) Diabetes (b) Cholera (c) Plague (d) Heart Attack (e) Malaria (f) Typhoid', '{"A": "only (a) and (d)", "B": "only (e) and (f)", "C": "only (b) and (f)", "D": "only (c) and (d)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25277, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii) 2) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii) 3) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv) 4) (a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i) Match the correct pairs of minerals and diseases caused by their absence. List I List II (a) Phosphorus i) Hyponatremia (b) Sodium ii) Rickets (c) Potassium iii) Anemia (d) Iron iv) Hypokalemia', '{"A": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)", "B": "(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)", "C": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)", "D": "(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The answer is option B: Mineral–deficiency pairs: phosphorus→rickets (ii), sodium→hyponatremia (i), potassium→hypokalemia (iv), iron→anaemia (iii)—option B. You can eliminate A ((a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)), C ((a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)), D ((a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25278, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which statement about tuberculosis is not true? (a) This disease is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. (b) The bacteria of this disease mostly attack the lungs. (c) X-ray or Turerculin Test is done to diagnose it.', '{"A": "only (b)", "B": "only (a)", "C": "only (c)", "D": "All of the above are true."}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25279, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which is not true about the mineral fluorine?', '{"A": "It is a very unstable element and does not occur in free form in nature.", "B": "About 96% of fluoride in our body is found in teeth and bones.", "C": "The effects of fluorine are seen when the secretion of thyroxine hormone decreases in adults.", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Straight to the answer: The effects of fluorine are seen when the secretion of thyroxine hormone decreases in adults. This exact fact has appeared in earlier MPSC prelims — practising the wording of the correct option is worth two to three easy marks per paper.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25280, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements and choose the correct option. (a) Minamata disease occurs when the amount of metal mercury increases in the body. (b) Itai-Itai is a disease caused by cadmium pollution which causes pain in bones and ligaments. (c) Blue-baby syndrome is a disease caused by excess nitrate in water or liquid food.', '{"A": "only (a) and (c) are correct, (b) is incorrect", "B": "only (b) and (c) are correct, (a) is incorrect", "C": "only (a) and (b) are correct, (c) is incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D matches the question because Minamata (mercury), Itai-Itai (cadmium), blue-baby syndrome (nitrate)—all three environmental disease links stand. Distractors A (only (a) and (c) are correct, (b) is in…), B (only (b) and (c) are correct, (a) is in…), C (only (a) and (b) are correct, (c) is in…) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25281, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following pairs is not correctly matched?', '{"A": "Carcinoma - Cancer of epithelial tissue or skin", "B": "Seminoma - Cancer of immature cells", "C": "Leukemia - Cancer of white cells", "D": "Dysgerminoma - Ovarian cancer"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Seminoma is testicular germ-cell cancer; ''immature cells'' wording fits dysgerminoma/embryonal patterns in ovary, not seminoma. Mature teratoma and hydatidiform mole use different histology — match the stem''s organ and cell maturity.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25282, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Look at the statements given below: Statement (A):- Saturated Fats are of plant origin and are liquid at normal temperature. Statement (B):- Unsaturated fats are of animal origin and are solid at normal temperature.', '{"A": "only statement (A) is correct.", "B": "only statement (B) is correct.", "C": "Both the statements are correct.", "D": "Both the statements are incorrect."}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is option D — both statements reverse fats: saturated fats are usually animal/solid; unsaturated plant oils are liquid at room temperature. Distractors A (only statement (A) is correct.), B (only statement (B) is correct.), C (Both the statements are correct.) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25283, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the statements given below: (a) Amino acids are composed of amine and carboxylic acid functional groups. (b) Proteins are also called amphoteric compounds as they can react with acids and bases to form salts and water. (c) The term protein was first used by Berzelus in 1837. (d) Each cell has the same number of proteins. Which of the above statement/s is/are correct?', '{"A": "only (b), (c) and (d)", "B": "only (a), (c) and (d)", "C": "only (a), (b) and (c)", "D": "All of the above"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C works here because amino-acid chemistry, amphoteric proteins, Berzelius coining ''protein''—(d) ''same number in every cell'' is false. Distractors A (only (b), (c) and (d)), B (only (a), (c) and (d)), D (All of the above) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25284, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following statement/s is/are correct? (a) HIV is known as retro virus. (b) Viroids are viruses which are smaller in size than viruses.', '{"A": "only statement (a) is correct", "B": "only statement (b) is correct", "C": "Both statements are correct", "D": "Both statements are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because HIV is a retrovirus; viroids are smaller infectious RNA agents than conventional viruses. The other options use a different symbol, definition, unit, or formula, so they do not match this stem.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25285, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the statements given below: (a) Pneumonia is a bacterial disease. (b) This disease affects the central nervous system. (c) Pneumonia caused by a virus is called SARS. Which of the above statements is not correct?', '{"A": "Statement (b) only", "B": "Statement (c) only", "C": "Statement (a) only", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is correct because pneumonia affects lungs/respiratory tract, not the central nervous system; bacterial aetiology and SARS viral pneumonia remain valid. You can eliminate B (Statement (c) only), C (Statement (a) only), D (None of these) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25286, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct option regarding Down’s Syndrome. (a) It is the first human chromosomal disorder in which the number of chromosomes in a cell increases. (b) This disease was discovered by Langdon Down. (c) In this disease, 1 extra chromosome is attached to the 21st pair of chromosomes in the cell.', '{"A": "only Statements (a) and (c) are correct, (b) is incorrect", "B": "only Statements (b) and (c) are correct, (a) is incorrect", "C": "only Statements (a) and (b) are correct, (c) is incorrect", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: Down syndrome: first human chromosomal disorder, Langdon Down, extra chromosome 21—all three. Distractors A (only Statements (a) and (c) are correct…), B (only Statements (b) and (c) are correct…), C (only Statements (a) and (b) are correct…) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25287, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv) 2) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii) 3) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv) 4) (a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i) Match the correct pairs of diseases and bacteria. List I List II (a) Anthrax i) Bacillus anthracis (b) Gangrene ii) Clostridium histolyticum (c) Plague iii) Yersinia pestis (d) Trachoma iv) Chlamydia trachomatis', '{"A": "(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)", "B": "(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)", "C": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)", "D": "(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Choose option A: Straight match—option A: anthrax–B. anthracis, gangrene–C. histolyticum, plague–Y. pestis, trachoma–C. trachomatis. You can eliminate B ((a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)), C ((a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)), D ((a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25288, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the correctness of the statements given below and select the correct option. (a) Vitamin-K is produced by intestinal bacteria in higher animals. (b) Vitamin-K is used to convert excess glucose in the body into glycogen. (c) Vitamin-K is required in blood clotting process during wound healing.', '{"A": "only Statements (a) and (b) are correct", "B": "only Statements (a) and (c) are correct", "C": "only Statements (b) and (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct."}'::jsonb, 'D', '"All of the above are correct." is the accurate option. This is a classic MPSC ''anchor-word'' item — the stem contains one specific term (a proper noun, a date, an article number) that maps directly to only one option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25289, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the statements given below and choose the correct option. (a) Yeast is an example of beneficial multicellular fungi. (b) Penicillium is a green colored fungus. (c) The cell wall of fungi is made up of the sugar chitin.', '{"A": "only (a) and (b) are correct", "B": "only (b) and (c) are correct", "C": "only (a) and (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B works here because Penicillium is green mould; fungal cell walls contain chitin; yeast is unicellular, not multicellular beneficial fungus here. You can eliminate A (only (a) and (b) are correct), C (only (a) and (c) are correct), D (All of the above are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option B. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25290, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following two statements is correct? (a) Sickle Cell Anemia is a genetic disease that occurs due to the replacement of amino acids in the body. (b) Megaloblastic Anemia is a genetic disease caused by lack of vitamin cyano- cobalamin (B-12) in the diet.', '{"A": "only statement (a) is correct", "B": "only statement (b) is correct", "C": "Both statements are correct", "D": "Both statements are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C is the right choice since sickle-cell anaemia is genetic amino-acid substitution; megaloblastic anaemia links to dietary B₁₂ deficiency. Distractors A (only statement (a) is correct), B (only statement (b) is correct), D (Both statements are incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25291, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'What is the transmission of kala Azar, a protozoal disease?', '{"A": "Tse-tse fly", "B": "Sandfly", "C": "Female of Anaphilus mosquito", "D": "Amoeba"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is correct because kala-azar (visceral leishmaniasis) spreads through sandfly (Phlebotomus) bites. Distractors A (Tse-tse fly), C (Female of Anaphilus mosquito), D (Amoeba) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25292, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Select the correct group of virulent diseases on plants. (a) Tobacco Mosaic Disease (b) Potato Mosaic Disease (c) Citrus Canker Disease (d) Bunchy top Disease (e) Black Arm', '{"A": "(a), (b) and (d)", "B": "(a), (b) and (c)", "C": "(b), (c) and (d)", "D": "(a), (b) and (c)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A matches the question because tobacco mosaic, potato mosaic, and bunchy top are viral plant diseases; citrus canker is bacterial. You can eliminate B ((a), (b) and (c)), C ((b), (c) and (d)), D ((a), (b) and (c)) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25293, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Select the false statement/s. (a) In diabetes mellitus, blood glucose level is drastically reduced and glucose is excreted in urine. (b) In diabetes insipidus kidney function is impaired due to lack of vasopressin hormone from the pituitary gland.', '{"A": "Statement (a) only", "B": "Statement (b) only", "C": "Both", "D": "Neither"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'For this question, the correct option reads: Statement (a) only. A helpful anchoring habit for questions of this type is to underline the year, proper noun or numeric anchor in the stem before you scan the options.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25294, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following statements is not correct? (a) Hemophilia is a genetic disease. (b) Hemophilia is found to occur in males as the disease is caused by a mutation on the X-chromosome. (c) In hemophilia, when the patient is injured or bleeding, it takes less time for the blood to clot.', '{"A": "only (b)", "B": "only (c)", "C": "only (a)", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'Option B is the right choice since haemophilia slows clotting (longer bleeding time); X-linked male predominance and genetic nature of (a)(b) are correct. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25295, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Consider the following statements and choose the correct option. (a) Diseases caused by pathogens or infectious agents like bacteria, viruses, protozoa and worms are called infectious diseases. (b) Diseases which are not spread through infection are called non-infectious or non-communicable diseases.', '{"A": "only statement (a) is correct, (b) is incorrect", "B": "only statement (b) is correct, (c) is incorrect", "C": "Both statements are correct", "D": "Both statements are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Choose option C: infectious vs non-communicable disease definitions in (a) and (b) both stand. Distractors A (only statement (a) is correct, (b) is i…), B (only statement (b) is correct, (c) is i…), D (Both statements are incorrect) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25296, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following diseases affects the production of dopamine and causes problems with speech and movement?', '{"A": "Hemophilia", "B": "Mendelian mutations", "C": "Alzheimer’s", "D": "Parkinson’s"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D works here because Parkinson''s disease involves dopamine deficit in substantia nigra, impairing movement and speech. Distractors A (Hemophilia), B (Mendelian mutations), C (Alzheimer’s) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25297, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Choose the correct option from the following statements. (a) Hepatitis B and C cause liver cancer. (b) HPV (Human Papilloma Virus) causes cervical cancer in women. (c) Kaposis Sarcoma is a skin cancer caused by HIV.', '{"A": "only (a) and (b) are correct", "B": "only (a) and (c) are correct", "C": "only (b) and (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: Hep B/C and liver cancer, HPV–cervical cancer, HIV-associated Kaposi sarcoma—all accepted. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25298, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the statements given below and choose the correct option. (a) Tuberculosis bacterium was discovered by scientist Robert Cocke. (b) Tuberculosis is currently considered as a highly contagious disease. (c) BCG vaccine is given as a vaccine against tuberculosis.', '{"A": "only (a) and (b) are correct", "B": "only (a) and (c) are correct", "C": "only (b) and (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The answer is option D: Koch''s bacillus discovery, TB contagion, and BCG immunisation—all three statements per key. You can eliminate A (only (a) and (b) are correct), B (only (a) and (c) are correct), C (only (b) and (c) are correct) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option D. Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25299, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following two statements is correct? (a) Turner syndrome is a disease in which the sex-linked portion of one X chromosome is defective. (b) In this disease the chromosomes in females are 44+X instead of 44+XX.', '{"A": "only statement (a) is correct", "B": "only statement (b) is correct", "C": "Both statements are correct", "D": "Both statements are incorrect"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'The correct answer is option C — Turner syndrome: missing/defective X segment and 44+X karyotype in affected females—both statements. You can eliminate A (only statement (a) is correct), B (only statement (b) is correct), D (Both statements are incorrect) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option C.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25300, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following two statements is false? (a) Albinism is a genetic disorder caused by a defect in the pigment melanin in the body. (b) Albinism disorder itself is called achromia.', '{"A": "Statement (a) only", "B": "Statement (b) only", "C": "Both", "D": "Neither"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct: Neither. This follows from the stem — Which of the following two statements is false? (a) Albinism is a genetic disorder caused by a defect in the pigment melanin in the body. (b) Albinism disorder Wrong options A (Statement (a) only), B (Statement (b) only), C (Both) do not match the definition or symbol tested here.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium')
INSERT INTO pyq_questions (id, topic, subject, exam, year, question, options, correct, explanation, tags, difficulty)
VALUES
  (25301, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, '(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv) 2) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii) 3) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv) 4) (a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i) Match the correct pairs of diseases and pathogens. List I List II (a) Common Cold i) Variola virus (b) Influenza ii) Flavi virus (c) Yellow fever iii) Myxo virus (d) Small pox iv) Rhino virus', '{"A": "(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)", "B": "(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)", "C": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)", "D": "(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D is correct because Virus matching—option D: common cold–rhinovirus (iv), influenza–myxovirus (iii), yellow fever–flavivirus (ii), smallpox–variola (i). If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25302, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Check the correctness of the statement given below and choose the statement which is not correct. (a) Color blindness is a genetic disease. (b) Color blindness is a disease caused by lack of a gene on the sex chromosome. (c) Genes for color appearance are usually located on the Y chromosome.', '{"A": "only (c)", "B": "only (b)", "C": "only (a)", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'Option A is the right choice since colour-blindness genes lie on the X chromosome, not the Y—statement (c) is incorrect. You can eliminate B (only (b)), C (only (a)), D (None of these) — each one conflicts with the principle or calculation that leads to option A. MPSC papers often recycle this type of detail, so read the question carefully before you commit to an option.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25303, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Observe the statements given below and choose the option of the correct statements. (a) Diabetes is a disease caused by deficiency of a hormone called insulin. (b) Diabetes is a disease in which blood sugar level decreases. (c) Diabetes is inflammation of pancreas.', '{"A": "only (a) and (b) are correct", "B": "only (b) and (c) are correct", "C": "only (a) and (c) are correct", "D": "All of the above are correct"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'Option C matches the question because diabetes is insulin-deficiency/hyperglycaemia with pancreatic involvement; (b) ''sugar decreases'' is wrong per key split. Distractors A (only (a) and (b) are correct), B (only (b) and (c) are correct), D (All of the above are correct) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. Keep the name, formula, and unit together in your notes — that helps you spot incomplete distractors quickly.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25304, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Which of the following pairs is improperly matched?', '{"A": "Skin cancer - Melanoma", "B": "Bone cancer - Osteosarcoma", "C": "Cartilage - Chondroma", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'The correct answer is option D — melanoma, osteosarcoma, and chondroma pairings all match standard oncology labels. Distractors A (Skin cancer - Melanoma), B (Bone cancer - Osteosarcoma), C (Cartilage - Chondroma) look plausible but do not follow from the facts stated in the question. If the question asks what is incorrect, identify the false statement first and then match it to the answer choice.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25305, 'GK 2025-26 Marathon', 'Current Affairs', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2025, 'Who won the ''Best Actress in a Role'' at Oscar 2026?', '{"A": "Emma Stone", "B": "Jessie Buckley", "C": "Kate Hudson", "D": "Rose Byrne"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'This maps to "Jessie Buckley". The year 2026 anchors this question — the correct option is the fact that dates to that year. The safest way to lock this item into recall is to write the correct option next to its associated year or body in your revision notebook and skim it weekly.', '{"Current Affairs","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2025","GK 2025-26 Marathon"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25306, 'आरोग्यशास्त्र', 'Science', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2024, 'Identify the incorrect pairing of diseases and their vaccines (a) Typhoid - Typhoid A and B (b) Cholera - Haffkines vaccine (c) Chicken pox - Varicella vaccine (d) Malaria - Artemicilin combination therapy', '{"A": "only (a) and (b)", "B": "only (b)", "C": "only (a)", "D": "None of these"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'Option D works here because Per key, Store the reasoning behind the answer, not just the letter, because topic packs revisit the same idea in different forms.', '{"Science","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2024","आरोग्यशास्त्र"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
  (25307, 'GK 2025-26 Marathon', 'Current Affairs', 'MPSC Practice Subject Test', 2025, 'Which state topped the 2026 Fiscal Health Index by NITI Aayog?', '{"A": "Gujarat", "B": "Odisha", "C": "Maharashtra", "D": "Karnataka"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'The examiner has marked Odisha as the correct response. NITI Aayog (National Institution for Transforming India) replaced the Planning Commission on 1 January 2015; the PM is its ex-officio Chairperson. For MPSC prelims, one line of revision is enough to lock this fact into memory — add it to your one-page factual notebook.', '{"Current Affairs","MPSC Practice Subject Test","2025","GK 2025-26 Marathon"}'::text[], 'Medium'),
